var e='@track bash\n@level advanced\n@title Terminal · Advanced\n@plainvoice true\n@name The command line, advanced: variables, scripts and automation\n@blurb Store values in variables, make decisions with exit codes, loop over files, write scripts that take arguments and check their input, and debug a script that misbehaves.\n\n=== term3-01 | Variables\n--- teach\nThe intermediate course taught you to combine commands into pipelines. This course turns commands into programs: values kept in variables, decisions made from exit codes, loops over files, and scripts that take arguments and check them. It starts with the smallest piece of all: giving a name to a value.\n\nThink of a label on a jar. You write "sugar" on the label once. After that you can say "pass me the sugar" instead of describing the jar every time. A **variable** is a label like that: a name that holds a piece of text, so you can use the text again without typing it again.\n\n### Setting a variable\n\nWrite the name, an equals sign `=`, and the value:\n\n```\n~/project $ planet=mars\n~/project $\n```\n\nNothing is printed. The [[shell|shell]] has quietly remembered that `planet` now holds the text `mars`.\n\n**Watch out:** no spaces around the `=`. With spaces, the shell reads `planet` as [[the name of a command to run|first-word]], and `=` and `mars` as its **arguments**: the extra words a command is given to work on. There is no command called `planet`, so nothing gets set:\n\n```\n~/project $ planet = mars\nplanet: command not found\n```\n\n### Reading a variable\n\nTo use the value, put a dollar sign `$` in front of the name. `$planet` is read "dollar planet", and it means "the text stored in `planet`". Before the command runs, the shell [[swaps it for that text|swap-in]], the same way it swapped `*.txt` for a list of file names in the wildcards lesson.\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "Next stop: $planet"\nNext stop: mars\n```\n\n`echo` never sees `$planet`. It receives `Next stop: mars` and prints it.\n\n### Where the name ends: curly braces\n\nAfter a `$`, the shell keeps reading the name for as long as it sees [[letters, digits or underscores|name-rules]]. So if letters follow straight after the name, they become part of it. Curly braces `{ }` mark where the name stops: `${planet}` is read "dollar, curly brace, planet, curly brace".\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "Welcome to ${planet}base"\nWelcome to marsbase\n~/project $ echo "Welcome to $planetbase"\nWelcome to\n```\n\nThe second line looked up a variable called `planetbase`, which does not exist. In the same way, `${planet}s` gives `marss`, but `$planets` looks up a different variable called `planets`.\n\nThe braces are always allowed, even when nothing follows. This makes an empty file called `mars-notes.txt`:\n\n```\n~/project $ touch "${planet}-notes.txt"\n~/project $ ls\nmars-notes.txt\n```\n\n### Double quotes and single quotes\n\nUntil now, quotes only kept text together. Now the two kinds behave differently:\n\n- **Double quotes** `" "` let the shell fill in variables: `"Next stop: $planet"` becomes `Next stop: mars`.\n- **Single quotes** `\' \'` keep every character exactly as typed, the dollar sign too.\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'Next stop: $planet\'\nNext stop: $planet\n```\n\n### Two quiet rules\n\nNames are **case-sensitive**: capital and small letters count as different. `$Planet` is not `$planet`.\n\nA variable that was never set is empty, and the shell gives no error at all:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "[$Planet]"\n[]\n```\n\nThe square brackets are only there to show that nothing came out between them. Because there is no error message, a typo in a variable name is [[sneaky|silent-typos]]: the command still runs, with a blank where your value should be.\n\nVariables are how scripts avoid repeating themselves. You [[set a version number or a folder name once|set-once]] at the top and use it everywhere below.\n\n::: context shell The program that reads your commands\nThe **shell** is the program that reads each line you type, works out what it means, and starts the commands. The one in this course is called **bash**. The terminal is the window; the shell is the program listening inside it.\n\nEverything in this lesson (remembering `planet`, swapping in `mars`) is the shell\'s work, not `echo`\'s. That is why variables work with every command, not only with `echo`.\n:::\n\n::: context first-word Why the spaces break it\nThe shell splits every line at its spaces into words. The first word is always the command to run, and the rest are its arguments. In `mkdir notes`, `mkdir` is the command and `notes` is its one argument.\n\n`planet=mars` is one word with an `=` inside, and the shell has a special rule for that shape: "set a variable". `planet = mars` is three words, so the special rule does not apply. The shell goes looking for a command called `planet` and hands it `=` and `mars`.\n:::\n\n::: context swap-in Filled in before the command runs\nThe shell rewrites the line first and runs it second. By the time `echo` starts, every `$name` has already been replaced by its text, so `echo` has no idea a variable was ever there.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 160" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="20" y="12" width="320" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="34" y="34" font-size="13" fill="#1f2a44">echo "Next stop: <tspan fill="#1d6fd1" font-weight="bold">$planet</tspan>"</text>\n  <line x1="120" y1="48" x2="120" y2="72" stroke="#6c7a93" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="120,80 114,70 126,70" fill="#6c7a93"/>\n  <text x="134" y="68" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">the shell swaps in the value</text>\n  <rect x="20" y="84" width="320" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="34" y="106" font-size="13" fill="#1f2a44">echo "Next stop: <tspan fill="#1d6fd1" font-weight="bold">mars</tspan>"</text>\n  <text x="34" y="144" font-size="12" fill="#6c7a93">only the second line ever reaches echo</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context name-rules What a name may look like\nA variable name may use letters, digits and the underscore `_`, and it may not start with a digit. So `planet`, `fuel_kg` and `stage2` are fine; `2stage` and `fuel-kg` are not.\n\nA dash can never be part of a name, which is why `$planet-base` would also give `mars-base`. The braces are still a good habit: they make it obvious where the name ends.\n\nBy custom, variables the whole system shares are written in capitals (`HOME`, `PATH`), and your own in small letters, so the two never clash.\n:::\n\n::: context silent-typos Catching the typo that makes no noise\nA misspelled name does not crash anything. It quietly becomes an empty string, and the damage shows up later: a file saved under the wrong name, a folder that was never made.\n\nNear the end of this course you will debug a script with exactly this bug, and meet `set -u`. That line at the top of a script turns any use of an unset variable into an immediate error, so the typo is caught where it happens.\n:::\n\n::: context set-once One place to change\nReal build scripts start with a few lines like these:\n\n```\nversion=2.4.1\ntarget=flight-computer\n```\n\nEverything below uses `$version` and `$target`. When the next release comes, one line changes, and every file name, folder and message follows. Without the variable, someone has to find every copy of `2.4.1` by hand, and the one they miss ships with the wrong number on it.\n:::\n--- task\nSet a variable called `project` to `launchpad` and a variable called `version` to `2`. Then, using only the variables (type `$project` and `$version`, never `launchpad` or `2` again):\n\n1. print the line `launchpad v2 is ready` with `echo`;\n2. make a folder called `launchpad-v2` with `mkdir`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nproject=launchpad\nversion=2\necho "$project v$version is ready"\nmkdir "$project-v$version"\n--- hint\nSet each one with `name=value` and no spaces around the `=`: `project=launchpad`, then `version=2`.\n--- hint\nPut the message in double quotes so the variables are filled in: `echo "$project v$version is ready"`.\n--- hint\nA dash cannot be part of a name, so `mkdir "$project-v$version"` builds `launchpad-v2` from both variables.\n--- check shell | Both variables are set\nvar project == launchpad\nvar version == 2\n--- check shell | You printed the message from the variables\nprinted-line launchpad v2 is ready\nused $project\n--- check shell | The folder is named from the variables\ndir launchpad-v2\nused $version\n\n+++ practice | A crew badge\n--- task\nSet a variable called `crew` to `ada` and a variable called `role` to `pilot`. Then, using only the variables (never typing `ada` or `pilot` again):\n\n1. print the line `ada: pilot` with `echo`;\n2. write the same line into a file called `badge.txt` with `echo` and `>`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\ncrew=ada\nrole=pilot\necho "$crew: $role"\necho "$crew: $role" > badge.txt\n--- hint\nSet each one with `name=value`, with no spaces around the `=`.\n--- hint\nA colon cannot be part of a name, so `$crew:` stops at the `w`. Put the whole message in double quotes so both variables are filled in.\n--- check shell | Both variables are set\nvar crew == ada\nvar role == pilot\n--- check shell | The line was printed from the variables\nprinted-line ada: pilot\nused $crew\n--- check shell | badge.txt holds the same line\nfile badge.txt == ada: pilot\nused $role\nused >\n\n+++ practice | Where the name ends\n--- task\nSet a variable called `name` to `probe`. Then, using `$name` every time you need the word `probe`:\n\n1. make a folder called `probes`;\n2. inside it, make an empty file called `probe_log.txt` with `touch`.\n\nThe letters and the underscore that follow the name are the trap: without curly braces, the shell reads them as part of the variable\'s name.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nname=probe\nmkdir "${name}s"\ntouch "${name}s/${name}_log.txt"\n--- hint\n`$names` looks up a variable called `names`, which is empty. Curly braces mark where the name stops: `${name}s`.\n--- hint\nAn underscore can be part of a name too, so `$name_log` looks up `name_log`. Write `${name}_log.txt`.\n--- check shell | The folder probes exists\ndir probes\nused ${name}\n--- check shell | probe_log.txt is inside it\nfile probes/probe_log.txt\n--- check shell | No stray file with a blank where the name should be\nmissing probes/.txt\nmissing probes/_log.txt\ncount * == 1\n\n+++ practice | A log line from variables\n--- task\n`events.log` already holds two lines. Set `level` to `WARN` and `part` to `pump`. Then, using only the variables for those two words:\n\n1. add the line `WARN pump pressure low` to the **end** of `events.log`, keeping the two lines already there;\n2. print how many lines of `events.log` contain `WARN`, as a single number, with `grep -c` and `$level` as the pattern.\n--- starter\necho "INFO pump start" > events.log\necho "WARN fan slow" >> events.log\n--- solution\nlevel=WARN\npart=pump\necho "$level $part pressure low" >> events.log\ngrep -c "$level" events.log\n--- hint\n`>>` adds to the end of a file; a single `>` would empty it first.\n--- hint\n`echo "$level $part pressure low" >> events.log` adds the line. Then give `grep -c` the variable as its pattern.\n--- check shell | The new line was added at the end, and the old lines kept\nfile events.log ~= INFO pump start\\nWARN fan slow\\nWARN pump pressure low\n--- check shell | Two lines contain WARN\nprinted-exactly 2\nused grep -c\n--- check shell | Built from the variables\nused $level\nused $part\n\n+++ practice | One line filled in, one line kept as typed\n--- task\nSet a variable called `fuel` to `98`. Then write exactly these two lines into `readout.txt`, the first with `>` and the second with `>>`:\n\n```\nfuel=98\nthe variable is $fuel\n```\n\nThe first line must come from the variable (type `$fuel`, not `98`). In the second line, the dollar sign and the word `fuel` must reach the file exactly as shown.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nfuel=98\necho "fuel=$fuel" > readout.txt\necho \'the variable is $fuel\' >> readout.txt\n--- hint\nDouble quotes fill variables in; single quotes keep every character as typed.\n--- hint\nThe first line wants the value, so double quotes. The second wants the dollar sign itself, so single quotes: `\'the variable is $fuel\'`.\n--- check shell | fuel is set\nvar fuel == 98\n--- check shell | readout.txt holds the value, then the name as typed\nfile readout.txt ~= fuel=98\\nthe variable is $fuel\n--- check shell | The first line came from the variable\nused =$fuel\nused >>\n\n+++ practice | The report with a blank in it\n--- task\nThe variable `mission` is already set to `artemis` in this shell. A teammate typed two commands that went wrong:\n\n```\necho "Report: $Mission"\ntouch "$mission_notes.txt"\n```\n\nThe first printed `Report:` with nothing after it. The second made a hidden file called `.txt` instead of `artemis_notes.txt`.\n\n1. Print the line `Report: artemis`, using the variable.\n2. Make the empty file `artemis_notes.txt`, using the variable.\n3. Delete the stray file `.txt`.\n--- starter\nmission=artemis\ntouch "$mission_notes.txt"\n--- solution\necho "Report: $mission"\ntouch "${mission}_notes.txt"\nrm .txt\n--- hint\nNames are case-sensitive: `$Mission` and `$mission` are two different variables, and one of them was never set.\n--- hint\nIn `$mission_notes`, the underscore and `notes` are read as part of the name. Braces fix it: `${mission}_notes.txt`.\n--- hint\n`ls -a` shows the hidden `.txt`. Delete it with `rm .txt`.\n--- check shell | The report names the mission\nprinted-line Report: artemis\nused $mission\n--- check shell | artemis_notes.txt exists\nfile artemis_notes.txt\nused ${mission}\n--- check shell | The stray .txt is gone\nmissing .txt\n\n+++ practice | A versioned build\n--- task\nSet `target` to `flight`, `major` to `3` and `minor` to `1`. Then, using only the variables:\n\n1. make a folder called `flight-v3.1`;\n2. inside it, make an empty file called `flight_3_1.bin`;\n3. write the line `built flight v3.1` into `flight-v3.1/NOTES.txt`.\n\nThen set `minor` to `2` and run the same three commands again, unchanged, so that `flight-v3.2` gets its own `flight_3_2.bin` and `NOTES.txt` holding `built flight v3.2`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\ntarget=flight\nmajor=3\nminor=1\nmkdir "$target-v$major.$minor"\ntouch "$target-v$major.$minor/${target}_${major}_${minor}.bin"\necho "built $target v$major.$minor" > "$target-v$major.$minor/NOTES.txt"\nminor=2\nmkdir "$target-v$major.$minor"\ntouch "$target-v$major.$minor/${target}_${major}_${minor}.bin"\necho "built $target v$major.$minor" > "$target-v$major.$minor/NOTES.txt"\n--- hint\nA dash and a dot can never be part of a name, so `"$target-v$major.$minor"` needs no braces.\n--- hint\nAn underscore can be part of a name, so the file needs braces: `${target}_${major}_${minor}.bin`.\n--- hint\nAfter `minor=2`, press the Up arrow to bring each of the three commands back. Because they only use variables, they build the new version by themselves.\n--- check shell | Version 3.1 is complete\nfile flight-v3.1/flight_3_1.bin\nfile flight-v3.1/NOTES.txt == built flight v3.1\n--- check shell | Version 3.2 is complete\nfile flight-v3.2/flight_3_2.bin\nfile flight-v3.2/NOTES.txt == built flight v3.2\nvar minor == 2\n--- check shell | Built from the variables\nused ${major}\nused $target\ncount * == 2\n\n=== term3-02 | Exporting variables to the programs you run\n--- teach\nLast lesson you stored text in variables and used it in your own commands. This lesson is about who else can see those variables: the programs you start from your shell.\n\nPicture a head chef handing a job to an assistant. The chef does not hand over everything in their head. They write a few things on a card, and the assistant takes a copy of the card. Anything not on the card, the assistant never learns. And if the assistant scribbles on their copy, the chef\'s card stays the same.\n\n### A program you start is a child\n\nEvery command you start (a script, `node`, `git`) runs as a separate [[process|process]]: a program that is running right now. Because your shell started it, it is called a **child process**, and your shell is its parent.\n\nTo see this, you need a program to start. `bash next-stop.sh` runs the commands saved in the file `next-stop.sh` in a brand-new shell, a child of yours. Scripts get a full lesson later in this course; here the script is ready-made. It holds one line:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat next-stop.sh\necho "Next stop: $PLANET"\n```\n\n### Plain variables stay behind\n\nA variable you set with `PLANET=Mars` lives in your shell only. The child does not get it, so inside the script `$PLANET` is empty:\n\n```\n~/project $ PLANET=Mars\n~/project $ bash next-stop.sh\nNext stop:\n```\n\n### Exporting: putting it on the card\n\nA child gets a copy of your **[[environment|environment]]**: the set of variables you have marked to be passed on. Marking a variable that way is called **exporting** it, and the command is `export`:\n\n```\n~/project $ export PLANET\n~/project $ bash next-stop.sh\nNext stop: Mars\n```\n\n`export PLANET=Mars` sets the variable and exports it in one go.\n\n**Watch out:** `export` wants the name, with no dollar sign. `export $PLANET` would first turn into `export Mars`, and export a variable called `Mars` instead.\n\n### Seeing the environment: env\n\n`env` lists every variable that is exported, one per line, as `NAME=value`:\n\n```\n~/project $ env\nHOME=/home/you\nPLANET=Mars\nPATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\nSHELL=/bin/bash\nUSER=you\n```\n\nOn a real computer the list is longer, but it looks the same. By custom, these names are [[in capital letters|capital-names]].\n\n### For one command only\n\nWrite `NAME=value` straight in front of a command, and the variable is set for that one command and nothing else. Your own `PLANET` is left alone:\n\n```\n~/project $ PLANET=Venus bash next-stop.sh\nNext stop: Venus\n~/project $ echo $PLANET\nMars\n```\n\nYou will see this a lot as `DEBUG=1 bash build.sh`: a way to [[switch on extra detail for one run|one-run]], without changing anything for later.\n\n### The copy only travels one way\n\nA script that changes a variable changes its own copy. Your shell never sees the change. Here `PLANET` is exported as `Mars`, and the script `set-pluto.sh` holds two lines: `PLANET=Pluto`, then `echo "In the script: $PLANET"`.\n\n```\n~/project $ bash set-pluto.sh\nIn the script: Pluto\n~/project $ echo "Back in the shell: $PLANET"\nBack in the shell: Mars\n```\n\nThis is how real configuration reaches programs: [[API keys|secrets]], `NODE_ENV=production` (which tells many web programs that they are running for real users), and `PATH`, [[the list of folders the shell searches for commands|path-list]].\n\n::: context process A program that is running\nA program sitting on the disk is a file, like a recipe in a book. A **process** is that program actually running, like a cook following the recipe right now. The same program can run as many processes at once, each with its own memory.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 170" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="8" y="16" width="150" height="96" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="83" y="36" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#1f2a44">your shell (parent)</text>\n  <text x="18" y="62" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">PLANET=Mars  exported</text>\n  <text x="18" y="88" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">planet=mars  plain</text>\n  <rect x="202" y="16" width="150" height="96" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="277" y="36" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#1f2a44">next-stop.sh (child)</text>\n  <text x="212" y="62" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">PLANET=Mars  a copy</text>\n  <text x="212" y="88" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">no planet at all</text>\n  <line x1="160" y1="58" x2="192" y2="58" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="200,58 190,53 190,63" fill="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <line x1="200" y1="96" x2="168" y2="96" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>\n  <polygon points="160,96 170,91 170,101" fill="#b4232c"/>\n  <text x="180" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">exported variables are copied into the child;</text>\n  <text x="180" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">changes in the child never come back</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context environment Why it is called the environment\nYour environment is everything around you that you did not choose: the room, the weather, the people. A process\'s environment is the same idea: the settings it finds around it when it starts, handed down by whoever started it.\n\nEvery process has one, and every child starts with a copy of its parent\'s. That is how a setting you export once reaches every program you run afterwards.\n:::\n\n::: context capital-names Capitals for shared settings\nNothing forces capitals, but nearly everyone follows the custom: variables in the environment, such as `HOME`, `USER` and `PATH`, are written in capitals, and a script\'s own working variables in small letters.\n\nThe custom does a real job. You can tell at a glance which variables came from outside, and your own `path` or `user` can never overwrite the system\'s `PATH` or `USER` by accident, because the names differ in case.\n:::\n\n::: context one-run Settings for a single run\nEngineers use the one-command form all the time, because it leaves nothing behind:\n\n```\nDEBUG=1 bash build.sh\n```\n\nThe build script checks `$DEBUG`, and if it is set, prints far more about what it is doing. The next time you run `bash build.sh`, it is quiet again. Test systems for flight software work the same way: a variable picks which simulator to talk to, or how much to log, for that one run.\n:::\n\n::: context secrets Keeping secrets out of the code\nAn **API key** is a secret password that lets a program use an online service. If it is written inside the code, it ends up everywhere the code goes: shared with the team, uploaded, copied into backups.\n\nSo programs read keys from the environment instead. The code says "use whatever is in `API_KEY`", and each computer that runs it sets its own. The key never has to be written in the code at all.\n:::\n\n::: context path-list How the shell finds a command\nWhen you type `ls`, the shell has to find a program file called `ls`. It looks through the folders listed in `PATH`, in order, and runs the first match. The folders are separated by colons:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo $PATH\n/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\n```\n\nIf a program is in none of those folders, you get `command not found`, even though the file exists. You will meet this again in the scripts lesson, where `./` tells the shell to run a script from the current folder.\n:::\n--- task\n`greet.sh` prints `Hello, ` followed by `$NAME`. `NAME` is already set to `Ada` in this shell, but it is a plain variable, so the script does not see it.\n\n1. Run the script with `bash greet.sh` and see the empty greeting.\n2. Export the variable with `export NAME`.\n3. Run `bash greet.sh` again, so it prints `Hello, Ada`.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "Hello, $NAME"\' > greet.sh\nNAME=Ada\n--- solution\nbash greet.sh\nexport NAME\nbash greet.sh\n--- hint\n`echo $NAME` shows that the variable is set in your shell. The script still cannot see it, because it was never exported.\n--- hint\nExporting takes the name without a dollar sign: `export NAME`.\n--- hint\nThen run `bash greet.sh` again; it should print `Hello, Ada`.\n--- check shell | NAME is exported\nenv NAME == Ada\n--- check shell | The script saw it\nprinted-line Hello, Ada\nran bash greet.sh\n\n+++ practice | Tell the script where it is\n--- task\n`where.sh` is a ready-made script that prints `Station: ` followed by `$STATION`.\n\n1. Set `STATION` to `Kourou` **and** export it, in one command.\n2. Run the script with `bash where.sh`, so it prints `Station: Kourou`.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "Station: $STATION"\' > where.sh\n--- solution\nexport STATION=Kourou\nbash where.sh\n--- hint\n`export` can set a variable and mark it to be passed on in the same command.\n--- hint\n`export STATION=Kourou`, with no spaces around the `=`, then `bash where.sh`.\n--- check shell | STATION is exported\nenv STATION == Kourou\nused export STATION=\n--- check shell | The script saw it\nprinted-line Station: Kourou\n--- check shell | You ran the script\nran bash where.sh\n\n+++ practice | Live for one run only\n--- task\n`mode.sh` prints `Mode: ` followed by `$MODE`. `MODE` is already exported as `test`.\n\n1. Run the script **once** with `MODE` set to `live` for that one run only, so it prints `Mode: live`.\n2. Run it again with plain `bash mode.sh`. It must print `Mode: test`, which shows your shell\'s `MODE` was never changed.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "Mode: $MODE"\' > mode.sh\nexport MODE=test\n--- solution\nMODE=live bash mode.sh\nbash mode.sh\n--- hint\nA `NAME=value` written straight in front of a command applies to that command and nothing else.\n--- hint\n`MODE=live bash mode.sh`, then `bash mode.sh` on its own.\n--- check shell | The one run saw live\nprinted-line Mode: live\nused MODE=live bash\n--- check shell | The normal run still sees test\nlast-printed-exactly Mode: test\n--- check shell | Your shell\'s MODE is unchanged\nenv MODE == test\n\n+++ practice | Only the launch settings\n--- task\nSettings for the launch vehicle all have names that start with `LV_`. Three of them are exported already. `LV_CREW` is set to `4`, but it is a plain variable, and `OTHER` is exported too, but it is not a launch setting.\n\n1. Export `LV_CREW`.\n2. With one pipeline of `env`, `grep` and `sort`, save every exported variable whose name **starts with** `LV_` into `lv.txt`, sorted, one `NAME=value` line each.\n--- starter\nexport LV_NAME=falcon\nexport LV_PAD=39A\nexport LV_STAGES=2\nLV_CREW=4\nexport OTHER=LV_backup\n--- solution\nexport LV_CREW\nenv | grep "^LV_" | sort > lv.txt\n--- hint\n`env` lists the exported variables as `NAME=value`. A plain variable does not appear until it is exported.\n--- hint\n`OTHER=LV_backup` contains `LV_` in its value. Pin the pattern to the start of the line with `^`.\n--- hint\n`env | grep "^LV_" | sort > lv.txt`.\n--- check shell | lv.txt holds the four launch settings, sorted\nfile lv.txt ~= LV_CREW=4\\nLV_NAME=falcon\\nLV_PAD=39A\\nLV_STAGES=2\n--- check shell | LV_CREW is exported\nenv LV_CREW == 4\n--- check shell | Built from env with a pinned pattern\nused env |\nused ^LV_\n\n+++ practice | A value with spaces in it\n--- task\n`greet.sh` prints the value of `$GREETING`.\n\n1. Set `GREETING` to `good morning, crew` and export it, in one command, then run `bash greet.sh`. It must print the whole greeting.\n2. Run the script once more with `GREETING` set to `night shift` for that one run only.\n3. Finish by printing your own `GREETING` with `echo "$GREETING"`. It must still be `good morning, crew`.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "$GREETING"\' > greet.sh\n--- solution\nexport GREETING="good morning, crew"\nbash greet.sh\nGREETING="night shift" bash greet.sh\necho "$GREETING"\n--- hint\nWithout quotes, the shell cuts `good morning, crew` at its spaces, so `GREETING` would only get `good`. Put the value in quotes.\n--- hint\nThe one-run form works with quotes too: `GREETING="night shift" bash greet.sh`.\n--- check shell | GREETING is exported whole\nenv GREETING == good morning, crew\n--- check shell | The script printed both greetings\nprinted-line good morning, crew\nprinted-line night shift\nran bash greet.sh\n--- check shell | Your own GREETING was left alone\nlast-printed-exactly good morning, crew\n\n+++ practice | The export that exported the wrong name\n--- task\n`connect.sh` prints `Using server: ` followed by `$SERVER`. A teammate set `SERVER` to `backup2` and then typed:\n\n```\nexport $SERVER\nbash connect.sh\n```\n\nThe script printed `Using server:` with nothing after it. `SERVER` is still set to `backup2` in this shell. Export it properly, then run the script so that it prints `Using server: backup2`.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "Using server: $SERVER"\' > connect.sh\nSERVER=backup2\nexport $SERVER\n--- solution\nexport SERVER\nbash connect.sh\n--- hint\nThe shell fills in `$SERVER` before `export` runs. What name did `export` really receive?\n--- hint\n`export` wants the name itself, with no dollar sign: `export SERVER`.\n--- check shell | SERVER is exported\nenv SERVER == backup2\nused export SERVER\n--- check shell | The script sees the server\nprinted-line Using server: backup2\n--- check shell | You ran the script\nran bash connect.sh\n\n+++ practice | Three runs of the launch script\n--- task\n`launch.sh` prints two lines: `vehicle: ` followed by `$VEHICLE`, and `log level: ` followed by `$LOG_LEVEL`.\n\n1. Export `VEHICLE` as `orion`, so every run sees it.\n2. Run the script with `LOG_LEVEL` set to `debug` for that one run only, and save what it prints into `debug-run.txt` with `>`.\n3. Finish with a plain `bash launch.sh`. Its second line must be `log level:` with nothing after it, which proves the debug setting did not stay behind.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "vehicle: $VEHICLE"\' > launch.sh\necho \'echo "log level: $LOG_LEVEL"\' >> launch.sh\n--- solution\nexport VEHICLE=orion\nLOG_LEVEL=debug bash launch.sh > debug-run.txt\nbash launch.sh\n--- hint\n`export VEHICLE=orion` puts it in the environment of every script you start from now on.\n--- hint\nThe one-run setting goes in front of the command, and the `>` goes after it: `LOG_LEVEL=debug bash launch.sh > debug-run.txt`.\n--- check shell | VEHICLE is exported\nenv VEHICLE == orion\n--- check shell | debug-run.txt holds the debug run\nfile debug-run.txt ~= vehicle: orion\\nlog level: debug\nused LOG_LEVEL=debug bash\n--- check shell | The last run is back to normal\nlast-printed-exactly vehicle: orion\\nlog level:\n\n=== term3-03 | Command substitution: $(…)\n--- teach\nSo far a variable has held text you typed yourself. This lesson lets a variable, or any part of a command, hold the **output** of another command.\n\nThink of a form with a blank in it: "There are ___ notes." You ask a friend to count the notes and write the number in the blank. You never type the number; it arrives from the counting.\n\n### Running a command inside a command\n\n`$(command)` is read "dollar, open bracket, command, close bracket". The shell runs the command inside the brackets first, then [[puts its output right there in the line|fill-the-blank]], as if you had typed it. This is called **command substitution**: the command is substituted, swapped, for what it printed.\n\n`whoami` is a small command that prints [[your user name|user-name]]. In this practice terminal, that is `you`:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "Report for $(whoami)"\nReport for you\n```\n\n### Keeping the output in a variable\n\nThe same thing works after `=`. Here a whole pipeline runs, and its answer is stored:\n\n```\n~/project $ count=$(ls *.txt | wc -l)\n~/project $ echo "There are $count notes"\nThere are 3 notes\n```\n\n`wc -l` printed `3` and then a new line. The shell drops [[the new lines at the end|trailing-newlines]], so `count` holds only `3`, and the sentence stays on one line.\n\n### Anywhere a word could go\n\nA `$(…)` can sit anywhere you could type a word: after `=` for a variable, inside an `echo`, or as the argument of another command, such as the file name after `touch` or `mkdir`:\n\n```\n~/project $ mkdir "backup-$(whoami)"\n~/project $ ls\nbackup-you/\n```\n\n### Double quotes keep the output whole\n\nIf the output has spaces in it, the shell [[splits it into separate words|word-splitting]], unless it is inside double quotes. Here `name.txt` holds the text `old boat`:\n\n```\n~/project $ touch $(cat name.txt)\n~/project $ ls\nboat  name.txt  old\n~/project $ touch "$(cat name.txt)"\n~/project $ ls\nboat  name.txt  old  old boat\n```\n\nWithout quotes, `touch` got two words and made two files. With quotes, `"$(…)"`, it got one name and made one file. So put it in double quotes when the output might contain spaces.\n\n### The old spelling: backticks\n\nIn older scripts you will meet the same thing written with **backticks**, the slanted quote mark that sits under Esc on a US keyboard. The command goes between two backticks, where `$(` and `)` would go:\n\n```\nn=`ls | wc -l`\n```\n\nIt does exactly what `n=$(ls | wc -l)` does. The `$(…)` spelling is easier to read and [[can be nested|nesting]], one inside another, so use that one.\n\n**Watch out:** the dollar and the brackets always go together.\n\n- `count=ls | wc -l` never runs `ls` at all. It prints `0` and leaves `count` unset.\n- `count=(ls)` is a different feature altogether, a list variable, that this course does not use.\n\nOnly `count=$(ls)` runs the command and keeps its output. This is how scripts [[pick up facts about the world|real-use]] while they run.\n\n::: context fill-the-blank The inside runs first\nThe shell works from the inside out. It finds the `$(…)`, runs what is inside, and puts the output in its place. Only then does it run the outer command.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 150" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="10" y="12" width="340" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="20" y="34" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">echo "There are <tspan fill="#1d6fd1" font-weight="bold">$(ls *.txt | wc -l)</tspan> notes"</text>\n  <line x1="150" y1="48" x2="150" y2="72" stroke="#6c7a93" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="150,80 144,70 156,70" fill="#6c7a93"/>\n  <text x="164" y="68" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">runs first, and prints 3</text>\n  <rect x="10" y="84" width="340" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="20" y="106" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">echo "There are <tspan fill="#1d6fd1" font-weight="bold">3</tspan> notes"</text>\n  <text x="20" y="140" font-size="12" fill="#6c7a93">then the outer command runs</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context user-name Who "you" is\nEvery computer keeps a list of **user accounts**, one for each person (and some for programs). `whoami` prints the name of the account your shell is running as.\n\nIn this practice terminal the account is called `you`, and your home folder is `/home/you`. On a real computer `whoami` prints your own login name, and your home folder is named after it.\n:::\n\n::: context trailing-newlines Why the ending is trimmed\nAlmost every command ends its output with a new line, so that the next prompt starts on a fresh line. If `$(…)` kept that new line, `echo "There are $count notes"` would print `There are 3`, then break the line, then `notes`.\n\nSo the shell removes every new line at the very end of the output. New lines in the middle stay where they are.\n:::\n\n::: context word-splitting The same split that breaks loops\nWhen an unquoted `$variable` or `$(…)` is filled in, the shell splits the result at every space into separate words. That is what went wrong with `old boat` above.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 120" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <text x="10" y="24" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">touch $(cat name.txt)</text>\n  <rect x="200" y="10" width="44" height="22" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="222" y="25" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">old</text>\n  <rect x="252" y="10" width="54" height="22" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="279" y="25" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">boat</text>\n  <text x="200" y="50" font-size="11" fill="#b4232c">two words, two files</text>\n  <text x="10" y="84" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">touch "$(cat name.txt)"</text>\n  <rect x="200" y="70" width="80" height="22" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="240" y="85" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">old boat</text>\n  <text x="200" y="110" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">one word, one file</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nIt comes back in the loops lesson, where a file called `old boat.jpeg` breaks an unquoted `$f`, and in the debugging lesson at the end of the course.\n:::\n\n::: context nesting One inside another\nNesting means putting one `$(…)` inside another. `pwd` prints the full path of the folder you are in, and `basename` keeps only its last part:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "Inside: $(basename "$(pwd)")"\nInside: project\n```\n\nThe inner `$(pwd)` runs first and gives `/home/you/project`; then `basename` turns that into `project`. With backticks, the inner pair would have to be written with backslashes in front, which is hard to read and easy to get wrong.\n:::\n\n::: context real-use Stamping a build\nScripts use `$(…)` to learn things they cannot know in advance: today\'s date, how many files there are, which version of the code they are building. A flight software build script might do this:\n\n```\ncommit=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\n```\n\nThat stores the short name of the exact git commit being built. The script then writes it into the finished program, so when the computer on a spacecraft reports its software version, engineers can tell exactly which code is running.\n:::\n--- task\nThis folder holds three `.txt` files and a picture.\n\n1. Store the number of `.txt` files in a variable called `count`, using `$(…)` around a pipeline of `ls` and `wc -l`.\n2. Write the sentence `There are 3 notes` into `summary.txt` with `echo` and `>`, using `$count` rather than typing the 3.\n--- starter\ntouch a.txt b.txt c.txt picture.png\n--- solution\ncount=$(ls *.txt | wc -l)\necho "There are $count notes" > summary.txt\n--- hint\nFirst find the pipeline that counts them: `ls *.txt | wc -l` prints `3`.\n--- hint\nWrap it in `$(…)` and store it, with no spaces around the `=`: `count=$(ls *.txt | wc -l)`.\n--- hint\nThen `echo "There are $count notes" > summary.txt`, and `cat summary.txt` to check.\n--- check shell | count holds the number of notes\nvar count == 3\nused $(\n--- check shell | summary.txt was written from the variable\nfile summary.txt == There are 3 notes\nused $count\n\n+++ practice | How many tables\n--- task\nThe folder `data` holds four `.csv` files and two other files.\n\n1. Store the number of `.csv` files in `data` in a variable called `n`, using `$(…)` around a pipeline of `ls` and `wc -l`.\n2. Print the line `data holds 4 tables`, using `$n` rather than typing the 4.\n--- starter\nmkdir data\ntouch data/crew.csv data/fuel.csv data/orbit.csv data/stages.csv data/readme.md data/logo.png\n--- solution\nn=$(ls data/*.csv | wc -l)\necho "data holds $n tables"\n--- hint\nFirst find the pipeline on its own: `ls data/*.csv | wc -l` should print `4`.\n--- hint\nWrap it in `$(…)` after `n=`, with no spaces around the `=`.\n--- check shell | n holds the number of tables\nvar n == 4\nused $(\n--- check shell | The sentence came from the variable\nprinted-line data holds 4 tables\nused $n\n--- check shell | Counted with ls and wc\nused wc -l\n\n+++ practice | A folder named from a file\n--- task\nThe first line of `version.txt` is the version number of the next release.\n\n1. Store that first line in a variable called `ver`, using `$(…)` around `head`.\n2. Make a folder called `release-` followed by the version, using `$ver`.\n3. Copy `notes.txt` into that folder, using `$ver` again.\n\nType the version number yourself nowhere: it must come from the file.\n--- starter\necho "3.2.0" > version.txt\necho "released on the ninth" >> version.txt\necho "fixed the radio timeout" > notes.txt\n--- solution\nver=$(head -n 1 version.txt)\nmkdir "release-$ver"\ncp notes.txt "release-$ver/"\n--- hint\n`head -n 1 version.txt` prints only the first line. Put it inside `$(…)`.\n--- hint\nThen `mkdir "release-$ver"`, and `cp notes.txt "release-$ver/"`.\n--- check shell | ver holds the version\nvar ver == 3.2.0\nused $(head\n--- check shell | The release folder exists\ndir release-3.2.0\n--- check shell | The notes are inside it\nfile release-3.2.0/notes.txt == fixed the radio timeout\nused $ver\n\n+++ practice | Errors out of total\n--- task\n`app.log` is a log file.\n\n1. Store the number of lines that contain `ERROR` in a variable called `errors`, using `$(…)` and `grep -c`.\n2. Store the total number of lines in a variable called `total`, using `$(…)` around a pipeline of `cat` and `wc -l`.\n3. Write the sentence `3 of 8 lines are errors` into `report.txt`, using both variables.\n--- starter\necho "INFO boot" > app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "INFO retry" >> app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "INFO link up" >> app.log\necho "WARN slow" >> app.log\necho "ERROR network" >> app.log\necho "INFO done" >> app.log\n--- solution\nerrors=$(grep -c ERROR app.log)\ntotal=$(cat app.log | wc -l)\necho "$errors of $total lines are errors" > report.txt\n--- hint\n`grep -c ERROR app.log` prints how many lines match. Store it with `errors=$(…)`.\n--- hint\n`cat app.log | wc -l` prints the number of lines with nothing else. Store that in `total`.\n--- check shell | Both counts are stored\nvar errors == 3\nvar total == 8\n--- check shell | report.txt was written from the variables\nfile report.txt == 3 of 8 lines are errors\nused $errors\nused $total\n--- check shell | Counted with grep -c\nused grep -c\n\n+++ practice | A title with a space in it\n--- task\n`title.txt` holds the name of the next folder you need: `crew roster`.\n\n1. Store the text of `title.txt` in a variable called `title`, using `$(cat title.txt)`.\n2. Make **one** folder whose name is exactly that text, using `$title`.\n3. Inside that folder, make an empty file called `list.txt`, using `$title` again for the folder\'s name.\n\nThere must be no folder called `crew` and none called `roster`.\n--- starter\necho "crew roster" > title.txt\n--- solution\ntitle=$(cat title.txt)\nmkdir "$title"\ntouch "$title/list.txt"\n--- hint\n`title=$(cat title.txt)` stores `crew roster`, space and all.\n--- hint\nUnquoted, `$title` is split at the space, and `mkdir` makes two folders. Wrap it in double quotes every time you use it.\n--- check shell | title holds the whole name\nvar title == crew roster\nused $(cat title.txt)\n--- check shell | One folder, named with the space, with list.txt inside\ncount crew?roster/list.txt == 1\n--- check shell | No folder was made from half the name\nmissing crew\nmissing roster\ncount * == 2\n\n+++ practice | The count with a name stuck to it\n--- task\n`flight.log` has four lines. A teammate wanted `summary.txt` to hold the line `flight.log has 4 lines`, and typed:\n\n```\necho "flight.log has $(wc -l flight.log) lines" > summary.txt\n```\n\nNow `summary.txt` holds `flight.log has 4 flight.log lines`. Write `summary.txt` again so that it holds exactly `flight.log has 4 lines`, still counting with `wc -l` inside `$(…)`.\n--- starter\necho "T+000 engines on" > flight.log\necho "T+010 tower cleared" >> flight.log\necho "T+060 max q" >> flight.log\necho "T+150 stage sep" >> flight.log\necho "flight.log has $(wc -l flight.log) lines" > summary.txt\n--- solution\necho "flight.log has $(cat flight.log | wc -l) lines" > summary.txt\n--- hint\nRun `wc -l flight.log` on its own and look at everything it prints. The command inside `$(…)` is filled in with all of it.\n--- hint\nGiven a file name, `wc` prints the name after the count. Given the text through a pipe, it never learns the name, so it prints only the number.\n--- hint\n`$(cat flight.log | wc -l)`.\n--- check shell | summary.txt holds the clean sentence\nfile summary.txt == flight.log has 4 lines\n--- check shell | Still counted inside $(…)\nused $(\nused wc -l\n--- check shell | The log was not changed\nfile flight.log lines == 4\n\n+++ practice | Stamp the archive\n--- task\nThe folder `runs` holds five `.dat` files from test runs, and `mission.txt` holds the mission\'s name, `artemis-2`.\n\n1. Make a folder whose name is the mission\'s name followed by `-archive`, using `$(cat mission.txt)`, and copy every `.dat` file from `runs` into it.\n2. Write this line into `stamp.txt`, with every changing part filled in by `$(…)`:\n\n```\nartemis-2: 5 runs in project, by you\n```\n\n- `artemis-2` comes from `mission.txt`;\n- `5` is the number of `.dat` files in the new archive folder, counted with `ls` and `wc -l`;\n- `project` is the name of the folder you are in, from `basename` and `pwd`;\n- `you` is your user name, from `whoami`.\n--- starter\nmkdir runs\ntouch runs/run1.dat runs/run2.dat runs/run3.dat runs/run4.dat runs/run5.dat\necho "artemis-2" > mission.txt\n--- solution\nmkdir "$(cat mission.txt)-archive"\ncp runs/*.dat "$(cat mission.txt)-archive/"\necho "$(cat mission.txt): $(ls "$(cat mission.txt)-archive" | wc -l) runs in $(basename "$(pwd)"), by $(whoami)" > stamp.txt\n--- hint\nBuild it in pieces and try each on its own first: `$(cat mission.txt)`, then `ls artemis-2-archive | wc -l`, then `basename "$(pwd)"`, then `whoami`.\n--- hint\nStoring the pieces in variables first is fine, and easier to read: `m=$(cat mission.txt)`, then use `"$m-archive"`.\n--- hint\nOne `$(…)` can sit inside another: `$(basename "$(pwd)")` runs `pwd` first, then `basename` on its answer.\n--- check shell | The archive holds all five runs\ncount artemis-2-archive/*.dat == 5\n--- check shell | stamp.txt says it all\nfile stamp.txt == artemis-2: 5 runs in project, by you\n--- check shell | Every changing part came from a command\nused $(cat mission.txt)\nused $(whoami)\nused pwd\nused basename\n\n=== term3-04a | Exit status: did it work?\n--- teach\nBack in the first Terminal course, `&&` ran its second command "only if the first worked". But how does the shell know a command worked? This lesson shows you the answer the shell reads, so that the next lesson can use it to make choices.\n\nPicture a helper who finishes each job and then, without a word, holds up a card: a thumbs-up if it went fine, or a number that says what went wrong. The card is always there, even when nothing is printed on the screen.\n\n### Every command leaves a number\n\nWhen a command finishes, it leaves behind a number called its **exit status** (also called its **exit code**): a number that says how it went.\n\n- `0` means success. It is [[the only number that means success|zero-success]].\n- Anything else, such as `1`, `2` or `127`, means [[some kind of failure|failure-codes]].\n\nThe number is not printed. It is kept quietly by the shell.\n\n### Seeing it: $?\n\n`$?` is read "dollar question mark". It is a special variable that holds the exit status of [[the last command|last-command]]. You see it with `echo $?`:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls\nnotes.txt\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n~/project $ ls missing\nls: cannot access \'missing\': No such file or directory\n~/project $ echo $?\n2\n```\n\nThe first `ls` worked, so its status is `0`. The second could not find `missing`, so its status is `2`.\n\n### grep answers with its status\n\n`grep` uses its exit status to tell you what it found:\n\n- `0` when it found at least one matching line;\n- `1` when it found none;\n- `2` when something went wrong, such as a file that does not exist.\n\nHere `deploy.log` contains the word `ERROR`:\n\n```\n~/project $ grep ERROR deploy.log\nERROR: health check failed\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n```\n\n### Asking quietly: grep -q\n\nOften you do not want to see the lines at all. You only want the yes-or-no answer. The flag `-q` means **quiet**: `grep` [[prints nothing and only sets its status|quiet-grep]].\n\n```\n~/project $ grep -q ERROR deploy.log\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n~/project $ grep -q WARN deploy.log\n~/project $ echo $?\n1\n```\n\nThe first found `ERROR`, so `0`. The second found no `WARN`, so `1`. Nothing else was printed.\n\n**Watch out:** `$?` is replaced after every command, and `echo $?` is a command too. So check it straight after the command you care about. Ask twice and the second answer is about the first `echo`, which worked:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls missing\nls: cannot access \'missing\': No such file or directory\n~/project $ echo $?\n2\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n```\n\nExit statuses are what scripts, build tools and test systems use to decide whether to [[carry on|carry-on]]. In the next lesson you will use them with `&&` and `||`, and they come back in `if` and in scripts [[later in this course|comes-back]].\n\n::: context zero-success Why zero means it worked\nIt looks backwards at first: in everyday life, zero sounds like "nothing happened". But think about how many ways a job can go. It can go right in only one way. It can go wrong in many: a file is missing, you are not allowed, the disk is full.\n\nSo success gets the single number `0`, and all the other numbers are left free to say which kind of failure happened.\n:::\n\n::: context failure-codes Numbers you will see\nA few statuses come up again and again in bash:\n\n- `1`: a general failure, or "no" (as with `grep` finding nothing).\n- `2`: the command was used wrongly, or could not open something.\n- `126`: the file was found, but it is not allowed to run.\n- `127`: no command by that name. Try `nosuchcommand` and then `echo $?`.\n- `130`: you stopped the command with Ctrl-C.\n\nEach command\'s manual lists its own statuses, and they can differ from tool to tool. The one rule every command keeps is that `0` means success.\n:::\n\n::: context last-command Only the latest answer is kept\n`$?` is like a whiteboard that only has room for one number. Every command wipes it and writes its own.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 130" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="6" y="14" width="104" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="58" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">ls missing</text>\n  <rect x="128" y="14" width="104" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">echo $?</text>\n  <rect x="250" y="14" width="104" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="302" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">echo $?</text>\n  <line x1="112" y1="29" x2="122" y2="29" stroke="#6c7a93" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <line x1="234" y1="29" x2="244" y2="29" stroke="#6c7a93" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="58" y="66" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">prints an error</text>\n  <text x="180" y="66" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">prints 2</text>\n  <text x="302" y="66" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">prints 0</text>\n  <rect x="28" y="84" width="60" height="30" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="58" y="104" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$? = 2</text>\n  <rect x="150" y="84" width="60" height="30" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="180" y="104" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$? = 0</text>\n  <rect x="272" y="84" width="60" height="30" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="302" y="104" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$? = 0</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nIf you need a status later, save it straight away in a variable of your own: `result=$?`.\n:::\n\n::: context quiet-grep Why a quiet grep is useful\nPrinting matching lines is for people. Scripts do not read the screen; they read the status. `grep -q` gives a script a clean yes-or-no, with nothing extra to clean up.\n\nIt is also fast. With `-q`, `grep` stops reading the moment it finds the first match, because the answer cannot change after that. On a log of millions of lines, that can save a lot of time.\n\nOne thing `-q` does not hide: if the file is missing, the error message still appears, because it is sent to standard error, not to standard output.\n:::\n\n::: context carry-on Go or no-go\nBefore a launch, every station is polled and answers "go" or "no-go". One no-go stops the count. Software teams run their checks the same way. A **CI system** (continuous integration: a computer that builds and tests every change automatically) runs step after step, and reads each step\'s exit status. A test runner that finds a failing test exits with a non-zero status, and that is how the system knows to stop, before broken code goes any further.\n:::\n\n::: context comes-back Where you will see it next\nExit statuses are the base of the rest of this course:\n\n- the next lesson uses them to choose between two commands with `&&` and `||`;\n- the `if` lesson runs a command and takes one branch or the other depending on its status;\n- in the last lesson you write a script that ends with `exit 2` or `exit 1` on purpose, so that other programs can tell what went wrong.\n:::\n--- task\nThere are two deploy logs. `deploy-mon.log` contains the word `ERROR`; `deploy-tue.log` does not. There is no `deploy-wed.log` at all.\n\n1. Ask quietly whether `deploy-mon.log` contains `ERROR`, with `grep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log`. Then print its exit status with `echo $?`. You should see `0`.\n2. Do the same for `deploy-tue.log`. You should see `1`.\n3. Do the same for `deploy-wed.log`. You should see `2`.\n--- starter\necho "build ok" > deploy-mon.log\necho "upload ok" >> deploy-mon.log\necho "ERROR: health check failed" >> deploy-mon.log\necho "build ok" > deploy-tue.log\necho "upload ok" >> deploy-tue.log\necho "health ok" >> deploy-tue.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log\necho $?\ngrep -q ERROR deploy-tue.log\necho $?\ngrep -q ERROR deploy-wed.log\necho $?\n--- hint\nEach question takes two commands: the `grep -q` line, then `echo $?` straight after it.\n--- hint\nStart with `grep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log`. It prints nothing; `echo $?` then shows `0`.\n--- hint\nRepeat with `deploy-tue.log` and then `deploy-wed.log` in place of `deploy-mon.log`, running `echo $?` after each one.\n--- check shell | Monday\'s log has ERROR: status 0\nran grep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log\nprinted-exactly 0\n--- check shell | Tuesday\'s log has none: status 1\nran grep -q ERROR deploy-tue.log\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | A log that does not exist: status 2\nran grep -q ERROR deploy-wed.log\nprinted-exactly 2\nused $?\n\n+++ practice | Is the folder there?\n--- task\nThis folder has a `logs` folder, but no `reports` folder.\n\n1. Run `ls logs`. Then print its exit status inside a sentence, as the line `logs: 0`, using `$?` in the `echo`.\n2. Run `ls reports`, then print the line `reports: 2` the same way.\n--- starter\nmkdir logs\ntouch logs/boot.log\n--- solution\nls logs\necho "logs: $?"\nls reports\necho "reports: $?"\n--- hint\n`$?` works inside double quotes, like any variable: the shell fills in the number before `echo` runs.\n--- hint\nEach question takes two commands: the `ls` line, then `echo "logs: $?"` straight after it.\n--- check shell | ls logs worked: status 0\nran ls logs\nprinted-line logs: 0\n--- check shell | ls reports failed: status 2\nran ls reports\nprinted-line reports: 2\n--- check shell | Read with $?\nused $?\n\n+++ practice | Keep the answers for later\n--- task\n`mon.log` contains the word `ERROR`; `tue.log` does not.\n\n1. Ask quietly whether `mon.log` contains `ERROR` with `grep -q`, and straight after it save the exit status in a variable called `mon`.\n2. Do the same for `tue.log`, saving its status in `tue`.\n3. Print the line `mon=0 tue=1`, using `$mon` and `$tue`.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR: pump stalled" > mon.log\necho "all nominal" > tue.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q ERROR mon.log\nmon=$?\ngrep -q ERROR tue.log\ntue=$?\necho "mon=$mon tue=$tue"\n--- hint\n`$?` only holds the status of the very last command, so save it on the next line: `mon=$?`.\n--- hint\nAfter both are saved, `echo "mon=$mon tue=$tue"`.\n--- check shell | Both statuses are saved\nvar mon == 0\nvar tue == 1\nused =$?\n--- check shell | The line shows both\nprinted-line mon=0 tue=1\n--- check shell | Asked quietly\nused grep -q\n\n+++ practice | Does any line begin with FAIL?\n--- task\n`tests.log` mentions `FAIL` in the middle of a line, but no line **begins** with `FAIL`.\n\n1. Ask quietly whether any line begins with `FAIL`, using `grep -q` and `^`. Then print the line `begins with FAIL: 1`, with the number coming from `$?`.\n2. Ask quietly whether the log mentions `fail` anywhere, in any mix of capitals. Then print the line `mentions fail: 0`, with the number coming from `$?`.\n--- starter\necho "test boot: ok" > tests.log\necho "test radio: ok (no FAIL seen)" >> tests.log\necho "test pump: ok" >> tests.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q "^FAIL" tests.log\necho "begins with FAIL: $?"\ngrep -qi fail tests.log\necho "mentions fail: $?"\n--- hint\n`^` pins the pattern to the start of the line. Put the pattern in double quotes.\n--- hint\n`-i` ignores capitals, and flags can share one dash: `-qi`.\n--- check shell | No line begins with FAIL: status 1\nprinted-line begins with FAIL: 1\nused ^FAIL\n--- check shell | fail appears somewhere: status 0\nprinted-line mentions fail: 0\n--- check shell | Both questions were asked quietly\nran grep\nused -q\nused $?\n\n+++ practice | The whiteboard gets wiped\n--- task\nThere is no folder called `nowhere`, and there is no command called `launchnow`.\n\n1. Run `ls nowhere`, and save its exit status in a variable called `code` **straight away**.\n2. Print the line `saved: 2`, using `$code`.\n3. Type `launchnow`, then print its exit status. You should see `127`, the status for "no command by that name".\n--- starter\n--- solution\nls nowhere\ncode=$?\necho "saved: $code"\nlaunchnow\necho $?\n--- hint\nThe save must be the very next command. Any command in between, even an `echo`, replaces `$?`.\n--- hint\n`code=$?` straight after `ls nowhere`, then `echo "saved: $code"`.\n--- check shell | code holds the failure\nvar code == 2\nused code=$?\n--- check shell | The saved status was printed\nprinted-line saved: 2\n--- check shell | A missing command: status 127\nran launchnow\nprinted-exactly 127\n\n+++ practice | The status of the wrong command\n--- task\n`deploy.log` has no `ERROR` in it. A teammate wanted the exit status of the `grep`, and typed this one line:\n\n```\ngrep -q ERROR deploy.log; echo "checking"; echo $?\n```\n\nIt printed `checking` and then `0`, every time, whatever the log said. Fix the line, keeping it **one line** with `;` between the commands, so that the number it prints is the real exit status of `grep -q ERROR deploy.log`. It should be `1`.\n--- starter\necho "build ok" > deploy.log\necho "upload ok" >> deploy.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q ERROR deploy.log; echo $?\n--- hint\n`$?` is about the command that ran just before it. In the teammate\'s line, what ran just before `echo $?`?\n--- hint\nThe `echo "checking"` is in the way. Take it out, so `echo $?` comes straight after the `grep`.\n--- check shell | You asked grep the question\nran grep -q ERROR deploy.log\n--- check shell | Its real answer is 1\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | One line, with $? straight after the grep\nused deploy.log; echo $?\n\n+++ practice | Which sensors reported in\n--- task\nEach sensor writes a log. A sensor that reported in wrote a line containing `OK`. There are four sensors, `s1` to `s4`, but `s3.log` is missing altogether.\n\nFor each sensor, in order, ask quietly whether its log contains `OK` with `grep -q`, and save the status in a variable: `a` for `s1.log`, `b` for `s2.log`, `c` for `s3.log` and `d` for `s4.log`. Then write the four statuses, in order and separated by spaces, into `status.txt`. It must hold exactly:\n\n```\n0 1 2 0\n```\n--- starter\necho "T+000 OK" > s1.log\necho "T+000 FAULT" > s2.log\necho "T+000 OK" > s4.log\necho "T+010 OK" >> s4.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q OK s1.log\na=$?\ngrep -q OK s2.log\nb=$?\ngrep -q OK s3.log\nc=$?\ngrep -q OK s4.log\nd=$?\necho "$a $b $c $d" > status.txt\n--- hint\n`grep` answers `0` when it finds a match, `1` when it finds none, and `2` when it cannot read the file.\n--- hint\nSave each status on the line straight after its `grep`: `a=$?`, and so on.\n--- hint\nFinish with `echo "$a $b $c $d" > status.txt`.\n--- check shell | status.txt holds the four answers\nfile status.txt == 0 1 2 0\n--- check shell | Each status was saved\nvar a == 0\nvar b == 1\nvar c == 2\nvar d == 0\n--- check shell | Asked quietly, and read with $?\nused grep -q\nused =$?\n\n=== term3-04 | Either-or: && and ||\n--- teach\nLast lesson you saw that every command leaves an exit status, and that `grep -q` answers with `0` for "found it" and `1` for "not found". Now you will use that answer to choose which command runs next.\n\nIt is like a plan for the weekend: if it rains, go to the museum; otherwise, go to the beach. You check one thing, and the answer decides what happens.\n\n### && runs the next command on success\n\nYou met `&&` in the first course. Now you can say exactly what it does: `a && b` runs `b` only if `a` finished with status `0`. People read `&&` as [["and"|and-or]].\n\n```\n~/project $ grep -q ERROR deploy.log && echo "found an error"\nfound an error\n```\n\n`deploy.log` contains `ERROR`, so `grep` finished with `0`, and the `echo` ran.\n\n### || runs the next command on failure\n\n`||` is [[two vertical bars|two-bars]], read "or". `a || b` runs `b` only if `a` failed, that is, finished with any status other than `0`.\n\n```\n~/project $ grep -q WARN deploy.log || echo "no warnings"\nno warnings\n~/project $ grep -q ERROR deploy.log || echo "no errors"\n~/project $\n```\n\nThere is no `WARN` in the log, so the first `grep` failed and the `echo` ran. There is an `ERROR`, so the second `grep` succeeded, and nothing was printed.\n\n### Together: an either-or\n\nPut both on one line and you get a compact [[either/or|either-or]]:\n\n```\ngrep -q ERROR deploy.log && echo "failed" || echo "ok"\n```\n\nFollow it for each case:\n\n- The log contains `ERROR`: `grep` gives `0`, so `&& echo "failed"` runs and prints `failed`. That `echo` worked, so the `|| echo "ok"` part is skipped.\n- The log has no `ERROR`: `grep` gives `1`, so the `&&` part is skipped. The last status is still a failure, so `|| echo "ok"` runs and prints `ok`.\n\n**Watch out:** this is [[not a true if/else|not-if-else]]. If the middle command fails, the `||` part runs as well, and you get both answers. `echo` never fails, so here it is fine. For anything bigger, use `if`, two lessons from now.\n\nExit codes are what scripts, build tools and CI systems use to decide whether to [[carry on|chain-stops]]. A failing test runner exits with a non-zero status, and that is how the pipeline knows to stop.\n\n::: context and-or Why "and" and "or"\nThe names come from logic. "Make the folder **and** go into it": if the first part fails, there is no point trying the second. "Find an error **or** say all is well": if the first part succeeds, the second is not needed.\n\nThe shell stops reading a chain as soon as it knows how things turned out. Engineers call this **short-circuiting**, and it is exactly why `b` sometimes never runs.\n:::\n\n::: context two-bars A pipe and an "or" look alike\nOn a US keyboard the vertical bar `|` is on the backslash key, above Enter, typed with Shift. One bar is a pipe: it sends the first command\'s output into the second, and both always run. Two bars, `||`, send nothing anywhere; they only decide whether the second command runs.\n\n```\n~/project $ grep -q ERROR deploy.log | echo "pipe"\npipe\n```\n\nWith one bar, `echo` ran even though `grep` found the error. A missing bar is an easy typo, and the shell will not warn you about it.\n:::\n\n::: context either-or Two roads from one answer\nThe status of the first command picks the road.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 150" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="90" y="10" width="180" height="32" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="31" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">grep -q ERROR deploy.log</text>\n  <line x1="150" y1="44" x2="90" y2="92" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="84,98 88,86 96,94" fill="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <line x1="210" y1="44" x2="270" y2="92" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="276,98 264,94 272,86" fill="#b4232c"/>\n  <text x="96" y="66" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#1d6fd1">status 0</text>\n  <text x="264" y="66" font-size="11" fill="#b4232c">status not 0</text>\n  <rect x="14" y="102" width="150" height="32" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="89" y="123" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">&amp;&amp; echo "failed"</text>\n  <rect x="196" y="102" width="150" height="32" rx="6" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="271" y="123" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">|| echo "ok"</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nHere "success" means `grep` found an error, so the good news is on the failure road. Read the status, not the word.\n:::\n\n::: context not-if-else When both sides run\nThe commands `true` and `false` do nothing except succeed and fail. They make the problem easy to see:\n\n```\n~/project $ true && false || echo "this runs too"\nthis runs too\n```\n\n`true` succeeded, so `false` ran. `false` failed, so `||` saw a failure and ran its part as well. With a real `if`, only one branch can ever run, whatever happens inside it. That is why `if` is the tool for anything that can fail.\n:::\n\n::: context chain-stops A chain that stops at the first failure\nReal projects chain their steps like this:\n\n```\nnpm test && npm run build && npm run deploy\n```\n\nIf a test fails, `npm test` exits with a non-zero status. The `&&` then skips the build and the deploy, so broken code never goes out. CI systems work on the same rule, one step after another, stopping at the first failure. Teams that build flight software lean on it hard: a build whose tests fail never gets as far as the hardware on the test bench.\n:::\n--- task\nThere are two deploy logs. For each one, write **one line** using `grep -q`, `&&` and `||`:\n\n- for `deploy-mon.log`, print `deploy-mon: failed` if it contains `ERROR`, and `deploy-mon: ok` if it does not;\n- for `deploy-tue.log`, print `deploy-tue: failed` if it contains `ERROR`, and `deploy-tue: ok` if it does not.\n--- starter\necho "build ok" > deploy-mon.log\necho "upload ok" >> deploy-mon.log\necho "ERROR: health check failed" >> deploy-mon.log\necho "build ok" > deploy-tue.log\necho "upload ok" >> deploy-tue.log\necho "health ok" >> deploy-tue.log\n--- solution\ngrep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log && echo "deploy-mon: failed" || echo "deploy-mon: ok"\ngrep -q ERROR deploy-tue.log && echo "deploy-tue: failed" || echo "deploy-tue: ok"\n--- hint\n`grep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log` prints nothing. Run `echo $?` straight after to see its answer: `0` means it found `ERROR`.\n--- hint\nThe shape is `question && echo "…" || echo "…"`: the `&&` part runs on success, the `||` part on failure.\n--- hint\n`grep -q ERROR deploy-mon.log && echo "deploy-mon: failed" || echo "deploy-mon: ok"`, then the same line with `tue` in place of `mon`.\n--- check shell | Monday\'s deploy is reported as failed\nprinted-line deploy-mon: failed\nnot-printed deploy-mon: ok\n--- check shell | Tuesday\'s deploy is reported as ok\nprinted-line deploy-tue: ok\nnot-printed deploy-tue: failed\n--- check shell | One line each, driven by grep\'s exit status\nused grep -q\nused &&\nused ||\n\n+++ practice | Only if it worked\n--- task\nWrite two lines, one for each job:\n\n1. Make a folder called `build`, and **only if that worked**, print `build ready`. Use `&&`. Run the same line a second time: `mkdir` fails because the folder is already there, so `build ready` must not appear again.\n2. Move into a folder called `release`, or, **if that fails**, print `no release folder`. Use `||`. There is no `release` folder, so the message appears.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nmkdir build && echo "build ready"\nmkdir build && echo "build ready"\ncd release || echo "no release folder"\n--- hint\n`a && b` runs `b` only when `a` finished with status `0`. `a || b` runs `b` only when `a` failed.\n--- hint\n`mkdir build && echo "build ready"`, twice. Then `cd release || echo "no release folder"`.\n--- check shell | The first run made the folder and said so\ndir build\nprinted-line build ready\nused &&\n--- check shell | The second run said nothing more\nran mkdir build\nlast-not-printed build ready\n--- check shell | The missing folder was reported\nprinted-line no release folder\nused ||\ncwd .\n\n+++ practice | The fallback copy\n--- task\nA program reads its settings from `config.txt`. The right settings for this computer should come from `config.local`, but it does not exist here; `config.default` does.\n\nWrite **one line** that copies `config.local` to `config.txt`, **or**, if that copy fails, copies `config.default` to `config.txt` instead.\n--- starter\necho "port=80" > config.default\n--- solution\ncp config.local config.txt || cp config.default config.txt\n--- hint\nThe second copy should run only if the first one fails. Which of `&&` and `||` means that?\n--- hint\n`cp config.local config.txt || cp config.default config.txt`.\n--- check shell | config.txt holds the default settings\nfile config.txt == port=80\n--- check shell | The local copy was tried first\nran cp config.local config.txt\n--- check shell | The default was the fallback\nused || cp config.default\n\n+++ practice | Clean up, or say there is nothing to clean\n--- task\nThis folder holds some `.tmp` files. Write **one line** that:\n\n- checks whether there are any `.tmp` files with `ls *.tmp`, sending both its outputs to `/dev/null` so it shows nothing;\n- if there are, deletes them with `rm *.tmp` and then prints `cleaned`;\n- if there are none, prints `nothing to clean`.\n\nRun the same line twice: the first run prints `cleaned`, the second `nothing to clean`.\n--- starter\ntouch a.tmp b.tmp notes.txt\n--- solution\nls *.tmp &> /dev/null && rm *.tmp && echo "cleaned" || echo "nothing to clean"\nls *.tmp &> /dev/null && rm *.tmp && echo "cleaned" || echo "nothing to clean"\n--- hint\n`&> /dev/null` throws away both standard output and standard error, so `ls` stays silent while it still sets its status.\n--- hint\nThe shape is `question && rm … && echo "cleaned" || echo "nothing to clean"`.\n--- check shell | The first run cleaned up\nprinted-line cleaned\ncount *.tmp == 0\nfile notes.txt\n--- check shell | The second run found nothing\nprinted-line nothing to clean\n--- check shell | ls was silenced, and rm did the deleting\nused /dev/null\nran rm\n\n+++ practice | Copy into a folder that is not there yet\n--- task\nWrite **one line** that copies `plan.txt` into the folder `backup/` and prints `saved` if the copy worked, or `copy failed` if it did not. Use `&&` and `||`.\n\nThere is no `backup` folder yet, so the first run must print `copy failed`, and must not create anything called `backup`. Then make the folder with `mkdir backup`, and run the same line again: this time it prints `saved`.\n--- starter\necho "burn at T+120" > plan.txt\n--- solution\ncp plan.txt backup/ && echo "saved" || echo "copy failed"\nmkdir backup\ncp plan.txt backup/ && echo "saved" || echo "copy failed"\n--- hint\nWithout the slash, `cp plan.txt backup` does not fail at all: it quietly makes a **file** called `backup`, and then `mkdir backup` cannot work. The slash says "into the folder".\n--- hint\n`cp plan.txt backup/ && echo "saved" || echo "copy failed"`.\n--- check shell | The first run failed, and said so\nprinted-line copy failed\n--- check shell | The second run saved the plan in the folder\ndir backup\nfile backup/plan.txt == burn at T+120\nprinted-line saved\n--- check shell | Decided with && and ||\nused &&\nused ||\n\n+++ practice | One bar short\n--- task\n`report.pdf` is here; `summary.pdf` is not. A teammate wanted a warning only when a file is missing, and typed:\n\n```\nls report.pdf | echo "report.pdf is missing"\n```\n\nIt prints `report.pdf is missing`, even though `report.pdf` is right there. Fix the line so that the warning appears only when `ls` fails. Run the fixed line for `summary.pdf` first (it prints `summary.pdf is missing`), and then, **last**, for `report.pdf`, where no warning may appear.\n--- starter\ntouch report.pdf\n--- solution\nls summary.pdf || echo "summary.pdf is missing"\nls report.pdf || echo "report.pdf is missing"\n--- hint\nOne bar is a pipe: both commands always run. Which join runs the second command only when the first failed?\n--- hint\nTwo bars: `ls summary.pdf || echo "summary.pdf is missing"`, then the same for `report.pdf`.\n--- check shell | The missing file is reported\nprinted-line summary.pdf is missing\n--- check shell | The file that is there raises no warning\nran ls report.pdf\nlast-not-printed report.pdf is missing\n--- check shell | Joined with two bars\nused report.pdf ||\n\n+++ practice | Go for launch\n--- task\nEach of `fuel.txt`, `weather.txt` and `range.txt` holds one word, either `GO` or `NO-GO`.\n\nWrite **one line** that prints `LAUNCH` only if all three files hold exactly `GO`, and prints `HOLD` otherwise. Use `grep -q` three times, joined with `&&`, then `||`.\n\nRun it: `range.txt` says `NO-GO`, so it prints `HOLD`. Then write `GO` into `range.txt` with `echo` and `>`, and run the same line again: it prints `LAUNCH`.\n\nCareful: `NO-GO` contains `GO`, so a plain `grep -q GO` would say yes to it.\n--- starter\necho "GO" > fuel.txt\necho "GO" > weather.txt\necho "NO-GO" > range.txt\n--- solution\ngrep -q "^GO$" fuel.txt && grep -q "^GO$" weather.txt && grep -q "^GO$" range.txt && echo "LAUNCH" || echo "HOLD"\necho "GO" > range.txt\ngrep -q "^GO$" fuel.txt && grep -q "^GO$" weather.txt && grep -q "^GO$" range.txt && echo "LAUNCH" || echo "HOLD"\n--- hint\nA pattern that must be the whole line starts with `^` and ends with `$`: `"^GO$"`.\n--- hint\nThe shell stops at the first question that fails, and jumps to the `||` part.\n--- hint\n`grep -q "^GO$" fuel.txt && grep -q "^GO$" weather.txt && grep -q "^GO$" range.txt && echo "LAUNCH" || echo "HOLD"`.\n--- check shell | The first poll held\nprinted-line HOLD\n--- check shell | After the fix, launch\nfile range.txt == GO\nprinted-line LAUNCH\n--- check shell | The whole word was matched\nused ^GO$\nused &&\nused ||\n\n=== term3-05a | Loops: for\n--- teach\nLast lesson, a command\'s exit status decided what ran next. This lesson is about something different: doing the same job many times without typing it many times.\n\nPicture a pile of letters that all need a stamp. Nobody writes new instructions for every letter. You say it once: "for each letter in the pile, stamp it." The shell can work the same way.\n\nA **[[loop|loop]]** is a set of commands the shell runs again and again. The **`for` loop** runs the same commands once for each item in a list:\n\n```\n~/project $ for planet in mercury venus earth; do echo "hello $planet"; done\nhello mercury\nhello venus\nhello earth\n```\n\nRead it aloud: "for each planet in the list mercury, venus, earth, do: echo hello planet. Done."\n\nHere is each part:\n\n- `for planet in` names a variable, `planet`, like the ones you made in the variables lesson. Each time round, the loop puts the next item into it. One trip round the loop is called an **[[iteration|iteration]]**.\n- `mercury venus earth` is the **list**: the items, separated by spaces.\n- `do` … `done` wrap the **body**: the commands to repeat.\n- `;`, the **[[semicolon|semicolon]]**, ends one command so the next can follow on the same line. The loop needs one before `do` and one before `done`.\n\n### The semicolon on its own\n\nA `;` runs commands one after another, whether or not the first one worked. That is the difference from `&&`, which only goes on after a success:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo one; echo two\none\ntwo\n~/project $ ls nothing.txt; echo "still here"\nls: cannot access \'nothing.txt\': No such file or directory\nstill here\n```\n\n### More than one command in the body\n\nSeparate them with `;`, and each iteration runs all of them, in order:\n\n```\n~/project $ for planet in mercury venus earth; do mkdir "$planet"; echo "made $planet"; done\nmade mercury\nmade venus\nmade earth\n```\n\n### A wildcard as the list\n\nThe list can be a wildcard. The shell turns `*.txt` into the matching names before the loop starts, as you saw in the wildcards lesson. Here `f` is short for "file"; any variable name works.\n\n```\n~/project $ ls\nchecklist.txt  flight plan.txt\n~/project $ for f in *.txt; do echo "checking $f"; done\nchecking checklist.txt\nchecking flight plan.txt\n```\n\n### Command output as the list\n\nThe list can also come from `$(…)`, the command substitution from earlier in this course:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat planets.txt\nmercury\nvenus\n~/project $ for p in $(cat planets.txt); do echo "planet: $p"; done\nplanet: mercury\nplanet: venus\n```\n\nThe output of `$(…)` is cut up at every space and new line, so this suits lists of single words. For file names, which can hold spaces, use a wildcard.\n\n### On several lines\n\nIn a script (coming soon in this course), the same loop is often spread over lines. Where a line ends, no `;` is needed:\n\n```\nfor f in *.txt\ndo\n  echo "checking $f"\ndone\n```\n\nThis practice terminal takes one line at a time, so type the one-line form here.\n\n**Watch out:** quote the loop variable, `"$f"`, whenever you hand it to a command. A file called `flight plan.txt` is one name. But an unquoted `$f` is cut at the space into two words, `flight` and `plan.txt`. This is called **[[word splitting|word-splitting]]**, and `cp` goes looking for two files that do not exist:\n\n```\n~/project $ for f in *.txt; do cp $f backup/; done\ncp: cannot stat \'flight\': No such file or directory\ncp: cannot stat \'plan.txt\': No such file or directory\n~/project $ for f in *.txt; do cp "$f" backup/; done\n~/project $ ls backup\nchecklist.txt  flight plan.txt\n```\n\nLoops are where the terminal starts to save [[real work|loops-for-real]]: ten files or ten thousand, the line is the same length.\n\n::: context loop Why it is called a loop\nDraw the steps as boxes with arrows between them, and a loop is where an arrow bends back to an earlier box. The shell checks for another item, runs the body, and comes back round. When the list runs out, it leaves the loop and carries on after `done`.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 180" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">\n      <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <rect x="16" y="46" width="118" height="44" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="75" y="66" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">any items left</text>\n  <text x="75" y="81" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">in the list?</text>\n  <rect x="212" y="20" width="132" height="52" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="278" y="41" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">put the next item</text>\n  <text x="278" y="57" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">in planet, run body</text>\n  <line x1="134" y1="58" x2="210" y2="46" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>\n  <text x="172" y="42" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">yes</text>\n  <path d="M 278 72 Q 250 118 136 82" fill="none" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>\n  <text x="250" y="118" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">back round</text>\n  <line x1="75" y1="90" x2="75" y2="126" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>\n  <text x="90" y="112" font-size="12" fill="#b4232c">no</text>\n  <rect x="30" y="128" width="90" height="34" rx="6" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="75" y="150" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">done</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context iteration One trip round\n**Iteration** comes from the Latin *iterum*, "again". Each trip round the loop is one iteration, so a list of three planets means three iterations, and the body runs three times.\n\nOne surprise: if a wildcard matches nothing, the shell passes the pattern along unchanged, as the wildcards lesson showed. So in a folder with no `.png` files, `for f in *.png; do echo "found $f"; done` runs once and prints `found *.png`. That is worth remembering when a loop does something odd.\n:::\n\n::: context semicolon Three ways to join commands\nYou now know three joins. `a ; b` runs `a`, then `b`, no matter what. `a && b` runs `b` only if `a` succeeded. `a || b` runs `b` only if `a` failed. The loop uses `;` because it is not asking a question; it only marks where one piece of the line ends and the next begins. Pressing Enter at the end of a line does the same job, which is why a loop written over several lines needs no semicolons.\n:::\n\n::: context word-splitting The shell cuts at spaces\nAfter the shell puts a variable\'s value into a line, it cuts the result into words at every space. Then each word is a separate argument. Double quotes stop the cutting: everything inside them stays one word.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 120" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <text x="10" y="24" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">cp $f backup/</text>\n  <rect x="10" y="32" width="40" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="30" y="50" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">cp</text>\n  <rect x="58" y="32" width="60" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="88" y="50" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">flight</text>\n  <rect x="126" y="32" width="72" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="162" y="50" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">plan.txt</text>\n  <rect x="206" y="32" width="70" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="241" y="50" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">backup/</text>\n  <text x="10" y="80" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">cp "$f" backup/</text>\n  <rect x="10" y="88" width="40" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="30" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">cp</text>\n  <rect x="58" y="88" width="140" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="128" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">flight plan.txt</text>\n  <rect x="206" y="88" width="70" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="241" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">backup/</text>\n  <text x="286" y="50" font-size="11" fill="#b4232c">4 words</text>\n  <text x="286" y="106" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">3 words</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context loops-for-real How engineers use loops\nReal projects are full of "do this to every file" jobs. A flight software team might run the same test on every recorded telemetry file, convert every data file from one format to another, or check that every source file compiles. Written as a loop, the job works the same for three files as for three thousand, and nobody has to type the names. Later in this course you will put loops inside scripts and use one to tidy a whole downloads folder.\n:::\n--- task\nMake three folders, `mercury`, `venus` and `earth`, with **one** `for` loop. Then write a second `for` loop over `*.txt` that, for every `.txt` file, copies it into the `backup` folder and prints `copied` followed by its name (for example `copied checklist.txt`). One of the notes, `flight plan.txt`, has a space in its name.\n--- starter\nmkdir backup\necho "fuel check" > "flight plan.txt"\necho "go for launch" > checklist.txt\n--- solution\nfor planet in mercury venus earth; do mkdir "$planet"; done\nfor f in *.txt; do cp "$f" backup/; echo "copied $f"; done\n--- hint\nThe first loop\'s list is the three names: `for planet in mercury venus earth; do …; done`, with `mkdir "$planet"` as the body.\n--- hint\nThe second loop\'s list is the wildcard `*.txt`. Its body has two commands, `cp` and `echo`, with a `;` between them.\n--- hint\n`for f in *.txt; do cp "$f" backup/; echo "copied $f"; done`. Keep the quotes around `"$f"`, or `flight plan.txt` falls apart.\n--- check shell | The three planet folders exist\ndir mercury\ndir venus\ndir earth\nused for\n--- check shell | Every note was copied into backup, space and all\ncount backup/*.txt == 2\nfile backup/checklist.txt == go for launch\nprinted-line copied checklist.txt\nprinted-line copied flight plan.txt\n--- check shell | Done with loops\nused do\nused done\nran cp\n\n+++ practice | A folder for every weekday\n--- task\nWith **one** `for` loop, make five folders, `mon`, `tue`, `wed`, `thu` and `fri`, and inside each one an empty file called `log.txt`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nfor day in mon tue wed thu fri; do mkdir "$day"; touch "$day/log.txt"; done\n--- hint\nThe list is the five names. The body has two commands, joined by `;`.\n--- hint\nInside the body, `"$day/log.txt"` is the file inside the folder that was just made.\n--- check shell | The five folders exist\ndir mon\ndir wed\ndir fri\ncount * == 5\n--- check shell | Each has its log.txt\ncount */log.txt == 5\n--- check shell | Done with one loop\nused for\nused done\n\n+++ practice | Badges from a list\n--- task\n`crew.txt` holds three names, one per line. With **one** `for` loop whose list comes from `$(cat crew.txt)`, do two things for each name:\n\n1. print `badge for ` followed by the name, like `badge for ada`;\n2. make an empty file called the name followed by `.badge`, like `ada.badge`.\n--- starter\necho "ada" > crew.txt\necho "lin" >> crew.txt\necho "sam" >> crew.txt\n--- solution\nfor name in $(cat crew.txt); do echo "badge for $name"; touch "$name.badge"; done\n--- hint\nThe list is `$(cat crew.txt)`: the shell runs `cat` first and cuts its output into words.\n--- hint\nThe body is `echo "badge for $name"; touch "$name.badge"`.\n--- check shell | One line per name, in order\nprinted-exactly badge for ada\\nbadge for lin\\nbadge for sam\n--- check shell | Three badge files\ncount *.badge == 3\nfile sam.badge\n--- check shell | The list came from the file\nused $(cat crew.txt)\nused for\n\n+++ practice | Errors in every log\n--- task\nThere are three `.log` files. With **one** `for` loop over `*.log`, print one line per log: its name, a space, and how many of its lines contain `ERROR`, like this:\n\n```\na.log 2\n```\n\nCount with `grep -c` inside `$(…)`.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR disk" > a.log\necho "INFO ok" >> a.log\necho "ERROR net" >> a.log\necho "INFO ok" > b.log\necho "INFO boot" > c.log\necho "ERROR fan" >> c.log\n--- solution\nfor f in *.log; do echo "$f $(grep -c ERROR "$f")"; done\n--- hint\n`grep -c ERROR "$f"` prints the count for one file. Put it inside `$(…)` in the `echo`.\n--- hint\n`for f in *.log; do echo "$f $(grep -c ERROR "$f")"; done`.\n--- check shell | A count for every log, zero included\nprinted-exactly a.log 2\\nb.log 0\\nc.log 1\n--- check shell | Looped over the logs\nused *.log\nused for\n--- check shell | Counted with grep -c\nused grep -c\n\n+++ practice | Pictures with spaces in their names\n--- task\nThere are three `.png` pictures here, and two of them have spaces in their names. With **one** `for` loop over `*.png`, copy every picture into the folder `pics` and print `saved ` followed by its name, like `saved moon.png`.\n\nEvery picture must arrive whole, spaces and all.\n--- starter\nmkdir pics\necho "red dust" > "mars photo.png"\ntouch moon.png "deep space 1.png"\n--- solution\nfor f in *.png; do cp "$f" pics/; echo "saved $f"; done\n--- hint\nUnquoted, `$f` is cut at every space, so `cp` goes looking for files called `mars` and `photo.png`.\n--- hint\nPut double quotes around `"$f"` in the `cp`.\n--- check shell | All three pictures are in pics\ncount pics/*.png == 3\ncount pics/deep?space?1.png == 1\n--- check shell | The names with spaces arrived whole\nfile pics/moon.png\ncount pics/mars?photo.png == 1\n--- check shell | Each one was reported whole\nprinted-line saved deep space 1.png\nprinted-line saved mars photo.png\n\n+++ practice | The loop that ran once\n--- task\nThe folder holds three `.csv` files. A teammate wanted one line printed per file and typed:\n\n```\nfor f in "*.csv"; do echo "row file: $f"; done\n```\n\nIt ran once and printed `row file: *.csv`. Fix the loop so it prints one line per `.csv` file:\n\n```\nrow file: crew.csv\nrow file: fuel.csv\nrow file: orbit.csv\n```\n--- starter\ntouch crew.csv fuel.csv orbit.csv notes.txt\n--- solution\nfor f in *.csv; do echo "row file: $f"; done\n--- hint\nQuotes switch wildcards off. With quotes around `*.csv`, the list is one word: the pattern itself.\n--- hint\nTake the quotes off the wildcard in the list. Keep them around `$f` in the body.\n--- check shell | One line per csv file\nprinted-exactly row file: crew.csv\\nrow file: fuel.csv\\nrow file: orbit.csv\n--- check shell | The wildcard was left for the shell\nused in *.csv\n--- check shell | Done with a loop\nused for\nused done\n\n+++ practice | Sort the telemetry by sensor\n--- task\nThe telemetry files are named after a run and a sensor: `t1-temp.dat`, `t2-volt.dat`, and so on.\n\n1. With **one** `for` loop over the list `temp volt`, make a folder for each sensor and move that sensor\'s files into it: every `temp` file into `temp/`, every `volt` file into `volt/`.\n2. With a second `for` loop over the same list, print each sensor and how many files its folder holds, counted with `ls` and `wc -l`:\n\n```\ntemp 2\nvolt 3\n```\n--- starter\ntouch t1-temp.dat t2-temp.dat t1-volt.dat t2-volt.dat t3-volt.dat\n--- solution\nfor s in temp volt; do mkdir "$s"; mv *-"$s".dat "$s"/; done\nfor s in temp volt; do echo "$s $(ls "$s" | wc -l)"; done\n--- hint\nInside the body, a wildcard can be built around the variable: `*-"$s".dat` means "any name ending in a dash, the sensor, then `.dat`".\n--- hint\nThe second loop\'s body is one `echo`, with `$(ls "$s" | wc -l)` inside it.\n--- check shell | Every file is in its sensor\'s folder\ncount temp/*.dat == 2\ncount volt/*.dat == 3\ncount *.dat == 0\n--- check shell | The counts were printed\nprinted-exactly temp 2\\nvolt 3\n--- check shell | Done with loops\nused in temp volt\nused wc -l\n\n=== term3-05 | Renaming many files: cutting the ends off names\n--- teach\nLast lesson, a `for` loop ran the same commands once for every file. Now you will make a loop **rename** files. To rename a file you need its new name, so this lesson shows how to build a new name out of the old one.\n\nThink of relabelling jars in a kitchen. You do not rewrite the whole label. You peel off the end, "jam (old)", and write a new ending. The shell can peel the end, or the start, off the text in a variable.\n\n### Cutting off the end: %\n\n`${f%.txt}` means "`f` with `.txt` cut off the **end**". The `%` sign is the "cut from the end" instruction, and the curly brackets hold it all together. This is one kind of **[[parameter expansion|parameter-expansion]]**: the shell changes a variable\'s value on its way into the line.\n\n```\n~/project $ f=log.txt\n~/project $ echo "${f%.txt}"\nlog\n~/project $ echo "${f%.txt}.md"\nlog.md\n```\n\nIn the second line, `.md` is stuck straight onto the trimmed name. The variable itself does not change. It still holds `log.txt`:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "$f"\nlog.txt\n```\n\nIf the end does not match, nothing is cut:\n\n```\n~/project $ f=photo.png\n~/project $ echo "${f%.txt}"\nphoto.png\n```\n\n### Cutting off the start: #\n\n`${f#draft_}` means "`f` with `draft_` cut off the **start**". The `#` sign is "cut from the start". There is a [[keyboard trick|keyboard-trick]] for remembering which is which.\n\n```\n~/project $ f=draft_plan.txt\n~/project $ echo "${f#draft_}"\nplan.txt\n```\n\n### Putting it in a loop\n\nNow join it to last lesson\'s loop. For every file whose name starts with `draft_`, move it to the same name without the `draft_`:\n\n```\nfor f in draft_*; do mv "$f" "${f#draft_}"; done\n```\n\n`mv` renames here, as it did in the basics course. Changing a name, even the [[file extension|file-extension]] at the end of it, only changes the name. What is inside the file stays exactly the same.\n\n### Rehearse first: the dry run\n\nA loop that renames things changes many files in one go, so check it before it runs. A **[[dry run|dry-run]]** is a rehearsal: put `echo` in front of the real command. Then `echo` prints each command instead of running it. Read them, and only then run the loop again without the `echo`.\n\n```\n~/project $ ls\ndraft_map.png  draft_old list.txt  draft_plan.txt  readme.txt\n~/project $ for f in draft_*; do echo mv "$f" "${f#draft_}"; done\nmv draft_map.png map.png\nmv draft_old list.txt old list.txt\nmv draft_plan.txt plan.txt\n```\n\nNothing has moved yet. Each printed line is one `mv` the real loop would run. `readme.txt` is not in the list, because `draft_*` does not match it. A dry run can also show you [[two files heading for the same name|mv-overwrites]] before any harm is done.\n\n**Watch out:** keep the quotes, `"$f"` and `"${f#draft_}"`. A file called `draft_old list.txt` is one name. Unquoted, it is cut at the space into `draft_old` and `list.txt`, and `mv` is handed three names instead of two and stops with an error. The dry run cannot warn you about this, because `echo` prints the words the same way with or without the quotes. So check the quotes with your own eyes.\n\n::: context parameter-expansion The official name\nThe shell calls a variable a **parameter**, and filling in its value is **expanding** it. Plain `$f` is the simplest parameter expansion. The curly-bracket forms do extra work on the way. A single `%` or `#` cuts the shortest match; doubled, `%%` and `##`, they cut the longest match. The pattern may hold wildcards too: with `f=a.tar.gz`, `${f%.*}` gives `a.tar` and `${f##*.}` gives `gz`. You will use `##` in the downloads-tidying lesson later in this course.\n:::\n\n::: context keyboard-trick # left, % right\nOn a standard US keyboard, look at the number keys 3, 4 and 5. The `#` is on the 3, to the left of the `$` on the 4. The `%` is on the 5, to its right. So `#` cuts from the left, the start, and `%` cuts from the right, the end.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 130" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="70" y="16" width="60" height="56" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="100" y="40" font-size="18" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">#</text>\n  <text x="100" y="62" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">3</text>\n  <rect x="150" y="16" width="60" height="56" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="40" font-size="18" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$</text>\n  <text x="180" y="62" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">4</text>\n  <rect x="230" y="16" width="60" height="56" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="260" y="40" font-size="18" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">%</text>\n  <text x="260" y="62" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">5</text>\n  <text x="100" y="96" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">left of $</text>\n  <text x="100" y="114" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">cuts the start</text>\n  <text x="260" y="96" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">right of $</text>\n  <text x="260" y="114" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">cuts the end</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context file-extension The end of a name is only a label\nA **file extension** is the part of a name after the last dot: `.jpeg`, `.txt`, `.pdf`. It is a hint for people and programs about what kind of file it is, but it is still only part of the name. `.jpeg` and `.jpg` are two spellings for the same picture format, JPEG, so renaming one to the other changes nothing inside the file. That is why the task can promise the contents survive.\n:::\n\n::: context dry-run Rehearsals are an engineering habit\nLaunch teams rehearse too. Before a new rocket flies, NASA runs countdown rehearsals where everything happens except the launch. Software tools copy the idea: many have a "dry run" flag that shows what would happen and changes nothing, like `rsync --dry-run` for copying files or `make -n` for builds. Putting `echo` in front of a command is the do-it-yourself version, and it works with any command.\n:::\n\n::: context mv-overwrites mv does not ask\n`mv` replaces a file that already has the new name, without asking. If `plan.txt` already existed, renaming `draft_plan.txt` to it would quietly wipe out the old `plan.txt`. `mv -n`, "no clobber", refuses to overwrite, and `mv -i`, "interactive", asks first. This is one more reason the dry run is worth the few seconds: you can spot two files heading for the same name before it happens.\n:::\n--- task\nRename every `.jpeg` file in this folder so it ends in `.jpg` instead (`beach.jpeg` becomes `beach.jpg`), using **one** `for` loop and `${f%.jpeg}`. One of the files, `old boat.jpeg`, has a space in its name, and the files\' contents must survive. Leave `readme.txt` alone.\n--- starter\necho "pixels" > beach.jpeg\ntouch sunset.jpeg "old boat.jpeg" readme.txt\n--- solution\nfor f in *.jpeg; do mv "$f" "${f%.jpeg}.jpg"; done\n--- hint\nTry a dry run first: `for f in *.jpeg; do echo mv "$f" "${f%.jpeg}.jpg"; done`. It only prints.\n--- hint\nWhen the printed commands look right, run the same loop again without the `echo`. Keep the quotes around `"$f"` and around `"${f%.jpeg}.jpg"`.\n--- hint\nThe real loop is the dry run minus the word `echo`: `for f in *.jpeg; do mv "$f" "${f%.jpeg}.jpg"; done`. Then `ls` should show three `.jpg` files and `readme.txt`.\n--- check shell | No .jpeg files are left\ncount *.jpeg == 0\n--- check shell | Every photo is now a .jpg, contents and all\ncount *.jpg == 3\nfile beach.jpg == pixels\nfile readme.txt\n--- check shell | Done with a loop\nused for\nused do\nused done\n\n+++ practice | Names without their ending\n--- task\nThere are three `.csv` files here. With **one** `for` loop over `*.csv`, print each file\'s name **without** `.csv` on the end, one per line:\n\n```\nfeb\njan\nmar\n```\n\nUse `${f%.csv}`. Nothing is renamed.\n--- starter\ntouch jan.csv feb.csv mar.csv notes.txt\n--- solution\nfor f in *.csv; do echo "${f%.csv}"; done\n--- hint\n`%` cuts from the end. `${f%.csv}` is the name held in `f`, with `.csv` cut off.\n--- hint\nThe body is a single `echo "${f%.csv}"`.\n--- check shell | Each name without its ending\nprinted-exactly feb\\njan\\nmar\n--- check shell | Cut with %\nused ${f%.csv}\nused for\n--- check shell | Nothing was renamed\ncount *.csv == 3\n\n+++ practice | From version 1 to version 2\n--- task\nTwo settings files start with `v1_`: `v1_engine.cfg` and `v1_radio.cfg`. Rename both so they start with `v2_` instead (`v1_engine.cfg` becomes `v2_engine.cfg`), with **one** `for` loop and `${f#v1_}`. Their contents must survive, and `notes.txt` stays as it is.\n--- starter\necho "thrust=high" > v1_engine.cfg\necho "band=S" > v1_radio.cfg\ntouch notes.txt\n--- solution\nfor f in v1_*; do mv "$f" "v2_${f#v1_}"; done\n--- hint\n`${f#v1_}` is the name without `v1_` at the start. Put `v2_` in front of it.\n--- hint\nThe list is `v1_*`, and the body is `mv "$f" "v2_${f#v1_}"`.\n--- check shell | No v1_ files are left\ncount v1_* == 0\n--- check shell | Both are v2_ now, contents and all\nfile v2_engine.cfg == thrust=high\nfile v2_radio.cfg == band=S\nfile notes.txt\n--- check shell | Done with a loop and #\nused for\nused ${f#v1_}\n\n+++ practice | Put your name on every log\n--- task\nRename every `.log` file so that a dash and your user name come before `.log`: `boot.log` becomes `boot-you.log`. Use **one** `for` loop, `${f%.log}` and `$(whoami)`, so the same line would work for anyone. The contents must survive.\n--- starter\necho "systems up" > boot.log\necho "link ok" > radio.log\necho "pressure ok" > pump.log\n--- solution\nfor f in *.log; do mv "$f" "${f%.log}-$(whoami).log"; done\n--- hint\nBuild the new name from three pieces: the old name without `.log`, then `-$(whoami)`, then `.log`.\n--- hint\nTry a dry run first: `for f in *.log; do echo mv "$f" "${f%.log}-$(whoami).log"; done`.\n--- check shell | Every log carries the name\ncount *-you.log == 3\nfile boot-you.log == systems up\n--- check shell | No old names are left\nmissing boot.log\nmissing radio.log\nmissing pump.log\n--- check shell | The name came from whoami\nused $(whoami)\nused ${f%.log}\n\n+++ practice | Double endings\n--- task\nThree archives end in `.tar.gz`; one of them has a space in its name. Rename each so it ends in `.tgz` instead: `site.tar.gz` becomes `site.tgz`. Use **one** `for` loop over `*.tar.gz` and `%`.\n\n`notes.gz` is not an archive of that kind: leave it alone. The contents must survive.\n--- starter\necho "html" > site.tar.gz\ntouch logs.tar.gz "old logs.tar.gz" notes.gz\n--- solution\nfor f in *.tar.gz; do mv "$f" "${f%.tar.gz}.tgz"; done\n--- hint\nCutting only `.gz` would leave `site.tar.tgz`. Cut the whole double ending.\n--- hint\n`"${f%.tar.gz}.tgz"`, and keep the quotes for the name with the space.\n--- check shell | Every archive is a .tgz now\ncount *.tgz == 3\nfile site.tgz == html\ncount old?logs.tgz == 1\n--- check shell | No double endings are left\ncount *.tar* == 0\n--- check shell | notes.gz was left alone\nfile notes.gz\n\n+++ practice | The wrong end\n--- task\nA teammate wanted to rename every `.jpeg` file to end in `.jpg`, and ran:\n\n```\nfor f in *.jpeg; do mv "$f" "${f#.jpeg}.jpg"; done\n```\n\nNow the photos are called `beach.jpeg.jpg`, `sunset.jpeg.jpg` and `old boat.jpeg.jpg`. Rename every `.jpeg.jpg` file so that it ends in just `.jpg` (`beach.jpeg.jpg` becomes `beach.jpg`), with one `for` loop. The contents must survive.\n--- starter\necho "pixels" > beach.jpeg\ntouch sunset.jpeg "old boat.jpeg"\nfor f in *.jpeg; do mv "$f" "${f#.jpeg}.jpg"; done\n--- solution\nfor f in *.jpeg.jpg; do mv "$f" "${f%.jpeg.jpg}.jpg"; done\n--- hint\n`#` cuts from the start of the name, and no name starts with `.jpeg`, so nothing was cut. Which sign cuts from the end?\n--- hint\nLoop over `*.jpeg.jpg`, and cut `.jpeg.jpg` off the end before adding `.jpg` back.\n--- check shell | No doubled names are left\ncount *.jpeg* == 0\n--- check shell | Three .jpg photos, contents and all\ncount *.jpg == 3\nfile beach.jpg == pixels\ncount old?boat.jpg == 1\n--- check shell | Fixed with %\nused %\nused for\n\n+++ practice | Tidy the camera names\n--- task\nThe camera saves pictures with an `IMG_` prefix and the ending `.jpg`: `IMG_launch.jpg`, `IMG_orbit.jpg` and `IMG_landing site.jpg`. Rename every file that starts with `IMG_` so it has **no** prefix and ends in `.jpeg`: `IMG_orbit.jpg` becomes `orbit.jpeg`.\n\n1. First do a dry run: the same loop with `echo` in front of `mv`, so it only prints the commands.\n2. Then run it for real.\n\nUse **one** `for` loop over `IMG_*`. Inside the body, store the name without `IMG_` in a variable first, then cut `.jpg` off that. `README.jpg` does not start with `IMG_` and must be left alone.\n--- starter\necho "stars" > IMG_orbit.jpg\ntouch IMG_launch.jpg "IMG_landing site.jpg" README.jpg\n--- solution\nfor f in IMG_*; do n="${f#IMG_}"; echo mv "$f" "${n%.jpg}.jpeg"; done\nfor f in IMG_*; do n="${f#IMG_}"; mv "$f" "${n%.jpg}.jpeg"; done\n--- hint\nOne expansion cuts one end. Do it in two steps: `n="${f#IMG_}"` cuts the start, then `"${n%.jpg}.jpeg"` cuts the end and adds the new ending.\n--- hint\nThe body has two commands: set `n`, then `mv`. For the dry run, put `echo` in front of `mv` only.\n--- check shell | The dry run printed the plan first\nprinted-line mv IMG_orbit.jpg orbit.jpeg\n--- check shell | Every IMG_ file was renamed\ncount IMG_* == 0\ncount *.jpeg == 3\nfile orbit.jpeg == stars\ncount landing?site.jpeg == 1\n--- check shell | README.jpg was left alone\nfile README.jpg\n\n=== term3-06a | Decisions: if\n--- teach\nIn the exit-status lessons, `&&` and `||` let a command\'s status pick what ran next, and you were told that `a && b || c` is not a true either/or. This lesson gives you the real one: `if`.\n\nYou make choices like this every day: "if it is raining, take an umbrella; otherwise, wear sunglasses." There is a question, and then one of two things happens, never both.\n\n### if, then, else, fi\n\n**`if`** runs a command and looks at its exit status. A `0`, success, takes the **`then`** part. Anything else takes the **`else`** part. Each part is called a **[[branch|branch]]**. The whole thing ends with **`fi`**, which is [["if" backwards|fi-backwards]].\n\n```\n~/project $ if cd reports; then echo "moved in"; else echo "no reports folder"; fi\nmoved in\n~/project/reports $\n```\n\nRead it aloud: "if cd into reports works, then echo moved in, else echo no reports folder. End of if." The command after `if` is the **[[condition|condition]]**: the question being asked. A handy one is `grep -q`: it prints nothing; it only sets the status, as you saw in the exit-status lesson.\n\nThe pieces are separated by `;`, like the loop: one before `then`, one before `else`, one before `fi`.\n\n### Without an else\n\nThe `else` part is optional. Leave it out, and when the condition fails, nothing happens:\n\n```\n~/project $ if grep -q WARN server.log; then echo "warnings found"; fi\nwarnings found\n```\n\n### Any command can be the question\n\n`if` does not care what the command is. It only looks at the status:\n\n```\n~/project $ if mkdir review; then echo "made it"; else echo "could not"; fi\nmade it\n~/project $ if mkdir review; then echo "made it"; else echo "could not"; fi\nmkdir: cannot create directory \'review\': File exists\ncould not\n```\n\nThe second time, `mkdir` failed, because the folder was already there, so the `else` branch ran.\n\n### More than two choices: elif\n\n**`elif`**, short for "else if", adds another question. The shell asks them in order and runs the first branch whose condition succeeds. `else` catches everything left over:\n\n```\n~/project $ if grep -q ERROR radio.log; then echo "errors"; elif grep -q WARN radio.log; then echo "warnings"; else echo "clean"; fi\nclean\n```\n\n### Why not && and ||?\n\nIn `a && b || c`, if `b` fails, `c` runs as well. Here `ls` fails in the middle, so both "sides" happen:\n\n```\n~/project $ grep -q ERROR app.log && ls nothing.txt || echo "all quiet"\nls: cannot access \'nothing.txt\': No such file or directory\nall quiet\n```\n\nThe log has errors, yet it also says "all quiet". With `if`, exactly one branch runs, whatever happens inside it. This is how real [[scripts make go/no-go calls|go-no-go]].\n\n**Watch out:** every `if` needs its `fi`, and a `;` before `then`. Leave out the `;` in `if grep -q WARN server.log then …`, and the shell hands `then` to `grep` as one more word. The line ends in `syntax error near unexpected token`, and nothing runs. When an `if` line fails that way, count the semicolons first.\n\n::: context branch A fork in the road\nA program usually runs straight down, one command after another. An `if` is a fork: the road splits in two, and the program goes down exactly one side before the roads meet again at `fi`. Programmers call each side a branch.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 190" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">\n      <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <rect x="95" y="10" width="170" height="32" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="31" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">grep -q ERROR app.log</text>\n  <line x1="150" y1="42" x2="88" y2="84" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>\n  <line x1="210" y1="42" x2="272" y2="84" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>\n  <text x="96" y="62" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">status 0</text>\n  <text x="270" y="62" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">not 0</text>\n  <rect x="14" y="86" width="150" height="36" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="89" y="102" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">then</text>\n  <text x="89" y="116" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">echo "look at the log"</text>\n  <rect x="196" y="86" width="150" height="36" rx="6" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="271" y="102" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">else</text>\n  <text x="271" y="116" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">echo "all quiet"</text>\n  <line x1="89" y1="122" x2="160" y2="152" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>\n  <line x1="271" y1="122" x2="200" y2="152" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>\n  <rect x="150" y="154" width="60" height="28" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="173" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">fi</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context fi-backwards Why fi, and why done\nThe shell\'s grammar comes from the Bourne shell, which Stephen Bourne wrote at Bell Labs in the 1970s. He borrowed an idea from a language called ALGOL 68: close a block with its opening word spelled backwards. So `if` ends with `fi`, and `case`, a command you may meet later, ends with `esac`. ALGOL closed a loop\'s `do` with `od`, but `od` was already the name of a Unix command, so the shell\'s loops end with `done` instead.\n:::\n\n::: context condition The question an if asks\nA **condition** is a question with a yes-or-no answer. In most programming languages it is a comparison, like `x > 3` in Python. In the shell it is always a command, and the answer is its exit status: `0` means yes, anything else means no. Next lesson you meet a command built for asking questions about files, words and numbers, so your conditions can ask much more than "did grep find it?".\n:::\n\n::: context go-no-go Go or no-go\nBefore a launch, the launch director polls each team, and each answers "go" or "no-go". One no-go and the count stops. Build scripts work the same way. A line like `if make test; then echo "go"; else echo "no-go"; exit 1; fi` runs the tests and stops the script unless they all pass. Continuous integration (CI) systems, which build and test every change automatically, rely on exactly these exit statuses to decide whether a change may go ahead.\n:::\n--- task\nWrite a one-line `if` that prints `app.log: look at the log` when `app.log` contains `ERROR`, and `app.log: all quiet` when it does not. Use `grep -q` as the condition. Then write the same line for `radio.log`, printing `radio.log: look at the log` or `radio.log: all quiet`.\n--- starter\necho "08:00 INFO systems up" > app.log\necho "08:02 ERROR disk almost full" >> app.log\necho "08:00 INFO radio check ok" > radio.log\necho "08:05 INFO signal strong" >> radio.log\n--- solution\nif grep -q ERROR app.log; then echo "app.log: look at the log"; else echo "app.log: all quiet"; fi\nif grep -q ERROR radio.log; then echo "radio.log: look at the log"; else echo "radio.log: all quiet"; fi\n--- hint\nThe shape is `if CONDITION; then …; else …; fi`, and the condition is `grep -q ERROR app.log`.\n--- hint\n`if grep -q ERROR app.log; then echo "app.log: look at the log"; else echo "app.log: all quiet"; fi`\n--- hint\nFor the second line, press the Up arrow to bring the first one back, and change `app.log` to `radio.log` in all three places.\n--- check shell | app.log\'s errors were spotted\nprinted-line app.log: look at the log\nnot-printed app.log: all quiet\n--- check shell | radio.log was reported as quiet\nprinted-line radio.log: all quiet\nnot-printed radio.log: look at the log\n--- check shell | Decided with if, on grep\'s exit status\nused if grep -q\nused then\nused else\nused fi\n\n+++ practice | Was abort called?\n--- task\nTwo radio transcripts, `mon.txt` and `tue.txt`. Someone may have called an abort, written as `abort` in any mix of capitals (`ABORT`, `Abort`, …).\n\nWrite a one-line `if` for `mon.txt` that prints `mon.txt: ABORT CALLED` if it mentions abort in any capitals, and `mon.txt: continue` if it does not. Then write the same line for `tue.txt`.\n--- starter\necho "T+030 all stations nominal" > mon.txt\necho "T+045 Abort, abort, abort" >> mon.txt\necho "T+030 all stations nominal" > tue.txt\necho "T+045 aboard and strapped in" >> tue.txt\n--- solution\nif grep -qi "abort" mon.txt; then echo "mon.txt: ABORT CALLED"; else echo "mon.txt: continue"; fi\nif grep -qi "abort" tue.txt; then echo "tue.txt: ABORT CALLED"; else echo "tue.txt: continue"; fi\n--- hint\nThe condition is a quiet `grep` that ignores capitals: `-q` and `-i` share one dash as `-qi`.\n--- hint\n`aboard` is not `abort`: check that `tue.txt` really comes out as `continue`.\n--- check shell | Monday\'s abort was spotted\nprinted-line mon.txt: ABORT CALLED\n--- check shell | Tuesday carries on\nprinted-line tue.txt: continue\n--- check shell | Decided with if\nused if grep\nused then\nused fi\n\n+++ practice | Make the archive, or say it is there\n--- task\nWrite a one-line `if` whose condition is `mkdir archive`:\n\n- if the folder was made, move every `.old` file into it, then print `archived`;\n- otherwise, print `archive already there`.\n\nRun the same line **twice**. The first run archives the two `.old` files; the second finds the folder already made.\n--- starter\ntouch plan.old list.old plan.txt\n--- solution\nif mkdir archive; then mv *.old archive/; echo "archived"; else echo "archive already there"; fi\nif mkdir archive; then mv *.old archive/; echo "archived"; else echo "archive already there"; fi\n--- hint\nAny command can be the condition. `mkdir archive` succeeds the first time and fails the second, because the folder is there.\n--- hint\nA branch can hold two commands, joined by `;`: `then mv *.old archive/; echo "archived"; else …`.\n--- check shell | The first run archived the old files\ncount archive/*.old == 2\ncount *.old == 0\nprinted-line archived\n--- check shell | The second run found the folder\nprinted-line archive already there\n--- check shell | Decided with if mkdir\nused if mkdir archive\nused else\nfile plan.txt\n\n+++ practice | Sort the logs into good and bad\n--- task\nThere are four `.log` files. Make two folders, `good` and `bad`. Then, with **one** `for` loop over `*.log` that has an `if` inside it, move every log that contains `ERROR` into `bad/`, and every other log into `good/`.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR disk" > a.log\necho "INFO ok" > b.log\necho "INFO boot" > c.log\necho "ERROR net" >> c.log\necho "INFO done" > d.log\n--- solution\nmkdir good bad\nfor f in *.log; do if grep -q ERROR "$f"; then mv "$f" bad/; else mv "$f" good/; fi; done\n--- hint\nThe whole `if … fi` is the body of the loop, and it needs its own `;` after `fi`, before `done`.\n--- hint\n`for f in *.log; do if grep -q ERROR "$f"; then mv "$f" bad/; else mv "$f" good/; fi; done`.\n--- check shell | The logs with errors are in bad\ncount bad/*.log == 2\nfile bad/c.log\n--- check shell | The others are in good\ncount good/*.log == 2\nfile good/d.log\n--- check shell | Nothing is left loose\ncount *.log == 0\nused if grep -q\n\n+++ practice | The log that is not there\n--- task\nThere are two logs, `app.log` and `radio.log`, and there should be a third, `gps.log`, but it is missing.\n\nWrite **one** `for` loop over the three names `app.log radio.log gps.log`, with an `if` inside it, that prints one line per name:\n\n- `NAME: errors` if the file contains `ERROR`;\n- otherwise `NAME: clean` if the file is there;\n- otherwise `NAME: missing`.\n\nCareful: `grep` answers `2` for a missing file, and `2` is not `0`, so a plain `if`/`else` would call the missing log clean. Use `elif` with `ls "$f"` as the second question, and send both of `ls`\'s outputs to `/dev/null` so it prints nothing.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR disk" > app.log\necho "INFO link up" > radio.log\n--- solution\nfor f in app.log radio.log gps.log; do if grep -q ERROR "$f"; then echo "$f: errors"; elif ls "$f" &> /dev/null; then echo "$f: clean"; else echo "$f: missing"; fi; done\n--- hint\nOnly a missing file makes `ls` fail. So `ls "$f" &> /dev/null` is a quiet way to ask "is it there?".\n--- hint\nThe body is `if grep …; then …; elif ls "$f" &> /dev/null; then …; else …; fi`.\n--- check shell | app.log has errors\nprinted-line app.log: errors\n--- check shell | radio.log is clean\nprinted-line radio.log: clean\n--- check shell | gps.log is missing, not clean\nprinted-line gps.log: missing\nused elif\n\n+++ practice | The if that never ran\n--- task\nA teammate typed this line to check the test results:\n\n```\nif grep -q FAIL tests.log then echo "tests failed"; else echo "tests passed"; fi\n```\n\nNothing ran at all; the shell printed a `syntax error` instead. Fix the line and run it for `tests.log`, which contains `FAIL`, so that it prints `tests failed`. Then run the same line for `tests2.log`, which does not, so that it prints `tests passed`.\n--- starter\necho "boot: ok" > tests.log\necho "radio: FAIL" >> tests.log\necho "boot: ok" > tests2.log\n--- solution\nif grep -q FAIL tests.log; then echo "tests failed"; else echo "tests passed"; fi\nif grep -q FAIL tests2.log; then echo "tests failed"; else echo "tests passed"; fi\n--- hint\nCount the semicolons. Every piece before `then`, `else` and `fi` must be ended with one.\n--- hint\nWithout the `;`, the word `then` is handed to `grep` as one more argument. Put a `;` straight after `tests.log`.\n--- check shell | tests.log failed\nprinted-line tests failed\n--- check shell | tests2.log passed\nprinted-line tests passed\nused grep -q FAIL tests2.log\n--- check shell | The condition is ended before then\nused tests.log; then\n\n+++ practice | The go/no-go poll\n--- task\nThe folder `poll` holds one file per station: `fuel.txt`, `range.txt` and `weather.txt`. Each holds one word, `GO` or `NO-GO`.\n\nGo into `poll` with `cd poll`. Then:\n\n1. With **one** `for` loop over `*.txt` with an `if` inside it, print each station\'s name without `.txt` and its answer, in small letters:\n\n```\nfuel: go\nrange: go\nweather: no-go\n```\n\n2. With a one-line `if`, print `HOLD` if **any** station said `NO-GO`, and `LAUNCH` otherwise. One `grep -q` can search all the files at once.\n\nA plain `grep -q GO` would match `NO-GO` too, so match the whole line.\n--- starter\nmkdir poll\necho "GO" > poll/fuel.txt\necho "GO" > poll/range.txt\necho "NO-GO" > poll/weather.txt\n--- solution\ncd poll\nfor f in *.txt; do if grep -q "^GO$" "$f"; then echo "${f%.txt}: go"; else echo "${f%.txt}: no-go"; fi; done\nif grep -q "NO-GO" *.txt; then echo "HOLD"; else echo "LAUNCH"; fi\n--- hint\n`${f%.txt}` gives the station\'s name without its ending, as in the renaming lesson.\n--- hint\n`"^GO$"` matches a line that is exactly `GO`. For the second part, `grep -q "NO-GO" *.txt` succeeds if any one of the files contains it.\n--- check shell | One line per station\nprinted-exactly fuel: go\\nrange: go\\nweather: no-go\n--- check shell | The poll holds\nprinted-line HOLD\n--- check shell | Worked inside poll, with if\ncwd poll\nused if grep -q\nused %.txt}\n\n=== term3-06 | Decisions: asking questions with [ … ]\n--- teach\nLast lesson, `if` ran a command and looked at its exit status. But often the question is not "did this command work?". It is "is there a file called `config.txt`?", or "is this number bigger than 10?". The shell has a command whose only job is to answer questions like those.\n\nThink of a yes-or-no quiz card. You hold it up, and the answer is a nod or a shake of the head. Nothing is said out loud.\n\n### The test command\n\n**`[ … ]`** is a command, also called **`test`**, that checks one thing. It prints nothing. It answers with its exit status: `0` for true, `1` for false. You can see the answer with `echo $?`:\n\n```\n~/project $ [ -f notes.txt ]\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n~/project $ [ -f nope.txt ]\n~/project $ echo $?\n1\n```\n\n`[ -f notes.txt ]` and `test -f notes.txt` do exactly the same thing. Almost everyone writes the brackets, because they read well after `if`.\n\n### Questions about files\n\n- `-f` asks "is there a **file** with this name?"\n- `-d` asks "is there a **directory**, a folder, with this name?"\n- `-e` asks "does it **exist** at all, file or folder?"\n\n```\n~/project $ if [ -d logs ]; then echo "logs ready"; else mkdir logs; echo "logs made"; fi\nlogs made\n~/project $ if [ -d logs ]; then echo "logs ready"; else mkdir logs; echo "logs made"; fi\nlogs ready\n```\n\nThe same line gives a different answer the second time, because the first run made the folder. The `else` branch holds two commands, joined by `;`.\n\n### Questions about words\n\n`=` asks "are these two strings equal?" A **string** is a piece of text. `!=` asks "are they different?"\n\n```\n~/project $ mode=prod\n~/project $ if [ "$mode" = "prod" ]; then echo "careful: production"; else echo "test mode"; fi\ncareful: production\n```\n\n`-z` asks "is this string empty?", z for "zero length":\n\n```\n~/project $ name=""\n~/project $ if [ -z "$name" ]; then echo "no name given"; fi\nno name given\n```\n\n### Questions about numbers\n\nNumbers have their own two-letter operators, each with a dash in front:\n\n- `-eq` equal, `-ne` not equal\n- `-lt` less than, `-le` less than or equal\n- `-gt` greater than, `-ge` greater than or equal\n\n```\n~/project $ count=12\n~/project $ if [ "$count" -gt 10 ]; then echo "more than ten"; fi\nmore than ten\n```\n\nYou cannot use `>` here. The shell has already given `>` [[another job|why-not-greater-than]]. And `=` compares [[text, not amounts|strings-versus-numbers]].\n\n### Turning a question around: !\n\nA `!` in front flips the answer, so true becomes false and false becomes true. `[ ! -f config.txt ]` asks "is there **no** file called `config.txt`?":\n\n```\n~/project $ if [ ! -f config.txt ]; then echo "no config yet"; fi\nno config yet\n```\n\n### The spaces matter\n\n`[` is the name of a [[command|bracket-is-a-command]], so it needs a space after it, like any command name. `[-f x]` asks the shell to run a command called `[-f`, and you get `command not found`. The closing `]` must be its own word too: `[ -f x]` fails with an error saying the `]` is missing.\n\n**Watch out:** put double quotes around variables inside the brackets. If `$mode` is empty, `[ $mode = prod ]` turns into `[ = prod ]`. That is not a sensible question, and bash answers with [[an error about a unary operator|unary-operator]]: `unary operator expected`. With quotes, `[ "$mode" = prod ]` stays a proper comparison, between an empty string and `prod`, and it is false. You will use checks like these to make scripts [[refuse bad input|guard-clauses]].\n\n::: context why-not-greater-than Why -gt and not >\nThe shell reads `>` as "send the output to a file" before `[` ever sees it. So `[ 12 > 10 ]` does not compare anything. It runs `[ 12 ]`, which is true because `12` is not an empty string, and it quietly creates an empty file called `10`. That is why numbers get their own letter operators: `-gt` can never be mistaken for a redirection.\n:::\n\n::: context strings-versus-numbers Text order and number order\n`=` and `!=` compare text, letter by letter. `-eq`, `-lt` and the rest compare amounts. The two can disagree. As numbers, 10 is bigger than 9, so `[ 10 -lt 9 ]` is false. As text, "10" comes before "9", because the first characters are compared first and "1" comes before "9". It is the same reason `sort` put 10 before 9 until you added `-n` in the intermediate course. When you mean numbers, use the number operators.\n:::\n\n::: context bracket-is-a-command [ really is a command\nOn most Linux systems there is a program file called `[`, next to `ls` and `cp`, and bash also has its own copy built in. `type [` answers `[ is a shell builtin`. That is why the rules feel strict: the shell does not see brackets as punctuation. It sees a command named `[`, followed by arguments, and `[` insists that its last argument is `]`. Each part, `-f`, the name and `]`, has to be a separate word, with spaces between them.\n:::\n\n::: context unary-operator What "unary operator expected" means\nA **unary** operator works on one thing, like `-f notes.txt`. A **binary** operator sits between two things, like `a = b`. When `$mode` is empty and unquoted, it disappears completely, so `[` is left with `= prod`. It sees a lonely `=` at the front, where only a one-thing test could go, and complains.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 120" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <text x="10" y="22" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">[ $mode = prod ]   (mode is empty)</text>\n  <rect x="10" y="30" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="25" y="48" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">[</text>\n  <rect x="48" y="30" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="63" y="48" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">=</text>\n  <rect x="86" y="30" width="50" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="111" y="48" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">prod</text>\n  <rect x="144" y="30" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="159" y="48" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">]</text>\n  <text x="186" y="48" font-size="11" fill="#b4232c">= comes first: broken</text>\n  <text x="10" y="80" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">[ "$mode" = prod ]</text>\n  <rect x="10" y="88" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="25" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">[</text>\n  <rect x="48" y="88" width="40" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>\n  <text x="68" y="106" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">empty</text>\n  <rect x="96" y="88" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="111" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">=</text>\n  <rect x="134" y="88" width="50" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="159" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">prod</text>\n  <rect x="192" y="88" width="30" height="26" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="207" y="106" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">]</text>\n  <text x="234" y="106" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">x = y: fine</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context guard-clauses Checking before doing\nCareful scripts ask their questions first. Is the config file there? Was a file name given? Is the number in a safe range? Only then do they start the real work. Engineers call these checks **guard clauses**. Flight software build and test scripts are full of them, because failing early with a clear message beats failing halfway with a confusing one. In the last lesson of this course you will write a tool that guards its own input this way.\n:::\n--- task\nWrite a one-line `if` that uses `[ -f config.txt ]`. It prints `config found` when `config.txt` exists. Otherwise it creates `config.txt` containing `mode=dev` and then prints `config created`. Run the same line **twice**: the first run creates the file, the second finds it.\n--- starter\n--- solution\nif [ -f config.txt ]; then echo "config found"; else echo "mode=dev" > config.txt; echo "config created"; fi\nif [ -f config.txt ]; then echo "config found"; else echo "mode=dev" > config.txt; echo "config created"; fi\n--- hint\nThe shape is `if [ -f config.txt ]; then …; else …; fi`. Mind the spaces inside the brackets.\n--- hint\nThe `else` branch has two commands: `echo "mode=dev" > config.txt; echo "config created"`.\n--- hint\nPress the Up arrow to bring the line back, then Enter to run it again.\n--- check shell | The first run created the file\nprinted-line config created\nfile config.txt == mode=dev\n--- check shell | The second run found it\nprinted-line config found\nused if [ -f\n\n+++ practice | Too hot?\n--- task\nSet a variable called `temp` to `31`. Write a one-line `if` with `[ … ]` that prints `too hot` when `temp` is **greater than** 30, and `ok` otherwise.\n\nThen set `temp` to `30` and run the same line again. 30 is not greater than 30, so this time it prints `ok`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\ntemp=31\nif [ "$temp" -gt 30 ]; then echo "too hot"; else echo "ok"; fi\ntemp=30\nif [ "$temp" -gt 30 ]; then echo "too hot"; else echo "ok"; fi\n--- hint\nNumbers are compared with two-letter operators: "greater than" is `-gt`.\n--- hint\n`if [ "$temp" -gt 30 ]; then echo "too hot"; else echo "ok"; fi`. Mind the spaces inside the brackets.\n--- check shell | 31 is too hot\nprinted-line too hot\n--- check shell | 30 is ok\nprinted-line ok\nvar temp == 30\n--- check shell | Compared as numbers\nused -gt\nused if [\n\n+++ practice | Defaults only where they are missing\n--- task\nTwo settings files should exist: `settings.txt` and `limits.txt`. `settings.txt` is already there, with settings someone chose by hand; `limits.txt` is not.\n\nFor each of the two files, write a one-line `if` that uses `[ ! -f … ]`: if the file is **not** there, it creates it holding the single line `default`. A file that is already there must not be touched.\n--- starter\necho "volume=7" > settings.txt\n--- solution\nif [ ! -f settings.txt ]; then echo "default" > settings.txt; fi\nif [ ! -f limits.txt ]; then echo "default" > limits.txt; fi\n--- hint\n`!` turns the question around: `[ ! -f limits.txt ]` asks "is there **no** file called `limits.txt`?".\n--- hint\nNo `else` is needed: when the file is there, nothing should happen.\n--- check shell | settings.txt kept its own settings\nfile settings.txt == volume=7\n--- check shell | limits.txt was created with the default\nfile limits.txt == default\n--- check shell | Asked with [ ! -f … ]\nused ! -f\nused if [\n\n+++ practice | Long logs and short logs\n--- task\nWith **one** `for` loop over `*.log`, print one line per log: its name, then `: long` if it has **more than 3** lines, or `: short` if it does not:\n\n```\na.log: long\nb.log: short\nc.log: long\n```\n\nCount the lines with `$(…)` around a pipeline of `cat` and `wc -l`, and compare with `[ … ]`. A log with exactly 3 lines is short.\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do echo "line $i" >> a.log; done\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "line $i" >> b.log; done\nfor i in 1 2 3 4; do echo "line $i" >> c.log; done\n--- solution\nfor f in *.log; do if [ "$(cat "$f" | wc -l)" -gt 3 ]; then echo "$f: long"; else echo "$f: short"; fi; done\n--- hint\n`$(cat "$f" | wc -l)` is the number of lines in one log. It can go straight inside the brackets.\n--- hint\n"More than 3" is `-gt 3`. With `-ge 3`, `b.log` would come out long.\n--- check shell | Each log is sorted by its length\nprinted-exactly a.log: long\\nb.log: short\\nc.log: long\n--- check shell | Compared as numbers, inside a loop\nused -gt 3\nused for\n--- check shell | Counted with wc -l\nused wc -l\n\n+++ practice | An empty name, and a name with a space\n--- task\nThe variable `callsign` is empty. Write a one-line `if` that prints `name missing` when `$callsign` is empty, and `callsign: ` followed by its value otherwise.\n\nRun it now: it must print `name missing`. Then set `callsign` to `Eagle One` (two words, one value) and run the same line again: it must print `callsign: Eagle One`.\n\nWithout double quotes around the variable, one of the two runs goes wrong.\n--- starter\ncallsign=""\n--- solution\nif [ -z "$callsign" ]; then echo "name missing"; else echo "callsign: $callsign"; fi\ncallsign="Eagle One"\nif [ -z "$callsign" ]; then echo "name missing"; else echo "callsign: $callsign"; fi\n--- hint\n`-z` asks "is this string empty?".\n--- hint\nQuote it: `[ -z "$callsign" ]`. Unquoted, an empty variable disappears altogether, and `Eagle One` becomes two separate words.\n--- check shell | The empty name was caught\nprinted-line name missing\n--- check shell | The full callsign was printed\nprinted-line callsign: Eagle One\nvar callsign == Eagle One\n--- check shell | Quoted inside the brackets, so no run printed an error\nused "$callsign"\nnot-printed operator expected\n\n+++ practice | The comparison that made a file\n--- task\n`count` holds `7`. A teammate ran this line, which should print `too many` only when `count` is greater than 10:\n\n```\nif [ $count > 10 ]; then echo "too many"; else echo "fine"; fi\n```\n\nIt printed `too many`, and a new empty file called `10` appeared. Fix the line and run it: with `count` at 7 it must print `fine`. Then delete the stray file `10`.\n--- starter\ncount=7\nif [ $count > 10 ]; then echo "too many"; else echo "fine"; fi\n--- solution\nif [ "$count" -gt 10 ]; then echo "too many"; else echo "fine"; fi\nrm 10\n--- hint\nTo the shell, `>` means "send the output into a file". It did that before `[` ever saw the line.\n--- hint\nNumbers are compared with `-gt`. Then `rm 10` removes the file the `>` made.\n--- check shell | 7 is fine\nprinted-line fine\n--- check shell | Compared as numbers\nused -gt 10\n--- check shell | The stray file is gone\nmissing 10\n\n+++ practice | Pre-flight checks\n--- task\nWrite **one** `if` line with `elif` that checks three things, in this order, and prints the first problem it finds:\n\n1. if there is no folder called `config`, print `no config`;\n2. otherwise, if `config/mode.txt` does not hold `flight`, print `wrong mode: ` followed by what it does hold;\n3. otherwise, if the folder `firmware` holds **fewer than 2** `.bin` files, print `firmware incomplete`;\n4. otherwise, print `ready`.\n\nRun it, fix what it reports, and run it again, until it prints `ready`:\n\n- make the folder `config`, with a `mode.txt` holding `test`, and run the line (it prints `wrong mode: test`);\n- write `flight` into `config/mode.txt`, and run the line (it prints `firmware incomplete`);\n- make an empty file `firmware/main.bin`, and run the line (it prints `ready`).\n--- starter\nmkdir firmware\ntouch firmware/boot.bin\n--- solution\nif [ ! -d config ]; then echo "no config"; elif [ "$(cat config/mode.txt)" != "flight" ]; then echo "wrong mode: $(cat config/mode.txt)"; elif [ "$(ls firmware/*.bin | wc -l)" -lt 2 ]; then echo "firmware incomplete"; else echo "ready"; fi\nmkdir config\necho "test" > config/mode.txt\nif [ ! -d config ]; then echo "no config"; elif [ "$(cat config/mode.txt)" != "flight" ]; then echo "wrong mode: $(cat config/mode.txt)"; elif [ "$(ls firmware/*.bin | wc -l)" -lt 2 ]; then echo "firmware incomplete"; else echo "ready"; fi\necho "flight" > config/mode.txt\nif [ ! -d config ]; then echo "no config"; elif [ "$(cat config/mode.txt)" != "flight" ]; then echo "wrong mode: $(cat config/mode.txt)"; elif [ "$(ls firmware/*.bin | wc -l)" -lt 2 ]; then echo "firmware incomplete"; else echo "ready"; fi\ntouch firmware/main.bin\nif [ ! -d config ]; then echo "no config"; elif [ "$(cat config/mode.txt)" != "flight" ]; then echo "wrong mode: $(cat config/mode.txt)"; elif [ "$(ls firmware/*.bin | wc -l)" -lt 2 ]; then echo "firmware incomplete"; else echo "ready"; fi\n--- hint\nEach question is its own `[ … ]`: `[ ! -d config ]`, then a string comparison with `!=`, then a number comparison with `-lt`.\n--- hint\n`"$(cat config/mode.txt)"` is what the file holds, and `"$(ls firmware/*.bin | wc -l)"` is how many `.bin` files there are. Both can go inside the brackets.\n--- hint\nWrite the line once, then use the Up arrow to run it again after each fix.\n--- check shell | The first run found no config\nprinted-line no config\n--- check shell | Then the wrong mode, then the missing firmware\nprinted-line wrong mode: test\nprinted-line firmware incomplete\n--- check shell | Finally ready\nprinted-line ready\nfile config/mode.txt == flight\ncount firmware/*.bin == 2\n--- check shell | One line, asking with [ … ] and elif\nused elif [\nused -lt 2\n\n=== term3-07a | Scripts: commands saved in a file\n--- teach\nYour loops and `if` lines are getting long. Typing one again every time you need it would be slow, and easy to get wrong. So save it in a file.\n\nThink of a recipe card. You work out the steps once and write them down. After that, anyone can follow the card, as many times as they like, and get the same result.\n\n### A script\n\nA **[[script|script-word]]** is a text file of commands, one per line. `bash hello.sh` reads the file and runs its lines in order, as if you had typed them. You ran a ready-made one, `greet.sh`, in the export lesson. Now you will write your own.\n\nThis practice terminal has no text editor, so build the file with `echo`, using `>` for the first line and `>>` to add each line after it:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'echo "Hello from a script"\' > hello.sh\n~/project $ cat hello.sh\necho "Hello from a script"\n~/project $ bash hello.sh\nHello from a script\n```\n\nThe outer single quotes hold the whole line you want saved. Everything inside them, double quotes included, goes into the file exactly as typed.\n\n### Arguments: $1, $2, …\n\nThe words you type after the script\'s name are its **[[arguments|arguments]]**. Inside the script, `$1` means "the first argument", `$2` "the second", and so on:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'echo "Hello, $1"\' > hi.sh\n~/project $ bash hi.sh Ada\nHello, Ada\n~/project $ bash hi.sh Lin\nHello, Lin\n```\n\nOne script, different results, depending on what you give it.\n\n### How many: $#\n\n`$#` means "how many arguments there are":\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'echo "You gave $# names"\' > count.sh\n~/project $ bash count.sh Ada Lin Sam\nYou gave 3 names\n```\n\n### All of them: "$@"\n\n`"$@"` means "all the arguments, each one kept whole". It fits perfectly as the list of a `for` loop:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'for name in "$@"; do echo "Hello, $name"; done\' > all.sh\n~/project $ bash all.sh Ada "Sally Ride"\nHello, Ada\nHello, Sally Ride\n```\n\n`"Sally Ride"` is one argument, because you typed it in quotes, and `"$@"` keeps it as one. The [[quotes around it matter|quoted-at]].\n\n### A script runs in its own shell\n\nA script runs in a **child** shell, a copy of your shell, like the child processes from the export lesson. So a `cd` inside a script moves only that copy. When the script ends, you are still where you were:\n\n```\n~/project $ mkdir deep\n~/project $ echo \'cd deep\' > move.sh\n~/project $ echo \'pwd\' >> move.sh\n~/project $ bash move.sh\n/home/you/project/deep\n~/project $ pwd\n/home/you/project\n```\n\n**Watch out:** build each line with **single** quotes. Double quotes let the shell fill in `$1` **now**, while you are writing the file, and right now `$1` is empty. So it vanishes:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "echo Hello, $1" > oops.sh\n~/project $ cat oops.sh\necho Hello,\n```\n\nWith single quotes, `$1` goes into the file untouched, and is filled in [[later, when the script runs|two-moments]]. `cat` shows you what really went into the file, so check with it.\n\n::: context script-word Why "script"\nIn a theater, the script tells the actors what to say and do, in order. A shell script does the same for the computer. People also call scripts "shell scripts" or "bash scripts", and give them names ending in `.sh`. The ending is only a label for people; `bash` will run the file whatever it is called. Most engineering projects keep a handful of them, with names like `build.sh` or `run_tests.sh`, so everyone on the team runs the same steps in the same way.\n:::\n\n::: context arguments The words after the name\nAn **argument** is a piece of information you hand to a command. In `cp notes.txt backup/`, `cp` gets two arguments. The shell cuts the line at spaces, and quotes keep a phrase together. A script sees its arguments as numbered variables, and `$0` holds the script\'s own name.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 110" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="10" y="20" width="56" height="30" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#6c7a93"/>\n  <text x="38" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">bash</text>\n  <rect x="74" y="20" width="82" height="30" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="115" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">greet.sh</text>\n  <rect x="164" y="20" width="56" height="30" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="192" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">Ada</text>\n  <rect x="228" y="20" width="120" height="30" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="288" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">Sally Ride</text>\n  <text x="115" y="70" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$0</text>\n  <text x="192" y="70" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">$1</text>\n  <text x="288" y="70" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">$2</text>\n  <text x="180" y="98" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">$# is 2: the script\'s name is not counted</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context quoted-at Why "$@" needs its quotes\nWithout quotes, `$@` gets cut at spaces like any other variable, so `"Sally Ride"` arrives in the loop as two names, `Sally` and `Ride`. With quotes, `"$@"` is special: it becomes one quoted word per argument, exactly as they were given. There is a cousin, `"$*"`, which glues all the arguments into one single word. For loops, `"$@"` is nearly always the one you want.\n:::\n\n::: context two-moments Two moments: writing and running\nA script line meets the shell twice. First when you write it with `echo`: your shell reads the line and fills in any variables not protected by single quotes. Later when the script runs: the new shell reads the saved line and fills in the variables again, this time with the script\'s arguments. Single quotes skip the first filling, so the variables wait for the second, where they belong.\n:::\n--- task\nBuild a script called `greet.sh` with these three lines, adding them one at a time with `echo \'…\'` (`>` for the first, `>>` for the others):\n\n1. `echo "First up: $1"`\n2. `echo "You gave $# names"`\n3. `for name in "$@"; do echo "Hello, $name"; done`\n\nThen run it with three names: `bash greet.sh Ada Lin "Sally Ride"`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'echo "First up: $1"\' > greet.sh\necho \'echo "You gave $# names"\' >> greet.sh\necho \'for name in "$@"; do echo "Hello, $name"; done\' >> greet.sh\nbash greet.sh Ada Lin "Sally Ride"\n--- hint\nThe first line is `echo \'echo "First up: $1"\' > greet.sh`. The single quotes keep `$1` out of your shell\'s hands.\n--- hint\nAdd the other two lines with `>>`. Then `cat greet.sh` should show all three lines, with every `$` still there.\n--- hint\nRun it with `bash greet.sh Ada Lin "Sally Ride"`, keeping the quotes around `Sally Ride` so it stays one name.\n--- check shell | The script uses its arguments\nfile greet.sh contains $1\nfile greet.sh contains $#\nfile greet.sh contains "$@"\n--- check shell | Run with three names, it counts them and greets each one whole\nran bash greet.sh\nprinted-line First up: Ada\nprinted-line You gave 3 names\nprinted-line Hello, Lin\nprinted-line Hello, Sally Ride\n\n+++ practice | A countdown script\n--- task\nBuild a script called `countdown.sh` with this one line, using `echo \'…\' >`:\n\n```\necho "T-minus $1 for $2"\n```\n\nThen run it with two arguments, `bash countdown.sh 10 Artemis-2`, so it prints `T-minus 10 for Artemis-2`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'echo "T-minus $1 for $2"\' > countdown.sh\nbash countdown.sh 10 Artemis-2\n--- hint\nSingle quotes around the whole line keep `$1` and `$2` safe until the script runs.\n--- hint\n`cat countdown.sh` should show both dollar signs. Then `bash countdown.sh 10 Artemis-2`.\n--- check shell | The script uses its first two arguments\nfile countdown.sh contains $1\nfile countdown.sh contains $2\n--- check shell | It printed the countdown\nprinted-line T-minus 10 for Artemis-2\n--- check shell | You ran it with bash\nran bash countdown.sh 10 Artemis-2\n\n+++ practice | One folder per argument\n--- task\nBuild a script called `mkall.sh` with these two lines, adding them one at a time with `echo \'…\'` (`>` for the first, `>>` for the second):\n\n1. `for d in "$@"; do mkdir "$d"; done`\n2. `echo "made $# folders"`\n\nThen run it as `bash mkall.sh logs "flight data" maps`. It must make three folders, one of them with a space in its name, and print `made 3 folders`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'for d in "$@"; do mkdir "$d"; done\' > mkall.sh\necho \'echo "made $# folders"\' >> mkall.sh\nbash mkall.sh logs "flight data" maps\n--- hint\n`"$@"` is every argument, each kept whole, so `"flight data"` stays one name.\n--- hint\nRun it with the quotes around `"flight data"`, or it arrives as two arguments.\n--- check shell | Three folders, one with a space\ndir logs\ndir maps\ncount flight?data == 1\nmissing flight\n--- check shell | It counted them\nprinted-line made 3 folders\n--- check shell | The script uses all its arguments\nfile mkall.sh contains "$@"\nfile mkall.sh contains $#\n\n+++ practice | An error counter\n--- task\nBuild a script called `errcount.sh` with this one line:\n\n```\necho "$1: $(grep -c ERROR "$1") errors"\n```\n\nThen run it on `a.log`, and again on `b.log`. It should print `a.log: 2 errors`, then `b.log: 0 errors`.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR disk" > a.log\necho "INFO ok" >> a.log\necho "ERROR net" >> a.log\necho "INFO ok" > b.log\n--- solution\necho \'echo "$1: $(grep -c ERROR "$1") errors"\' > errcount.sh\nbash errcount.sh a.log\nbash errcount.sh b.log\n--- hint\nThe outer single quotes keep everything inside, the `$(…)` included, for the script to run later.\n--- hint\n`echo \'echo "$1: $(grep -c ERROR "$1") errors"\' > errcount.sh`, then run it once per log.\n--- check shell | a.log has two errors\nprinted-line a.log: 2 errors\n--- check shell | b.log has none\nprinted-line b.log: 0 errors\n--- check shell | The count happens when the script runs\nfile errcount.sh contains $(grep -c ERROR "$1")\n\n+++ practice | A name with a space in it\n--- task\nBuild a script called `tag.sh` with one line that makes an empty file named after its first argument, with `.tag` on the end. Then run it as `bash tag.sh "launch day"`: it must make one file, `launch day.tag`. Run it once more as `bash tag.sh engine-2`, which makes `engine-2.tag`.\n\nTwo things can go wrong: the `$1` can vanish while you write the file, and the name can be cut in two when the script runs. Check what went in with `cat tag.sh`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'touch "$1.tag"\' > tag.sh\nbash tag.sh "launch day"\nbash tag.sh engine-2\n--- hint\nBuild the line with single quotes, so `$1` goes into the file untouched.\n--- hint\nInside the script, put `"$1.tag"` in double quotes, so `launch day` stays one name.\n--- check shell | One file, named with the space, and one for engine-2\ncount launch?day.tag == 1\nfile engine-2.tag\n--- check shell | Not two files from half the name\nmissing launch\nmissing day.tag\n--- check shell | The script holds $1, quoted\nfile tag.sh contains "$1.tag"\n\n+++ practice | The script that forgot its argument\n--- task\nA teammate built `mk.sh` like this:\n\n```\necho "mkdir -p $1" > mk.sh\necho "echo made $1" >> mk.sh\n```\n\nNow `bash mk.sh reports` prints an error and `made`, and makes no folder. Look at the script with `cat mk.sh`, build it again so it works (same two commands inside), and run `bash mk.sh flight-logs`. It must make the folder `flight-logs` and print `made flight-logs`.\n--- starter\necho "mkdir -p $1" > mk.sh\necho "echo made $1" >> mk.sh\n--- solution\necho \'mkdir -p "$1"\' > mk.sh\necho \'echo "made $1"\' >> mk.sh\nbash mk.sh flight-logs\n--- hint\n`cat mk.sh` shows `mkdir -p` with nothing after it. Double quotes filled `$1` in while the file was being written, and it was empty then.\n--- hint\nBuild each line with single quotes: `echo \'mkdir -p "$1"\' > mk.sh`, then `echo \'echo "made $1"\' >> mk.sh`.\n--- check shell | The folder was made\ndir flight-logs\n--- check shell | The script said so\nprinted-line made flight-logs\n--- check shell | The script keeps its $1\nfile mk.sh contains $1\n\n+++ practice | Line counts for any number of logs\n--- task\nBuild a script called `report.sh` with two lines. Given any number of log files as arguments, it must:\n\n1. print one line per file, the file\'s name, a colon, and its number of lines, like `boot.log: 3 lines`, counting with `$(…)` around `cat` and `wc -l`;\n2. then print `checked ` followed by how many files it was given, like `checked 3 files`.\n\nRun it as `bash report.sh boot.log "night shift.log" radio.log`. It must print:\n\n```\nboot.log: 3 lines\nnight shift.log: 2 lines\nradio.log: 4 lines\nchecked 3 files\n```\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "boot $i" >> boot.log; done\nfor i in 1 2; do echo "night $i" >> "night shift.log"; done\nfor i in 1 2 3 4; do echo "radio $i" >> radio.log; done\n--- solution\necho \'for f in "$@"; do echo "$f: $(cat "$f" | wc -l) lines"; done\' > report.sh\necho \'echo "checked $# files"\' >> report.sh\nbash report.sh boot.log "night shift.log" radio.log\n--- hint\nLine 1 is a loop over `"$@"`, with an `echo` inside it. Line 2 uses `$#`.\n--- hint\nInside the loop, `"$f"` must be quoted in the `cat`, or `night shift.log` is cut in two.\n--- hint\nLine 1: `for f in "$@"; do echo "$f: $(cat "$f" | wc -l) lines"; done`. Line 2: `echo "checked $# files"`.\n--- check shell | One line per log, then the total\nprinted-exactly boot.log: 3 lines\\nnight shift.log: 2 lines\\nradio.log: 4 lines\\nchecked 3 files\n--- check shell | The script loops over its arguments\nfile report.sh contains "$@"\nfile report.sh contains $#\n--- check shell | You ran it on the three logs\nran bash report.sh\n\n=== term3-07 | Scripts you run by name: #!, chmod +x and ./\n--- teach\nLast lesson you wrote scripts and ran them with `bash greet.sh`. Real programs like `ls` or `git` you run by their name alone. This lesson makes your script work like that, in three steps.\n\nThink of a key card for a door. The card has to say which door it opens, the door has to be set to accept it, and you have to be standing at the right door. Scripts need the same three things.\n\nRecall the tools from last lesson: inside a script, `$1` is the first argument, `$2` the second, `$#` how many there are, and `"$@"` all of them. `"$1.bak"` is the first argument with `.bak` stuck on the end. And a script runs in a child shell, so a `cd` inside it does not move you.\n\n### Step 1: the shebang line\n\nThe first line of the script should be `#!/bin/bash`. It says which program should run the file, here `bash`, which lives in the folder `/bin`. This line is called the **[[shebang|shebang]]**, from the names of its first two characters.\n\n```\n~/project $ echo \'#!/bin/bash\' > hi.sh\n~/project $ echo \'echo "Hello, $1"\' >> hi.sh\n~/project $ cat hi.sh\n#!/bin/bash\necho "Hello, $1"\n```\n\nNote the `>` for the shebang, so it lands on the very first line, and `>>` for the lines after it.\n\n### Step 2: make it executable\n\nFiles are not allowed to run as programs until you say so. Each file carries **[[permissions|permissions]]**: who may read it, change it, or run it. A file that may be run is **executable**. `chmod +x hi.sh` makes it so. `chmod` is "change mode", and `+x` is "add execute permission".\n\n```\n~/project $ ./hi.sh Ada\nbash: ./hi.sh: Permission denied\n~/project $ chmod +x hi.sh\n~/project $ ./hi.sh Ada\nHello, Ada\n```\n\n### Step 3: run it as ./\n\n`./hi.sh` means "the `hi.sh` in this folder". A single dot, `.`, means "this folder", the way `..` means "the folder above". You need it because the shell does not look in the current folder for commands. It only searches the folders listed in `PATH`, the variable you met in the export lesson. So plain `hi.sh` [[is not found|dot-slash]]:\n\n```\n~/project $ hi.sh Ada\nbash: hi.sh: command not found\n```\n\n`bash hi.sh` still works too. It runs the file with bash directly, so it needs neither the shebang nor `chmod`.\n\n**Watch out:** the shebang must be the **first line**, starting with the very first characters, `#!`. If you add it with `>>` after other lines, it is no longer at the top, and the shell reads it as an ordinary [[comment|comment]] and ignores it.\n\nMaking a script executable is a real part of how teams [[share tools|executable-in-git]].\n\n::: context shebang Hash, bang: shebang\nProgrammers call `#` "hash" and `!` "bang", so `#!` is "hash-bang", squeezed to "shebang". When you run `./hi.sh`, the operating system looks at the file\'s first two characters. If they are `#!`, it reads the rest of that line as the program to run, and hands it the file. You will also see `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, which asks the system to find `bash` wherever it is installed. Both are common.\n:::\n\n::: context permissions Read, write, execute\n`ls -l` shows each file\'s permissions as ten characters. The first says what it is: `-` for a file, `d` for a folder. Then come three groups of three: for the owner, for the owner\'s group, and for everyone else. Each group says `r` read, `w` write, `x` execute, or `-` for "not allowed".\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 130" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="30" y="20" width="30" height="36" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#6c7a93" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="45" y="44" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">-</text>\n  <rect x="70" y="20" width="84" height="36" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="112" y="44" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">rwx</text>\n  <rect x="164" y="20" width="84" height="36" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="206" y="44" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">r-x</text>\n  <rect x="258" y="20" width="84" height="36" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="300" y="44" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">r-x</text>\n  <text x="45" y="76" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">file</text>\n  <text x="112" y="76" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">owner</text>\n  <text x="206" y="76" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">group</text>\n  <text x="300" y="76" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">everyone else</text>\n  <text x="180" y="108" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">r read   w write   x execute   - not allowed</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n`-rwxr-xr-x` means: a file, which the owner may read, change and run, and everyone else may read and run.\n:::\n\n::: context dot-slash Why the shell ignores the current folder\nIt is a safety rule. If the shell ran programs from whatever folder you happened to be in, someone could leave a fake program called `ls` in a shared folder, and you would run it the moment you typed `ls` there. So the shell only runs programs from the trusted folders in `PATH`, unless you point at one yourself with a path such as `./hi.sh`. `echo $PATH` shows the list, folder names separated by colons.\n:::\n\n::: context comment Lines the shell skips\nIn bash, a `#` at the start of a word begins a **comment**: the shell ignores the rest of that line. Comments are notes for people, such as `# copy the file before changing it`. The shebang is a clever trick: to bash it looks like a comment, so bash skips it, while the operating system reads it as the instruction "run this with bash". That only works on the very first line.\n:::\n\n::: context executable-in-git Engineers share runnable scripts\nGit, the tool you will meet in the Git course, remembers whether a file is executable, not only what is in it. So when you run `chmod +x build.sh` and commit it, everyone who downloads the project gets a script they can run straight away with `./build.sh`. Flight software teams keep build, test and check scripts in the project this way, and CI systems run the very same scripts on every change.\n:::\n--- task\nCreate `backup.sh`, a script that starts with the shebang line `#!/bin/bash`, then copies the file named in its first argument to the same name with `.bak` added, then prints `backed up <name>`. Run it on `notes.txt` with `bash`. Then make it executable and run it as `./backup.sh todo.txt`.\n--- starter\necho "remember the fuel" > notes.txt\necho "launch" > todo.txt\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > backup.sh\necho \'cp "$1" "$1.bak"\' >> backup.sh\necho \'echo "backed up $1"\' >> backup.sh\nbash backup.sh notes.txt\nchmod +x backup.sh\n./backup.sh todo.txt\n--- hint\nThree lines, each with `echo \'…\'`: the shebang first (with `>`), then `cp "$1" "$1.bak"` and `echo "backed up $1"` (with `>>`).\n--- hint\n`bash backup.sh notes.txt` runs it. `cat backup.sh` shows what you wrote.\n--- hint\n`chmod +x backup.sh`, then `./backup.sh todo.txt`.\n--- check shell | backup.sh works when run with bash\nfile notes.txt.bak == remember the fuel\nprinted-line backed up notes.txt\n--- check shell | …and on its own, once it is executable\nfile backup.sh exec\nfile todo.txt.bak == launch\nran ./backup.sh todo.txt\n\n+++ practice | A stamp you run by name\n--- task\nCreate `stamp.sh`, a script of exactly two lines: the shebang line `#!/bin/bash`, then `echo "stamped by $(whoami)"`. Make it executable, and run it as `./stamp.sh`, so it prints `stamped by you`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > stamp.sh\necho \'echo "stamped by $(whoami)"\' >> stamp.sh\nchmod +x stamp.sh\n./stamp.sh\n--- hint\nThe shebang goes in with `>`, so it is the first line; the second line goes in with `>>`. Single quotes keep the `$(whoami)` for later.\n--- hint\n`chmod +x stamp.sh`, then `./stamp.sh`.\n--- check shell | The script is exactly the shebang and the echo\nfile stamp.sh ~= #!/bin/bash\\necho "stamped by $(whoami)"\n--- check shell | It is executable\nfile stamp.sh exec\n--- check shell | Run by name, it stamped\nran ./stamp.sh\nprinted-line stamped by you\n\n+++ practice | Permission denied\n--- task\n`deploy.sh` is a finished script with a shebang: it prints `deploying to ` followed by its first argument. Try to run it as `./deploy.sh staging-eu`: the shell refuses. Fix that, then run `./deploy.sh staging-eu` again, so it prints `deploying to staging-eu`.\n--- starter\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > deploy.sh\necho \'echo "deploying to $1"\' >> deploy.sh\n--- solution\n./deploy.sh staging-eu\nchmod +x deploy.sh\n./deploy.sh staging-eu\n--- hint\n`Permission denied` means the file is not allowed to run as a program yet.\n--- hint\n`chmod +x deploy.sh` adds the execute permission.\n--- check shell | deploy.sh is executable\nfile deploy.sh exec\n--- check shell | It ran by name\nprinted-line deploying to staging-eu\n--- check shell | Run with ./\nran ./deploy.sh staging-eu\n\n+++ practice | A TODO finder\n--- task\nCreate an executable script called `todo.sh` that starts with the shebang line and then searches the folder named in its first argument, and every folder inside it, for lines containing `TODO`, showing the line numbers. Use `grep` with its flags for a whole-folder search and for line numbers.\n\nRun it as `./todo.sh flight-sw`. It must print:\n\n```\nflight-sw/main.js:2:// TODO check fuel\nflight-sw/ui/menu.js:1:// TODO draw logo\n```\n--- starter\nmkdir -p flight-sw/ui\necho "start()" > flight-sw/main.js\necho "// TODO check fuel" >> flight-sw/main.js\necho "// TODO draw logo" > flight-sw/ui/menu.js\necho "done()" > flight-sw/ui/end.js\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > todo.sh\necho \'grep -rn TODO "$1"\' >> todo.sh\nchmod +x todo.sh\n./todo.sh flight-sw\n--- hint\n`grep -rn TODO flight-sw` does the job for one folder. In the script, `"$1"` takes the place of `flight-sw`.\n--- hint\nShebang with `>`, the `grep` line with `>>`, then `chmod +x todo.sh` and `./todo.sh flight-sw`.\n--- check shell | Both TODO lines, with where they are\nprinted-exactly flight-sw/main.js:2:// TODO check fuel\\nflight-sw/ui/menu.js:1:// TODO draw logo\n--- check shell | The script takes the folder as an argument\nfile todo.sh contains #!/bin/bash\nfile todo.sh contains $1\nfile todo.sh exec\n--- check shell | Run by name\nran ./todo.sh flight-sw\n\n+++ practice | The shebang that came last\n--- task\n`ping.sh` already holds one line, `echo "pong"`, but no shebang. Give it the shebang line `#!/bin/bash` as its **first** line, so that it holds exactly:\n\n```\n#!/bin/bash\necho "pong"\n```\n\nThen make it executable and run it as `./ping.sh`.\n\n`echo \'#!/bin/bash\' >> ping.sh` would put the shebang at the end, where it is only a comment. Build the file again from the top instead.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "pong"\' > ping.sh\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > ping.sh\necho \'echo "pong"\' >> ping.sh\nchmod +x ping.sh\n./ping.sh\n--- hint\n`>` empties a file and writes from the top. Start again with the shebang, using `>`.\n--- hint\nThen add the `echo "pong"` line back with `>>`, and check the order with `cat ping.sh`.\n--- check shell | The shebang is the first line\nfile ping.sh ~= #!/bin/bash\\necho "pong"\n--- check shell | It is executable\nfile ping.sh exec\n--- check shell | It runs by name\nran ./ping.sh\nprinted-line pong\n\n+++ practice | The backup that split the name\n--- task\n`backup.sh` is executable, but it breaks on names with spaces. It holds:\n\n```\n#!/bin/bash\ncp $1 $1.bak\n```\n\n`./backup.sh "flight plan.txt"` fails with errors about files called `flight` and `plan.txt`. Write the script again so that names with spaces work (same two lines, fixed), check that it is still executable, and run `./backup.sh "flight plan.txt"`. It must make `flight plan.txt.bak`. Then back up `crew-list.txt` the same way.\n--- starter\necho "burn at T+120" > "flight plan.txt"\necho "ada lin sam" > crew-list.txt\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > backup.sh\necho \'cp $1 $1.bak\' >> backup.sh\nchmod +x backup.sh\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > backup.sh\necho \'cp "$1" "$1.bak"\' >> backup.sh\n./backup.sh "flight plan.txt"\n./backup.sh crew-list.txt\n--- hint\nUnquoted, `$1` is cut at the space inside the script, so `cp` is given four words.\n--- hint\nPut double quotes around both: `cp "$1" "$1.bak"`. Writing the file again with `>` keeps its execute permission, so `ls -l backup.sh` still shows an `x`.\n--- check shell | Both backups exist, one with a space in its name\ncount flight?plan.txt.bak == 1\nfile crew-list.txt.bak == ada lin sam\n--- check shell | The script quotes its argument\nfile backup.sh contains "$1"\nfile backup.sh exec\n--- check shell | Run by name\nran ./backup.sh\n\n+++ practice | A log maker for the team\n--- task\nCreate an executable script called `newlog.sh`, starting with the shebang line, that:\n\n1. makes the folder `logs`, and does not complain if it is already there;\n2. makes an empty file inside it, named after its first argument with `.log` on the end;\n3. prints `created logs/` followed by that file\'s name, like `created logs/engine-2.log`.\n\nRun it as `./newlog.sh engine-2`, then as `./newlog.sh "fuel pump"`. The second run must make `logs/fuel pump.log`, one file, space and all, and print `created logs/fuel pump.log`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > newlog.sh\necho \'mkdir -p logs\' >> newlog.sh\necho \'touch "logs/$1.log"\' >> newlog.sh\necho \'echo "created logs/$1.log"\' >> newlog.sh\nchmod +x newlog.sh\n./newlog.sh engine-2\n./newlog.sh "fuel pump"\n--- hint\n`mkdir -p` makes a folder and says nothing if it already exists, so the second run does not fail.\n--- hint\nQuote the path inside the script: `touch "logs/$1.log"`.\n--- hint\nFour lines: the shebang, `mkdir -p logs`, `touch "logs/$1.log"`, and `echo "created logs/$1.log"`. Then `chmod +x newlog.sh`.\n--- check shell | Both logs were made, one with a space\nfile logs/engine-2.log\ncount logs/fuel?pump.log == 1\ncount logs/* == 2\n--- check shell | Each run said what it made\nprinted-line created logs/engine-2.log\nprinted-line created logs/fuel pump.log\n--- check shell | A proper executable script\nfile newlog.sh contains #!/bin/bash\nfile newlog.sh exec\nran ./newlog.sh\n\n=== term3-08 | Find and replace: sed\n--- teach\nLast lesson you built scripts out of lines of text. This lesson gives you a tool that changes the text inside files, with no editor at all.\n\nYou have probably used "find and replace" in a word processor: type what to find, type what to put instead, press Replace. `sed` does the same from the command line.\n\n### Substitute: s/old/new/\n\n**`sed`** is the **s**tream **ed**itor. It reads text line by line as it flows past, like water through a pipe, changes it, and prints the result. This flowing text is called a **[[stream|stream]]**. Its most used command is `s`, **substitute**:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat game-setup.conf\nplayer=guest\nsound=on\nlevel=3\n~/project $ sed \'s/guest/ada/\' game-setup.conf\nplayer=ada\nsound=on\nlevel=3\n```\n\nRead `s/guest/ada/` as "substitute: find `guest`, put `ada`". The slashes separate the three parts. The single quotes around it stop the shell from touching any of the characters inside.\n\n### It prints; the file stays the same\n\n`sed` prints the changed text. The file itself is not changed:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat game-setup.conf\nplayer=guest\nsound=on\nlevel=3\n```\n\nThat is useful: you can look at the result safely, as many times as you like.\n\n### Every match: g\n\n`s` changes only the **first** match on each line. Add `g`, for "global", after the last slash to change every match:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat welcome-note.txt\nhello guest, bye guest\n~/project $ sed \'s/guest/ada/\' welcome-note.txt\nhello ada, bye guest\n~/project $ sed \'s/guest/ada/g\' welcome-note.txt\nhello ada, bye ada\n```\n\n### Changing the file itself: -i\n\n`-i` means "**in place**": `sed` rewrites the file instead of printing. It prints nothing, so look with `cat` afterwards:\n\n```\n~/project $ sed -i \'s/hello/hi/\' welcome-note.txt\n~/project $ cat welcome-note.txt\nhi guest, bye guest\n```\n\n### Patterns, not only words\n\nWhat goes between the first two slashes is a **[[regular expression|regular-expression]]**, like the patterns you gave `grep`: `.` means any one character, `^` the start of a line, `$` the end of a line.\n\n```\n~/project $ sed \'s/^level/LEVEL/\' game-setup.conf\nplayer=guest\nsound=on\nLEVEL=3\n~/project $ sed \'s/on$/off/\' game-setup.conf\nplayer=guest\nsound=off\nlevel=3\n```\n\n### Text with slashes in it\n\nIf the text itself holds slashes, like a folder path, pick another separator. `sed` uses whatever character comes right after the `s`, so `s|/usr/local|/opt|` replaces `/usr/local` with `/opt`:\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "path=/usr/local/bin" > paths.conf\n~/project $ sed \'s|/usr/local|/opt|\' paths.conf\npath=/opt/bin\n```\n\n### Deleting lines: d\n\n`/pattern/d` deletes every line that matches the pattern. Here, the line that starts with `sound`:\n\n```\n~/project $ sed \'/^sound/d\' game-setup.conf\nplayer=guest\nlevel=3\n```\n\n### Printing only some lines: -n and p\n\n`-n` means "**no** automatic printing", and `p` means "print". Together, `sed -n \'/pattern/p\'` prints only the lines that match, much like `grep`:\n\n```\n~/project $ sed -n \'/^level/p\' game-setup.conf\nlevel=3\n```\n\n**Watch out:** there are two classic surprises. Forgetting `g` means only the first match on each line changes. Forgetting `-i` means the output looks right, but the file never changed. And because `-i` really does rewrite the file, [[check the command without -i first|check-before-i]], then add `-i` when the output is what you want.\n\n`sed` is one of the oldest tools you have met, and it still turns up [[all over real projects|sed-for-real]].\n\n::: context stream Text that flows\nA **stream** is data that arrives a piece at a time, like water from a tap, rather than all at once. You met two streams in the intermediate course: standard output and standard error. `sed` takes one line in, applies its commands, sends the line out, and reads the next. It never needs the whole file in memory, so it can edit a file far bigger than the computer\'s memory, or text arriving through a pipe.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 120" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="flow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">\n      <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x="10" y="40" font-size="11" fill="#1f2a44">host=localhost</text>\n  <text x="10" y="60" font-size="11" fill="#1f2a44">debug=true</text>\n  <text x="10" y="80" font-size="11" fill="#1f2a44">port=5432</text>\n  <line x1="102" y1="58" x2="130" y2="58" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#flow)"/>\n  <rect x="132" y="30" width="100" height="56" rx="8" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="182" y="54" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">sed</text>\n  <text x="182" y="72" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">one line at a time</text>\n  <line x1="232" y1="58" x2="258" y2="58" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#flow)"/>\n  <text x="262" y="40" font-size="11" fill="#1d6fd1">host=db.internal</text>\n  <text x="262" y="60" font-size="11" fill="#1f2a44">debug=true</text>\n  <text x="262" y="80" font-size="11" fill="#1f2a44">port=5432</text>\n  <text x="182" y="110" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6c7a93">s/localhost/db.internal/</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context regular-expression Patterns, again\nA **regular expression** is a small language for describing text: "a line that starts with port", "any character, then 3". `grep`, `sed` and most programming languages understand them. Because `.` means "any character", `s/5.3/X/` also matches `5a3` or `593`. To mean a real dot, put a backslash in front: `\\.`. You will see `\\.` in the downloads-tidying lesson later in this course.\n:::\n\n::: context check-before-i Test first, then write\nA `sed -i` command can change hundreds of lines in one go, and there is no undo. So engineers run it without `-i` first and read the output, or send it through `head` to see the first few lines. `sed -i.bak \'s/a/b/\' file` is another safety net: it writes the change and keeps the original as `file.bak`. On a Mac, plain `-i` needs an extra empty argument, `sed -i \'\' \'s/a/b/\' file`. On Linux, which this terminal copies, `-i` alone is fine.\n:::\n\n::: context sed-for-real Where sed shows up\n`sed` was written at Bell Labs in the early 1970s, and it is still on nearly every Linux and Mac computer. Build scripts use it to stamp a version number into a file, to switch a setting from a test value to a real one, or to clean up log files before counting their lines. When a job is "change this text in every file", the answer is often a `for` loop with a `sed -i` inside it.\n:::\n--- task\nIn `settings.conf`, change **every** `localhost` to `db.internal`, in the file itself. One line has it twice. Then, also with `sed -i`, delete the line that starts with `debug`.\n--- starter\necho "host=localhost" > settings.conf\necho "url=http://localhost:5432/localhost" >> settings.conf\necho "debug=true" >> settings.conf\necho "port=5432" >> settings.conf\n--- solution\nsed -i \'s/localhost/db.internal/g\' settings.conf\nsed -i \'/^debug/d\' settings.conf\n--- hint\nTry it without `-i` first: `sed \'s/localhost/db.internal/g\' settings.conf`. The `g` catches the second match on the url line.\n--- hint\nWhen the output looks right, add `-i` to change the file. Then use `/^debug/d`, also with `-i`, to delete the debug line.\n--- hint\n`sed -i \'s/localhost/db.internal/g\' settings.conf`, then `sed -i \'/^debug/d\' settings.conf`. `cat settings.conf` shows the result.\n--- check shell | Every localhost is now db.internal, and the debug line is gone\nfile settings.conf ~= host=db.internal\\nurl=http://db.internal:5432/db.internal\\nport=5432\n--- check shell | Edited in place with sed\nused sed -i\nused /g\n\n+++ practice | Preview the release notes\n--- task\n`release.txt` talks about a `beta` build, twice on one line. Print the whole file with **every** `beta` replaced by `stable`, using `sed`. Only print it: `release.txt` itself must not change.\n--- starter\necho "version 2.0 beta" > release.txt\necho "the beta build replaces the old beta build" >> release.txt\necho "tested on the bench" >> release.txt\n--- solution\nsed \'s/beta/stable/g\' release.txt\n--- hint\n`s/old/new/` changes the first match on each line. Add `g` after the last slash to change every match.\n--- hint\nLeave out `-i`, so `sed` prints the result and the file stays as it is.\n--- check shell | The preview shows every beta as stable\nprinted-exactly version 2.0 stable\\nthe stable build replaces the old stable build\\ntested on the bench\n--- check shell | The file itself did not change\nfile release.txt contains the beta build replaces the old beta build\n--- check shell | Substituted with sed, every match\nran sed\nused /g\n\n+++ practice | What the names would become\n--- task\nThe folder `notes` holds three `.txt` files. Print the list of their names as they **would** look with `.md` in place of `.txt`, one per line, by piping `ls notes` into `sed`. No file is renamed.\n\n`a.txt.bak` is not a `.txt` file: its name must come out unchanged, so change `txt` only where it is at the very end of a name.\n--- starter\nmkdir notes\ntouch notes/a.txt notes/b.txt notes/c.txt notes/a.txt.bak\n--- solution\nls notes | sed \'s/\\.txt$/.md/\'\n--- hint\nGiven no file name, `sed` works on the text arriving through the pipe.\n--- hint\n`$` pins the pattern to the end of the line, and `\\.` is a real dot.\n--- check shell | The names as they would be\nprinted-exactly a.md\\na.txt.bak\\nb.md\\nc.md\n--- check shell | Nothing was renamed\ncount notes/*.txt == 3\nfile notes/a.txt.bak\n--- check shell | Piped into sed, pinned to the end\nused | sed\nused $/\n\n+++ practice | Switch debug off everywhere\n--- task\nEvery `.conf` file here has the line `DEBUG=1`. With **one** `for` loop over `*.conf` and `sed -i`, change it to `DEBUG=0` in every one of them, in the files themselves. One file has a space in its name. `notes.txt` also says `DEBUG=1`, and must be left alone.\n\nThen print `old site.conf` with `cat` to check it.\n--- starter\necho "DEBUG=1" > app.conf\necho "port=80" >> app.conf\necho "DEBUG=1" > db.conf\necho "name=main" > "old site.conf"\necho "DEBUG=1" >> "old site.conf"\necho "remember: DEBUG=1 on the bench" > notes.txt\n--- solution\nfor f in *.conf; do sed -i \'s/DEBUG=1/DEBUG=0/\' "$f"; done\ncat "old site.conf"\n--- hint\nThe loop runs one `sed -i` per file, with `"$f"` as the file name.\n--- hint\nQuote `"$f"`, or `old site.conf` is cut into two names.\n--- check shell | app.conf and db.conf are switched off\nfile app.conf ~= DEBUG=0\\nport=80\nfile db.conf == DEBUG=0\n--- check shell | So is the file with a space, and notes.txt is untouched\nprinted-exactly name=main\\nDEBUG=0\nfile notes.txt == remember: DEBUG=1 on the bench\n--- check shell | Changed in place, in a loop\nused sed -i\nused for\n\n+++ practice | Paths with slashes in them\n--- task\nThe website moved from `/var/www/old` to `/srv/site`. In `site.conf`, change **every** `/var/www/old` to `/srv/site`, in the file itself. One line has it twice.\n\nThe paths are full of slashes, so pick another separator for the `s` command.\n--- starter\necho "root=/var/www/old" > site.conf\necho "logs=/var/www/old/logs:/var/www/old/archive" >> site.conf\necho "port=8080" >> site.conf\n--- solution\nsed -i \'s|/var/www/old|/srv/site|g\' site.conf\n--- hint\n`sed` uses whatever character comes straight after the `s` as the separator, so `s|…|…|` works.\n--- hint\nThe second line has two matches, so it needs `g`. Try it without `-i` first, then add `-i`.\n--- check shell | Every old path is the new one\nfile site.conf ~= root=/srv/site\\nlogs=/srv/site/logs:/srv/site/archive\\nport=8080\n--- check shell | Changed in place\nused sed -i\n--- check shell | Another separator, every match\nused s|\nused |g\n\n+++ practice | It looked right, but nothing changed\n--- task\nEvery link in `links.txt` should use `https:` instead of `http:`, in the file itself. A teammate ran:\n\n```\nsed \'s/http:/https:/\' links.txt\n```\n\nThe output looked almost right, but `cat links.txt` shows the file never changed, and in the output the second link on the mirror line was still `http:`. Fix the command so that `links.txt` itself ends up with `https:` everywhere.\n--- starter\necho "home http://orbit.example" > links.txt\necho "mirror http://a.example http://b.example" >> links.txt\necho "docs http://docs.example" >> links.txt\n--- solution\nsed -i \'s/http:/https:/g\' links.txt\n--- hint\nTwo things are missing: the flag that changes the file itself, and the letter that changes every match on a line.\n--- hint\n`-i` for in place, and `g` at the end: `sed -i \'s/http:/https:/g\' links.txt`.\n--- check shell | Every link is https\nfile links.txt ~= home https://orbit.example\\nmirror https://a.example https://b.example\\ndocs https://docs.example\n--- check shell | In place\nused sed -i\n--- check shell | Every match\nused /g\n\n+++ practice | Clean the telemetry\n--- task\n`telemetry.csv` has comment lines that **begin** with `#`, blank lines, and readings like `T1,20.5`: a sensor, a comma, a value.\n\n1. With `sed -i`, delete every comment line. A reading line can have a `#` further along, and it must stay.\n2. With `sed -i`, delete every blank line: a line with nothing between its start and its end.\n3. With `sed -i`, change every comma into a space.\n4. Finally, with `sed -n` and `p`, print only the lines for sensor `T1`: lines that **begin** with `T1` followed by a space.\n--- starter\necho "# telemetry, one reading per line" > telemetry.csv\necho "T1,20.5" >> telemetry.csv\necho "" >> telemetry.csv\necho "T2,19.0" >> telemetry.csv\necho "# T1 was recalibrated here" >> telemetry.csv\necho "T1,21.0" >> telemetry.csv\necho "T10,5.5 # new sensor" >> telemetry.csv\necho "" >> telemetry.csv\n--- solution\nsed -i \'/^#/d\' telemetry.csv\nsed -i \'/^$/d\' telemetry.csv\nsed -i \'s/,/ /g\' telemetry.csv\nsed -n \'/^T1 /p\' telemetry.csv\n--- hint\n`/^#/d` deletes the lines that start with `#`. A blank line starts and ends at once: `^$`.\n--- hint\nEvery comma, so `s/,/ /g`. For the last step, `T10` also starts with `T1`, so put the space in the pattern: `/^T1 /p`.\n--- check shell | Only the readings are left, with spaces\nfile telemetry.csv ~= T1 20.5\\nT2 19.0\\nT1 21.0\\nT10 5.5 # new sensor\n--- check shell | Only T1\'s readings were printed\nprinted-exactly T1 20.5\\nT1 21.0\n--- check shell | Deleted and printed with sed\nused /d\nused sed -n\nused /p\n\n=== term3-09 | Lists into arguments: xargs\n--- teach\nLast lesson, `sed` changed the text inside files. This lesson joins two things you already know, pipes and arguments, with one new tool: `xargs`. It turns a list that comes down a pipe into the file names a command works on.\n\nPicture a helper who only acts on what you say to them out loud. Push a shopping list under their door and nothing happens: they never read it. But a friend can pick the list up and read it out to them. `xargs` is that friend.\n\n### Two ways into a command\n\nIn the intermediate course you met the two ways a command gets information. **Arguments** are the words written after its name. **Standard input** is text streaming in, for example through a pipe. `grep`, `sort` and `wc` read their standard input when you give them no file name. Others, such as `rm`, `mkdir`, `touch` and `mv`, [[only ever look at their arguments|never-reads]].\n\nSo piping a list into `rm` deletes nothing:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls *.tmp\na.tmp  b.tmp\n~/project $ ls *.tmp | rm\nrm: missing operand\n```\n\nThe names flowed towards `rm`, and `rm` ignored them. With no arguments it had nothing to delete, and it said so. An [[operand|operand]] is one of the things a command works on.\n\n### xargs: turning input into arguments\n\n**`xargs`** reads the words arriving on its standard input, puts them on the end of a command as arguments, and runs that command. You write the command you want straight after `xargs`:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls *.tmp | xargs rm\n~/project $ ls *.tmp\nls: cannot access \'*.tmp\': No such file or directory\n```\n\n`xargs` read `a.tmp` and `b.tmp` from the pipe, [[built the command|built-command]] `rm a.tmp b.tmp`, and ran it. Both files are gone.\n\n### A list from find\n\n`find`, from the intermediate course, prints the path of every match, at every depth, one per line. That makes it a perfect list for `xargs`. Build it one stage at a time: run `find` on its own and look, then add `| xargs` and the command.\n\n```\n~/project $ find cache -name "*.bak"\ncache/old.bak\ncache/img/pic.bak\n~/project $ find cache -name "*.bak" | xargs ls -l\n-rw-r--r-- 1 you you 120 Mar  3 09:14 cache/img/pic.bak\n-rw-r--r-- 1 you you  64 Mar  2 17:40 cache/old.bak\n```\n\n`xargs` ran `ls -l cache/old.bak cache/img/pic.bak`, so you see the details of both files, however deep they sit.\n\n### A list from a file\n\nThe list can come from a file too. Here `files.txt` names one file per line:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat files.txt\nnotes.txt\ntodo.txt\nplan.txt\n~/project $ cat files.txt | xargs touch\n~/project $ ls\nfiles.txt  notes.txt  plan.txt  todo.txt\n```\n\n`xargs` ran one command, `touch notes.txt todo.txt plan.txt`, and made three empty files. Flags work after `xargs` too: they go right after the command\'s name, as they always do. You could get the same list with the `$(…)` from earlier in this course, but [[xargs has an edge|or-substitution]] on long lists.\n\n### One item per run: -n 1\n\nNormally `xargs` puts every item into one single command. `-n 1`, read "n one", means "at most one item per run": run the command once for each item. `echo` shows the difference:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat planets.txt\nmercury\nvenus\nearth\n~/project $ cat planets.txt | xargs echo\nmercury venus earth\n~/project $ cat planets.txt | xargs -n 1 echo\nmercury\nvenus\nearth\n```\n\nThe first ran `echo` once, with three arguments. The second ran it three times, with one each. `-n 2` would mean two items per run. There is a [[reason one big run is the normal way|batching]].\n\n### The item in the middle: -I {}\n\n`xargs` puts the items at the end. But `mv` needs the file first and the folder last, as in `mv one.log old/`. `-I {}` means "replace: wherever `{}` appears in the command, put the item there". `{}` is an opening and a closing curly brace, with nothing between. With `-I`, the command runs once per item:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls *.log | xargs -I {} mv {} old/\n~/project $ ls old\none.log  two.log\n```\n\nThis ran `mv one.log old/`, then `mv two.log old/`. The `{}` is only a [[marker you choose|marker]].\n\n### Seeing what it runs: -t\n\n`-t` means "trace": print each command right before running it. It is handy while you are checking a new `xargs` line. Here each `echo` command appears, and then what it printed:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat planets.txt | xargs -t -n 1 echo\necho mercury\nmercury\necho venus\nvenus\necho earth\nearth\n```\n\nThe traced lines [[go to standard error|trace-stderr]], not to standard output.\n\n**Watch out:** `xargs` cuts its input at every space, not only at the ends of lines. So a file called `my notes.tmp` arrives as two words, `my` and `notes.tmp`, and `rm` looks for two files that do not exist:\n\n```\n~/project $ ls *.tmp | xargs rm\nrm: cannot remove \'my\': No such file or directory\nrm: cannot remove \'notes.tmp\': No such file or directory\n```\n\nIt is the same word splitting that broke the unquoted `$f` in the loops lesson. For names that may contain spaces, use `find`\'s own `-exec` instead:\n\n```\n~/project $ find . -name "*.tmp" -exec rm {} \\;\n```\n\n`-exec` means "run this command for each file you find". The file\'s name goes where `{}` is, kept whole, spaces and all. The `\\;` at the end is a backslash and a semicolon, and it marks where the command stops. The [[backslash|backslash-semicolon]] is there so the shell leaves the `;` for `find`.\n\n::: context never-reads Readers and non-readers\nWhether a command reads its standard input is decided by whoever wrote it. `sort` and `wc` are built to work on a stream of text, so they read one when you name no file. `rm`, `mkdir` and `mv` act on files and folders you name, so their writers never gave them a reason to read a pipe.\n\nThe intermediate course showed the same thing with `echo`: `ls | echo` prints an empty line, because `echo` only prints its arguments. Whenever a pipe seems to do nothing, ask: does the command on the right read its input at all?\n:::\n\n::: context operand What "missing operand" means\nAn **operand** is a thing an operation is done to. In arithmetic, `3 + 4` has the operands 3 and 4, and `+` is the operator. For `rm`, the operands are the files to delete.\n\nSo "missing operand" is `rm`\'s way of saying "you asked me to delete, but you did not say what". Other tools print the same words: `mkdir` with no folder name says `mkdir: missing operand` too. You will see exactly that message in the next lesson, where it is the first clue in a debugging job.\n:::\n\n::: context built-command How xargs builds the line\n`xargs` collects the words from the pipe and sticks them onto the end of the command you gave it. Only then does it run anything.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 150" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <text x="14" y="26" font-size="12" fill="#6c7a93">from the pipe</text>\n  <rect x="14" y="36" width="66" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="47" y="54" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">a.tmp</text>\n  <rect x="14" y="68" width="66" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="47" y="86" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">b.tmp</text>\n  <line x1="86" y1="65" x2="120" y2="65" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <polygon points="128,65 118,60 118,70" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n  <rect x="132" y="48" width="70" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="167" y="70" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">xargs rm</text>\n  <line x1="208" y1="65" x2="222" y2="65" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <polygon points="230,65 220,60 220,70" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n  <rect x="234" y="48" width="112" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="290" y="70" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">rm <tspan fill="#1d6fd1">a.tmp b.tmp</tspan></text>\n  <text x="180" y="124" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">the words from the pipe become arguments</text>\n</svg>\n```\n:::\n\n::: context or-substitution xargs or $(…)?\n`ls -l $(find cache -name "*.bak")` does the same job: the shell runs `find` first and puts its output in the line, as you saw in the command substitution lesson. It splits at spaces in the same way, too.\n\nThe difference shows with a huge list. A single command line has a size limit, and a folder with hundreds of thousands of files can go over it, so the `$(…)` version fails with `Argument list too long`. `xargs` knows the limit, and when the list is too long it splits the work into several runs of the command on its own.\n:::\n\n::: context batching Why one big run is the default\nStarting a program takes a little time each go. Deleting ten thousand files with one `rm` that is given all ten thousand names is much faster than starting `rm` ten thousand times. So `xargs` packs as many items into each run as it can.\n\n`-n 1` is for commands that take only one item at a time, or when you want each run\'s output kept apart, as with `echo` above.\n:::\n\n::: context marker Any marker, whole lines\n`{}` is only a custom. `-I` takes whatever marker you write after it: `xargs -I % mv % old/` works the same way. People pick `{}` because it matches `find -exec`, and because it rarely appears in real file names.\n\n`-I` changes one more thing. It reads its input a whole line at a time, so each line is one item even if it contains spaces. That makes `ls *.tmp | xargs -I {} rm {}` safe for `my notes.tmp`, as long as every name is on its own line.\n:::\n\n::: context trace-stderr Where the trace goes\n`xargs -t` writes each command it is about to run to standard error, the stream for messages, which you met in the intermediate course. Standard output keeps only what the commands themselves print.\n\nThat is a sensible split. If you save the results with `> out.txt`, the file gets only the results, while the trace still shows on your screen, so you can watch the work without spoiling the output. Near the end of this course, your own scripts send their error messages the same way.\n:::\n\n::: context backslash-semicolon Why \\; and not ;\nYou met `;` in the loops lesson: to the shell it means "this command ends here, start the next one". If you typed a bare `;` after `find . -name "*.tmp" -exec rm {}`, the shell would cut the line there, and `find` would never see where its command stops. It complains: `missing argument to -exec`.\n\nA backslash in front, `\\;`, tells the shell "this is an ordinary character, pass it on". So `find` receives a `;` as one of its arguments and knows the command is finished. Writing `-exec rm {} +` instead gathers many names into one `rm`, the way `xargs` does.\n:::\n--- task\nThe folder `build` has `.tmp` files at several depths, mixed in with `app.js` and `site.css`, which you must keep.\n\n1. Delete every `.tmp` file anywhere under `build`, using `find` piped into `xargs rm`.\n2. `dirs.txt` lists three folders, one per line. Make all of them with one command: `cat dirs.txt` piped into `xargs mkdir -p`.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p build/css build/js/vendor\ntouch build/a.tmp build/css/b.tmp build/js/vendor/c.tmp build/js/app.js build/css/site.css\necho "assets" > dirs.txt\necho "assets/img" >> dirs.txt\necho "logs" >> dirs.txt\n--- solution\nfind build -name "*.tmp" | xargs rm\ncat dirs.txt | xargs mkdir -p\n--- hint\nLook at the list before deleting anything: `find build -name "*.tmp"` should print three paths, and nothing else.\n--- hint\nWhen the list looks right, press the Up arrow and add `| xargs rm` to the end of that same line.\n--- hint\n`find build -name "*.tmp" | xargs rm`, then `cat dirs.txt | xargs mkdir -p`. `ls` should show `assets` and `logs`.\n--- check shell | Every .tmp file is gone, and nothing else\nmissing build/a.tmp\nmissing build/css/b.tmp\nmissing build/js/vendor/c.tmp\nfile build/js/app.js\nfile build/css/site.css\nused xargs rm\n--- check shell | The folders from dirs.txt exist\ndir assets/img\ndir logs\nused xargs mkdir\n\n+++ practice | Delete what the list says\n--- task\n`obsolete.txt` names three old files, one per line. Delete exactly those three with **one** pipeline: `cat obsolete.txt` piped into `xargs rm`. `keep.txt` is not on the list and must stay.\n--- starter\ntouch old-map.png draft1.txt draft2.txt keep.txt\necho "old-map.png" > obsolete.txt\necho "draft1.txt" >> obsolete.txt\necho "draft2.txt" >> obsolete.txt\n--- solution\ncat obsolete.txt | xargs rm\n--- hint\n`rm` never reads its standard input. `xargs` turns the names arriving through the pipe into arguments for it.\n--- hint\n`cat obsolete.txt | xargs rm`.\n--- check shell | The three listed files are gone\nmissing old-map.png\nmissing draft1.txt\nmissing draft2.txt\n--- check shell | Everything else stayed\nfile keep.txt\nfile obsolete.txt\n--- check shell | Deleted through xargs\nused xargs rm\n\n+++ practice | Copy the chosen ones\n--- task\n`wanted.txt` names three files, one per line. Copy each of them into the folder `pick`, with **one** pipeline: `cat wanted.txt` piped into `xargs` with `-I {}`, so each name goes in the middle of the `cp` command, before the folder.\n--- starter\nmkdir pick\necho "burn at T+120" > plan.txt\ntouch crew.txt map.png notes.txt\necho "plan.txt" > wanted.txt\necho "crew.txt" >> wanted.txt\necho "map.png" >> wanted.txt\n--- solution\ncat wanted.txt | xargs -I {} cp {} pick/\n--- hint\n`cp` needs the file first and the folder last. `-I {}` puts each item wherever `{}` is written.\n--- hint\n`xargs -I {} cp {} pick/`.\n--- check shell | The three chosen files are in pick\ncount pick/* == 3\nfile pick/plan.txt == burn at T+120\nfile pick/map.png\n--- check shell | Nothing else was copied\nmissing pick/notes.txt\n--- check shell | Placed with -I\nused xargs -I\n\n+++ practice | Replace the old call, only where it is marked\n--- task\nSome files under `src` are marked with the word `DEPRECATED`. In **those files only**, change every `oldApi` to `newApi`, in the files themselves.\n\nUse one pipeline: `grep` to list the names of the marked files (at any depth), piped into `xargs sed -i`. `src/extra.js` uses `oldApi` too, but it is not marked, so it must stay as it is.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p src/net\necho "// DEPRECATED: move off oldApi" > src/main.js\necho "x = oldApi(1) + oldApi(2)" >> src/main.js\necho "// DEPRECATED" > src/net/client.js\necho "y = oldApi(url)" >> src/net/client.js\necho "z = oldApi(0)" > src/extra.js\n--- solution\ngrep -rl DEPRECATED src | xargs sed -i \'s/oldApi/newApi/g\'\n--- hint\n`grep -rl DEPRECATED src` prints the name of each marked file, once. Look at that list before going on.\n--- hint\nThen add `| xargs sed -i \'s/oldApi/newApi/g\'`. The `g` catches the line that calls it twice.\n--- check shell | main.js now calls newApi everywhere\nfile src/main.js ~= // DEPRECATED: move off newApi\\nx = newApi(1) + newApi(2)\n--- check shell | So does client.js\nfile src/net/client.js ~= // DEPRECATED\\ny = newApi(url)\n--- check shell | The unmarked file was left alone\nfile src/extra.js == z = oldApi(0)\nused xargs sed -i\n\n+++ practice | Backups with spaces in their names\n--- task\nThe folder `work` has `.bak` files at several depths, and two of them have spaces in their names. Move every one of them into the folder `trash`, with **one** `find` command that searches `work`. Everything else in `work` must stay where it is.\n\n`find … | xargs mv …` would cut the names with spaces in two, so let `find` run `mv` itself on each file.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p work/docs/old trash\ntouch work/a.bak "work/flight plan.bak" work/docs/b.bak "work/docs/old/crew list.bak" work/docs/readme.txt work/notes.txt\n--- solution\nfind work -name "*.bak" -exec mv {} trash/ \\;\n--- hint\n`-exec` runs a command for each file `find` finds, with the whole name, spaces and all, where `{}` is written. `{}` can sit anywhere in the command, here before the folder.\n--- hint\nThe command ends with `\\;`, a backslash and a semicolon. The backslash keeps the shell\'s hands off the `;`.\n--- check shell | Every .bak file is in trash, names whole\ncount trash/*.bak == 4\ncount trash/flight?plan.bak == 1\ncount trash/crew?list.bak == 1\n--- check shell | None are left in work, and the rest stayed\ncount work/*.bak == 0\nmissing work/docs/b.bak\nfile work/docs/readme.txt\nfile work/notes.txt\n--- check shell | find ran mv itself\nused -exec mv\n\n+++ practice | The folder in the wrong place\n--- task\nA teammate wanted to copy every `.log` file under `logs`, at any depth, into the folder `archive`, and typed:\n\n```\nfind logs -name "*.log" | xargs cp archive/\n```\n\nIt failed with an error about a target that is not a directory, and nothing was copied. Copy every `.log` file under `logs` into `archive` with a corrected pipeline that still starts with the same `find` and pipes into `xargs`.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p logs/day1 logs/day2 archive\necho "boot ok" > logs/day1/boot.log\ntouch logs/day1/radio.log logs/day2/pump.log logs/day2/notes.txt\n--- solution\nfind logs -name "*.log" | xargs -I {} cp {} archive/\n--- hint\n`xargs` puts the items at the **end** of the command, so the teammate\'s line ran `cp archive/ logs/day1/boot.log …`. What did `cp` think the target was?\n--- hint\n`-I {}` lets you place each item before the folder: `cp {} archive/`.\n--- check shell | Every log was copied into archive\ncount archive/*.log == 3\nfile archive/boot.log == boot ok\n--- check shell | The originals stayed, and notes.txt was not copied\nfile logs/day2/pump.log\nmissing archive/notes.txt\n--- check shell | Still find piped into xargs\nused find logs -name\nused | xargs -I\n\n+++ practice | Gather the failed runs\n--- task\nUnder `runs` are test logs at several depths. A run failed if its log contains `FAIL`.\n\n1. Make a folder called `failed`.\n2. With one pipeline, copy every log under `runs` that contains `FAIL` into `failed`: `grep` to list the file names, piped into `xargs` with `-I {}` and `cp`.\n3. Print how many logs are now in `failed`, as a single number, with `ls` and `wc -l`.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p runs/day1 runs/day2/night\necho "boot ok" > runs/day1/t1.log\necho "radio FAIL" > runs/day1/t2.log\necho "pump ok" > runs/day2/t3.log\necho "fan FAIL" > runs/day2/t4.log\necho "valve FAIL" > runs/day2/night/t5.log\necho "all ok" > runs/day2/night/t6.log\n--- solution\nmkdir failed\ngrep -rl FAIL runs | xargs -I {} cp {} failed/\nls failed | wc -l\n--- hint\n`grep -rl FAIL runs` lists every file under `runs` with at least one `FAIL` line.\n--- hint\nPipe that into `xargs -I {} cp {} failed/`. Then `ls failed | wc -l`.\n--- check shell | The three failed logs were gathered\ncount failed/*.log == 3\nfile failed/t4.log == fan FAIL\nmissing failed/t1.log\n--- check shell | Counted: three\nprinted-exactly 3\n--- check shell | Gathered through xargs\nused xargs\nused grep -rl\n\n=== term3-10 | Debugging a script\n--- teach\nLast lesson ended with a warning about spaces in file names. Back in the variables lesson, you were warned that a misspelled variable makes no noise. This lesson hands you a script with both of those bugs in it, and a method for finding them.\n\nThink of a good mechanic with a car that will not start. They do not swap parts at random. They listen to it, look at what is really happening, test one thing at a time, and after the repair they start the car again to be sure. Finding and fixing mistakes in a program is called **[[debugging|debugging-word]]**, and it works the same way.\n\nHere is the bug report you will work on:\n\n> `archive.sh` should move every `.txt` note into `archive/`. Running `bash archive.sh` prints errors, and the notes stay where they are.\n\n### Step 1: make it happen, and read from the top\n\nFirst, **reproduce** the problem: make it happen again yourself, so you can see it. Run the script:\n\n```\n~/project $ bash archive.sh\nmkdir: missing operand\nmv: cannot move \'ideas.txt\' to \'archive/\': Not a directory\nmv: target \'archive/\' is not a directory\nmv: cannot move \'todo.txt\' to \'archive/\': Not a directory\n```\n\nFour errors, but read them **from the top**. The first error is often the real cause. The ones after it are often **knock-on effects**: trouble caused by the first problem, not new problems of their own. Here, `mkdir` was given no folder name (the `missing operand` from last lesson). So `archive` was never made, and then every `mv` failed because there was [[no folder to move into|knock-on]].\n\n### Step 2: watch it run: bash -x\n\n`bash -x archive.sh` runs the script in **trace** mode: `-x` makes bash print every command right before it runs it, with a `+` in front. The printed command is shown **after** the variables have been filled in. So you see exactly what really ran, not what you meant to write.\n\nA tiny script shows the idea. `hello.sh` has a typo in it: `$nme` where `$name` was meant.\n\n```\n~/project $ cat hello.sh\nname=Ada\necho "Hello, $nme"\n~/project $ bash -x hello.sh\n+ name=Ada\n+ echo \'Hello, \'\nHello,\n```\n\nThe lines starting with `+` are the trace. The last line is the script\'s real output. The trace shows `echo` received `Hello, ` with nothing after it: the variable was empty. The single quotes around `\'Hello, \'` are bash showing you [[where one word starts and ends|trace-quotes]].\n\nNow trace the broken script. These are the first lines it prints:\n\n```\n~/project $ bash -x archive.sh\n+ dest=archive\n+ mkdir -p\nmkdir: missing operand\n```\n\nAfter `-p` there is nothing. The variable on that line was empty.\n\n### Step 3: check your assumptions\n\nAn **assumption** is something you believe is true without having checked it. Bugs [[hide in assumptions|assumptions]]. So look at the script itself, with `cat`, and ask two questions of every line:\n\n- Is every variable spelled the same everywhere it is used?\n- Is every variable in double quotes, like `"$f"`, so a name with spaces stays one word?\n\nThe trace helps with the second question too. An unquoted `$f` holding `meeting notes.txt` shows up as `+ mv meeting notes.txt archive/`: three separate words, with no quotes to hold the name together.\n\n### Step 4: fix the cause, then run it again\n\nFix the **cause**, the line that is really wrong. Then run the script again, and check the result: not only that the errors stopped, but that the files are where they should be. `ls archive` answers that. A script can finish with no errors and still do the wrong thing.\n\n### Catching typos early: set -u\n\n`set -u` as the first line of a script turns on one extra rule: using a variable that was never set is an error, and the script stops right there. The `-u` stands for "unset". Here is the typo from `hello.sh`, in a script that starts with `set -u`:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat check.sh\nset -u\nname=Ada\necho "Hello, $nme"\n~/project $ bash check.sh\ncheck.sh: line 3: nme: unbound variable\n```\n\n**Unbound** means "not tied to any value". The message names the line and the variable, so the silent typo from the variables lesson [[now makes a noise|strict-mode]].\n\n**Watch out:** do not "fix" the knock-on effects. Making the `archive` folder by hand with `mkdir archive` would stop the `mv` errors, but the script would still be broken: on the next computer it runs on, it fails again. Fix the line that caused the first error.\n\n::: context debugging-word Where "bug" comes from\nEngineers were calling faults "bugs" long before computers; Thomas Edison used the word for problems in his inventions in the 1870s. A favorite story comes from 1947: operators of the Harvard Mark II computer found a moth stuck in a relay and taped it into their logbook as the "first actual case of bug being found".\n\nThe joke works because the word was already common. Debugging now means the whole job: making the fault happen, finding its cause, fixing it and checking the fix.\n:::\n\n::: context knock-on One mistake, many errors\nA single typo started the whole chain. Every error after the first was a result of it.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 200" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="80" y="8" width="200" height="30" rx="6" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="180" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">typo: $dets, not $dest</text>\n  <line x1="180" y1="38" x2="180" y2="50" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <polygon points="180,58 175,48 185,48" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n  <rect x="80" y="58" width="200" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="78" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">mkdir -p gets no name</text>\n  <line x1="180" y1="88" x2="180" y2="100" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <polygon points="180,108 175,98 185,98" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n  <rect x="80" y="108" width="200" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="128" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">archive is never made</text>\n  <line x1="180" y1="138" x2="180" y2="150" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <polygon points="180,158 175,148 185,148" fill="#1f2a44"/>\n  <rect x="80" y="158" width="200" height="30" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="180" y="178" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">every mv fails</text>\n  <text x="290" y="28" font-size="11" fill="#b4232c">the cause</text>\n  <text x="290" y="178" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">what you see</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThat is why you start from the top: fix the cause, and the errors below it often disappear too.\n:::\n\n::: context trace-quotes Reading the trace\nIn a trace, bash writes each command so that you could type it back in and get the same thing. A word that contains a space, or is empty, is shown inside single quotes, so you can see where it begins and ends.\n\nSo `+ mv \'meeting notes.txt\' archive/` means `mv` got two arguments, and the first was the whole name. `+ mv meeting notes.txt archive/`, with no quotes, means it got three. Those quotes are how you spot word splitting in a trace.\n\nYou can also switch tracing on inside a script with the line `set -x`, and off again with `set +x`, to watch only the part you suspect.\n:::\n\n::: context assumptions The costliest assumption\nIn 1999 NASA lost the Mars Climate Orbiter as it arrived at Mars. One team\'s software reported thruster data in pound-force seconds, a US unit; the navigation software assumed it was in newton-seconds, the metric unit. Nobody checked, and the small error in each number built up over the months-long trip until the spacecraft passed far too close to the planet and was lost.\n\nThe fix would have been one question: "what unit is this number really in?". Checking what a variable really holds is the same habit at a smaller size.\n:::\n\n::: context strict-mode Stricter scripts\nMany real scripts begin with a line like this:\n\n```\nset -euo pipefail\n```\n\n`-u` you have met. `-e` stops the script as soon as a command fails, instead of carrying on into knock-on errors. (A command used as the question of an `if` is allowed to fail; that is its job.) `-o pipefail` makes a pipeline count as failed if any stage in it fails, not only the last one. Together they make a script stop at the first sign of trouble, close to the cause, which is exactly where you want to start debugging.\n:::\n--- task\n`archive.sh` is the script from the bug report. It has **two** bugs. The four steps above will find both.\n\n1. Run it with `bash archive.sh` and read the errors from the top.\n2. Trace it with `bash -x archive.sh`.\n3. Fix both bugs by writing `archive.sh` again: the same three lines, built with `echo \'…\'` (`>` for the first line, `>>` for the others), like the original.\n4. Run `bash archive.sh` again. All three notes must end up in `archive`, with no errors.\n--- starter\ntouch "meeting notes.txt" ideas.txt todo.txt\necho \'dest=archive\' > archive.sh\necho \'mkdir -p $dets\' >> archive.sh\necho \'for f in *.txt; do mv $f $dest/; done\' >> archive.sh\n--- solution\nbash archive.sh\nbash -x archive.sh\necho \'dest=archive\' > archive.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "$dest"\' >> archive.sh\necho \'for f in *.txt; do mv "$f" "$dest/"; done\' >> archive.sh\nbash archive.sh\n--- hint\n`cat archive.sh` shows the script. In the `bash -x` trace, look at the line that begins `+ mkdir -p`: what came after `-p`?\n--- hint\nLine 1 sets `dest`, but line 2 uses a different spelling. And in the loop, `mv $f` has no quotes, so `meeting notes.txt` is split into two words. Write it as `"$f"`.\n--- hint\nWrite it again: `echo \'dest=archive\' > archive.sh`, then add with `>>` the line `mkdir -p "$dest"`, then the loop `for f in *.txt; do mv "$f" "$dest/"; done`. Finish with `bash archive.sh` and `ls archive`.\n--- check shell | You traced the script\nran bash -x archive.sh\nprinted-line + mkdir -p\n--- check shell | Every note is in archive, and none are left behind\ncount archive/*.txt == 3\ncount *.txt == 0\n--- check shell | The script is fixed, and ran cleanly\nfile archive.sh excludes $dets\nfile archive.sh contains "$f"\nstatus 0\n\n+++ practice | The build folder with no number\n--- task\n`build.sh` should make a folder called `build-4` and print `built 4`. Instead it makes a folder called `build-` and prints `built 4`.\n\n1. Trace it with `bash -x build.sh`, and find the line where the value goes missing.\n2. Write `build.sh` again with the bug fixed: the same three lines, built with `echo \'…\'` (`>` for the first, `>>` for the others).\n3. Run `bash build.sh`, then delete the wrong folder `build-` that the broken runs made.\n--- starter\necho \'version=4\' > build.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "build-$verison"\' >> build.sh\necho \'echo "built $version"\' >> build.sh\n--- solution\nbash -x build.sh\necho \'version=4\' > build.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "build-$version"\' >> build.sh\necho \'echo "built $version"\' >> build.sh\nbash build.sh\nrm -r build-\n--- hint\nIn the trace, look at the `+ mkdir` line. What came after `build-`?\n--- hint\nLine 1 sets `version`, but line 2 spells it differently. `cat build.sh` shows the script as it really is.\n--- hint\nFix line 2 to `mkdir -p "build-$version"`, run the script, then remove the empty folder with `rm -r build-`.\n--- check shell | You traced the script\nran bash -x build.sh\nprinted-line + mkdir -p build-\n--- check shell | build-4 exists, and build- is gone\ndir build-4\nmissing build-\n--- check shell | The typo is fixed\nfile build.sh excludes verison\nfile build.sh contains $version\n\n+++ practice | Make the typo loud\n--- task\n`hello.sh` prints `Hello, ` and then nothing, because of a misspelled variable. Make the script catch its own typo:\n\n1. Write `hello.sh` again with `set -u` as a new **first** line, followed by its two lines unchanged, still with the typo. Run it with `bash hello.sh`: now it stops with an `unbound variable` error that names the misspelled variable.\n2. Write it once more with the typo fixed, keeping `set -u` at the top, and run it so it prints `Hello, Lin`.\n--- starter\necho \'crew=Lin\' > hello.sh\necho \'echo "Hello, $crwe"\' >> hello.sh\n--- solution\necho \'set -u\' > hello.sh\necho \'crew=Lin\' >> hello.sh\necho \'echo "Hello, $crwe"\' >> hello.sh\nbash hello.sh\necho \'set -u\' > hello.sh\necho \'crew=Lin\' >> hello.sh\necho \'echo "Hello, $crew"\' >> hello.sh\nbash hello.sh\n--- hint\n`set -u` must come before the line it guards, so it goes in with `>`, and the other lines follow with `>>`.\n--- hint\nThe error message names the variable that was never set: `crwe`. The one that was set is `crew`.\n--- check shell | set -u caught the typo\nprinted crwe: unbound variable\n--- check shell | The fixed script greets Lin\nprinted-line Hello, Lin\n--- check shell | set -u is still the first line\nfile hello.sh ~= set -u\\ncrew=Lin\\necho "Hello, $crew"\n\n+++ practice | The report with two bugs\n--- task\n`report.sh` takes a log file as its first argument and should print a line like:\n\n```\nnight shift.log: 2 of 5 lines\n```\n\nRun on `night shift.log`, it prints errors, and a line with blanks in it. It has **two** bugs.\n\n1. Trace it with `bash -x report.sh "night shift.log"`.\n2. Write it again with both bugs fixed: the same three lines, built with `echo \'…\'`.\n3. Run `bash report.sh "night shift.log"` so it prints `night shift.log: 2 of 5 lines`.\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "INFO $i" >> "night shift.log"; done\necho "ERROR fan" >> "night shift.log"\necho "ERROR pump" >> "night shift.log"\necho \'total=$(cat $1 | wc -l)\' > report.sh\necho \'errors=$(grep -c ERROR "$1")\' >> report.sh\necho \'echo "$1: $errors of $totl lines"\' >> report.sh\n--- solution\nbash -x report.sh "night shift.log"\necho \'total=$(cat "$1" | wc -l)\' > report.sh\necho \'errors=$(grep -c ERROR "$1")\' >> report.sh\necho \'echo "$1: $errors of $total lines"\' >> report.sh\nbash report.sh "night shift.log"\n--- hint\nIn the trace, find the `cat` line: how many words did `cat` get? And on the last line, which variable is empty?\n--- hint\nOne `$1` has no quotes around it, so the name is cut in two. And the last line uses a name that was never set.\n--- hint\nLine 1 becomes `total=$(cat "$1" | wc -l)`, and line 3 uses `$total`.\n--- check shell | You traced it\nran bash -x report.sh\n--- check shell | The report is right\nprinted-line night shift.log: 2 of 5 lines\n--- check shell | Both bugs are fixed\nfile report.sh excludes $totl\nfile report.sh contains cat "$1"\n\n+++ practice | Names with spaces, and the mess they left\n--- task\n`done.sh` marks a job as finished by making an empty file named after its first argument with `.done` on the end. It works for `engine`, but a teammate ran it as `bash done.sh "flight plan"`, and it made two files, `flight` and `plan.done`, instead of one.\n\n1. Trace `bash -x done.sh "flight plan"` and look at the `touch` line.\n2. Write `done.sh` again so that names with spaces work.\n3. Delete the two stray files, `flight` and `plan.done`.\n4. Run `bash done.sh "flight plan"`, then `bash done.sh engine`.\n--- starter\necho \'touch $1.done\' > done.sh\nbash done.sh "flight plan"\n--- solution\nbash -x done.sh "flight plan"\necho \'touch "$1.done"\' > done.sh\nrm flight plan.done\nbash done.sh "flight plan"\nbash done.sh engine\n--- hint\nIn the trace, `+ touch flight plan.done` has no quotes: `touch` was given two words.\n--- hint\nInside the script, `"$1.done"` keeps the name whole. `rm flight plan.done` removes the two stray files.\n--- check shell | One file for the job with a space\ncount flight?plan.done == 1\nfile engine.done\n--- check shell | The stray files are gone\nmissing flight\nmissing plan.done\n--- check shell | The script quotes its argument\nfile done.sh contains "$1.done"\nran bash -x done.sh\n\n+++ practice | Fix the cause, not the knock-on\n--- task\n`collect.sh` should gather every `.csv` file into a folder called `collected` and then list it. Running it prints three errors. It holds:\n\n```\nout_dir=collected\nmkdir -p $outdir\ncp *.csv $out_dir/\nls $out_dir\n```\n\n1. Run `bash collect.sh` and read the errors from the top.\n2. Fix the line that causes the **first** error, by writing `collect.sh` again (all four lines, built with `echo \'…\'`). Do not make the folder by hand: the script must make it itself.\n3. Run `bash collect.sh` again. It must list the three `.csv` files, with no errors.\n--- starter\ntouch crew.csv fuel.csv orbit.csv notes.txt\necho \'out_dir=collected\' > collect.sh\necho \'mkdir -p $outdir\' >> collect.sh\necho \'cp *.csv $out_dir/\' >> collect.sh\necho \'ls $out_dir\' >> collect.sh\n--- solution\nbash collect.sh\necho \'out_dir=collected\' > collect.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "$out_dir"\' >> collect.sh\necho \'cp *.csv "$out_dir/"\' >> collect.sh\necho \'ls "$out_dir"\' >> collect.sh\nbash collect.sh\n--- hint\nThe first error is `mkdir: missing operand`. The `cp` and `ls` errors come after it, because the folder was never made.\n--- hint\nLine 1 sets `out_dir`, with an underscore. Line 2 uses a name without it.\n--- hint\nLine 2 becomes `mkdir -p "$out_dir"`. Quoting the other two is a good habit too.\n--- check shell | The csv files were collected\ncount collected/*.csv == 3\nmissing collected/notes.txt\n--- check shell | The last run listed them, with no errors\nlast-printed-exactly crew.csv fuel.csv orbit.csv\n--- check shell | The script makes the folder itself\nfile collect.sh excludes $outdir\nran bash collect.sh\n\n+++ practice | Two bugs in the log check\n--- task\n`check.sh` counts the `.log` files and should print `too many logs: 4` when there are more than 3, or `logs ok: ` and the count otherwise. It holds:\n\n```\nset -u\nlimit=3\ncount=$(ls *.log | wc -l)\nif [ $count -gt $limt ] then echo "too many logs: $count"; else echo "logs ok: $count"; fi\n```\n\nIt has two bugs on its last line. Find them by running it and reading the errors (fix one, run again, and read what comes next). Write the script again with both fixed, all four lines, and run it: with four logs it prints `too many logs: 4`. Then delete `old.log` and run it again: it prints `logs ok: 3`.\n--- starter\ntouch boot.log radio.log pump.log old.log\necho \'set -u\' > check.sh\necho \'limit=3\' >> check.sh\necho \'count=$(ls *.log | wc -l)\' >> check.sh\necho \'if [ $count -gt $limt ] then echo "too many logs: $count"; else echo "logs ok: $count"; fi\' >> check.sh\n--- solution\nbash check.sh\necho \'set -u\' > check.sh\necho \'limit=3\' >> check.sh\necho \'count=$(ls *.log | wc -l)\' >> check.sh\necho \'if [ "$count" -gt "$limit" ]; then echo "too many logs: $count"; else echo "logs ok: $count"; fi\' >> check.sh\nbash check.sh\nrm old.log\nbash check.sh\n--- hint\nA `syntax error near unexpected token` means the shell could not even read the line. Count the semicolons around `then`.\n--- hint\n`set -u` names the variable that was never set. Compare it with the one set on line 2.\n--- hint\nThe last line becomes `if [ "$count" -gt "$limit" ]; then …`.\n--- check shell | Four logs are too many\nprinted-line too many logs: 4\n--- check shell | Three logs are ok\nprinted-line logs ok: 3\nmissing old.log\n--- check shell | Both bugs are fixed, and set -u stayed\nfile check.sh excludes $limt\nfile check.sh contains ]; then\nfile check.sh contains set -u\n\n=== term3-11 | Problem solving: tidying a downloads folder\n--- teach\nLast lesson was a method for fixing a script. This lesson is a method for writing one. The problem is a real one: a downloads folder with thirty files of every kind, all mixed up. The goal is one folder per kind of file. You will work it out in five steps before you type the loop.\n\nThink of sorting a big pile of laundry into drawers. You do not start throwing things about. First you say what "sorted" means (socks here, shirts there). You look through the pile for anything odd. You put one sock away by hand. Then you do the rest the same way, and at the end you check the drawers.\n\n### Step 1: say the problem in your own words\n\n**[[Restating|restate]]** a problem means saying exactly what "done" looks like, before you begin. Here: every file with an extension goes into a folder named after that extension. `photo-1.jpg` goes into `jpg/`, and `invoice-3.pdf` goes into `pdf/`.\n\n### Step 2: look at the data\n\n**Surveying** the data means looking at what is really there before you write anything. `ls | head` shows the first ten names:\n\n```\n~/project $ cd downloads\n~/project/downloads $ ls | head\nREADME\nbackup.zip\nfonts.zip\ninvoice-1.pdf\ninvoice-2.pdf\ninvoice-3.pdf\ninvoice-4.pdf\ninvoice-5.pdf\ninvoice-6.pdf\ninvoice-7.pdf\n```\n\nAlready there is a surprise: `README` has no dot at all. To count each kind of file, use a pipeline:\n\n```\n~/project/downloads $ ls | sed \'s/.*\\.//\' | sort | uniq -c\n      1 README\n     12 jpg\n      9 pdf\n      6 txt\n      2 zip\n```\n\nThe `sed` part, `s/.*\\.//`, reads "find: any characters, then a real dot; put: nothing". So it cuts each name down to its extension. `photo-1.jpg` becomes `jpg`. Two new pieces of pattern are in there:\n\n- `.*` is "a dot and a star". In a regular expression, `*` means "the thing before me, repeated any number of times, even none". So `.*` means [[any run of characters|greedy-star]].\n- `\\.` is "backslash, dot". In the sed lesson you saw that `.` means "any one character". A backslash in front [[turns it back into a real dot|backslash-dot]].\n\nA name with no dot is left untouched, which is why `README` shows up in the count. Then `sort | uniq -c`, from the intermediate course, counts each kind. Ask two questions of the survey: are there files **without** an extension? Are there names **with spaces**? (The full `ls` shows one: `notes final.txt`. The quotes in your loop will keep it whole.)\n\n### Step 3: solve one case by hand\n\nTake one file and write down the commands that tidy it:\n\n```\n~/project/downloads $ f=photo-1.jpg\n~/project/downloads $ ext="${f##*.}"\n~/project/downloads $ echo "$ext"\njpg\n~/project/downloads $ mkdir -p "$ext"\n~/project/downloads $ mv "$f" "$ext/"\n```\n\n`${f##*.}` is read "f with everything up to the last dot cut off". It is a cousin of `${f#draft_}` from the renaming lesson, where `#` cut text off the start. Here the thing to cut is the pattern `*.`, which means "any characters, then a dot" (the `*` is the file-name wildcard again, not the regular expression one). One `#` cuts the **shortest** match, up to the first dot. Two, `##`, cut the **longest**, up to the [[last dot|shortest-longest]]:\n\n```\n~/project/downloads $ f=site.tar.gz\n~/project/downloads $ echo "${f#*.}"\ntar.gz\n~/project/downloads $ echo "${f##*.}"\ngz\n```\n\n### Step 4: turn it into a loop\n\nNow put those steps inside a `for` loop, with `"$f"` changing each time round. The list matters. `*.*` is read "anything, a dot, anything". It matches only names that contain a dot, so files without an extension, such as `README`, are left out of the loop, and stay where they are.\n\n### Step 5: dry run, run, check\n\nRehearse first, as in the renaming lesson. A quick way is to print where each file would go:\n\n```\n~/project/downloads $ for f in *.*; do echo "$f goes to ${f##*.}/"; done\nbackup.zip goes to zip/\nfonts.zip goes to zip/\ninvoice-1.pdf goes to pdf/\n```\n\n(and so on, one line per file). Or put `echo` in front of the `mv` in your real loop. When every line looks right, run it for real. Then **verify**: count what landed in each folder, for example `ls jpg | wc -l`, and compare it with the survey. Twelve jpg files before means [[twelve in jpg/ after|verify]].\n\nInside the loop, `mkdir -p` runs once for every file, so twelve times for `jpg`. That is fine: `-p` does nothing when the folder already exists. Plain `mkdir` would complain eleven times. `-p` is what makes the loop [[safe to repeat|repeatable]].\n\n**Watch out:** loop over `*.*`, not over `*`. With `*`, `README` goes into the loop too. `${f##*.}` finds no dot to cut, so the "extension" comes out as the whole name, `README`, and the loop tries to move `README` into a folder called `README`. You get errors, and a file you were meant to leave alone is in the middle of it.\n\n::: context restate Why engineers restate the problem\nIn aerospace, the written statement of what a system must do is called its **requirements**, and a surprising number of expensive mistakes start with a requirement that two people read in two different ways. So teams restate: "so what you want is…", in words exact enough to check.\n\nThe restatement here also settles questions before they cause trouble. What about a file with no extension? The words "every file **with** an extension" answer it: leave it alone.\n:::\n\n::: context greedy-star The star takes as much as it can\n`.*` does not stop at the first dot it could. It is **greedy**: it grabs as many characters as it can while still letting the rest of the pattern, `\\.`, match. So it runs all the way to the last dot in the name.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 110" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="20" y="20" width="148" height="30" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="94" y="40" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">site.tar</text>\n  <rect x="172" y="20" width="22" height="30" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <text x="183" y="40" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">.</text>\n  <rect x="198" y="20" width="50" height="30" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="223" y="40" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">gz</text>\n  <text x="94" y="70" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">.*</text>\n  <text x="183" y="70" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">\\.</text>\n  <text x="223" y="70" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">kept</text>\n  <text x="180" y="98" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">orange is cut away, blue is kept</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nCareful: this star is the regular expression star, which repeats the thing before it. The file-name wildcard `*` means "any characters" on its own. They look the same and do different jobs.\n:::\n\n::: context backslash-dot A backslash takes away the special meaning\nIn a regular expression, a few characters have special jobs: `.` for any character, `^` for the start, `$` for the end, `*` for "repeat". A backslash in front of one of them **escapes** it: it becomes an ordinary character again. So `\\.` is a real dot.\n\nWithout it, the survey goes wrong. `sed \'s/.*.//\'` means "any characters, then any one character", which matches the whole name, and every line comes out empty.\n:::\n\n::: context shortest-longest One # or two\nBoth forms cut from the start of the value. They differ in how much they cut.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 130" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <text x="10" y="30" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">${f#*.}</text>\n  <rect x="100" y="14" width="44" height="26" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="122" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">site.</text>\n  <rect x="148" y="14" width="80" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <text x="188" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">tar.gz</text>\n  <text x="240" y="32" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">shortest cut</text>\n  <text x="10" y="80" font-size="12" fill="#1f2a44">${f##*.}</text>\n  <rect x="100" y="64" width="84" height="26" rx="4" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44"/>\n  <text x="142" y="82" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">site.tar.</text>\n  <rect x="188" y="64" width="40" height="26" rx="4" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <text x="208" y="82" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">gz</text>\n  <text x="240" y="82" font-size="11" fill="#6c7a93">longest cut</text>\n  <text x="180" y="118" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">orange is cut off, blue is what is left</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThe same doubling works at the end: `${f%.*}` gives `site.tar`, and `${f%%.*}` gives `site`.\n:::\n\n::: context verify Checking that the numbers add up\nThe survey said 12 + 9 + 6 + 2 = 29 files with an extension, plus `README`, which makes the 30 that `ls | wc -l` counts. After the loop, the four folders should hold 29 files between them, and `README` should be the only file left loose.\n\nChecking a result against a count you made a different way is a habit worth keeping. A mission control team does the same when two separate systems track a spacecraft: if their answers disagree, something is wrong, even when each answer looks fine on its own.\n:::\n\n::: context repeatable Safe to run twice\nA command that leaves things the same whether you run it once or many times is called **idempotent** (say "eye-dem-POH-tent"). `mkdir -p jpg` is idempotent: after the first run, the rest change nothing and print nothing.\n\nSteps like that are easy to trust. If a script stops halfway, you can fix the problem and run its `mkdir -p` lines again without any harm. Setup scripts in real projects use `mkdir -p` almost every time for exactly this reason.\n:::\n--- task\nThe folder `downloads` holds thirty files: photos, invoices, notes, zip files and a `README`.\n\nGo into `downloads` with `cd downloads`. Then, with **one** `for` loop, move every file **that has an extension** into a folder named after its extension: `photo-1.jpg` goes to `downloads/jpg/`, `invoice-1.pdf` to `downloads/pdf/`, and so on. Use `${f##*.}` to get the extension and `mkdir -p` to make the folder. Leave `README`, which has no extension, where it is.\n--- starter\nmkdir downloads\nfor i in $(seq 1 12); do touch "downloads/photo-$i.jpg"; done\nfor i in $(seq 1 9); do touch "downloads/invoice-$i.pdf"; done\nfor i in $(seq 1 5); do touch "downloads/notes-$i.txt"; done\ntouch "downloads/notes final.txt" downloads/backup.zip downloads/fonts.zip downloads/README\n--- solution\ncd downloads\nfor f in *.*; do ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; done\n--- hint\nWork inside the folder: `cd downloads`. Then try step 3 by hand: `f=photo-1.jpg`, then `echo "${f##*.}"` should print `jpg`.\n--- hint\nThe loop\'s list is `*.*`, so `README` is left out. Its body is the three commands from step 3, joined by `;`: set `ext`, make the folder with `mkdir -p "$ext"`, then `mv "$f" "$ext/"`.\n--- hint\n`for f in *.*; do ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; done`. Try it first with `echo` in front of `mv`, then run it for real and check with `ls`.\n--- check shell | Every file with an extension is in its folder\ncount downloads/jpg/*.jpg == 12\ncount downloads/pdf/*.pdf == 9\ncount downloads/txt/*.txt == 6\ncount downloads/zip/*.zip == 2\n--- check shell | Nothing with an extension is left loose, and README stayed\ncount downloads/*.* == 0\nfile downloads/README\n--- check shell | Done with a loop\nused for\nused mkdir -p\n\n+++ practice | Survey the inbox\n--- task\nThe folder `inbox` holds files of several kinds. Survey it: with **one** pipeline that starts with `ls inbox`, print how many files there are of each extension, **most first**, like this first line:\n\n```\n4 png\n```\n\nCut each name down to its extension with `sed`, then count.\n--- starter\nmkdir inbox\ntouch inbox/a.png inbox/b.png inbox/c.png inbox/d.png inbox/e.csv inbox/f.csv inbox/g.csv inbox/h.md\n--- solution\nls inbox | sed \'s/.*\\.//\' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n--- hint\n`sed \'s/.*\\.//\'` cuts everything up to the last real dot, leaving the extension.\n--- hint\nThen the counting pipeline from the intermediate course: `sort | uniq -c`, and `sort -rn` to put the biggest count first.\n--- check shell | A count for each extension, most first\nprinted-exactly 4 png\\n3 csv\\n1 md\n--- check shell | Cut with sed, counted with uniq -c\nused sed\nused uniq -c\n--- check shell | Nothing was moved\ncount inbox/* == 8\n\n+++ practice | Sort by the start of the name\n--- task\nThe files in `parts` are named after the part of the ship, then a dash, then more: `pump-01.log`, `fan-notes.txt`, and so on. Go into `parts` with `cd parts`. Then, with **one** `for` loop over `*-*`, move every file into a folder named after the part of its name **before the first dash**: `pump-01.log` goes to `pump/`, `fan-notes.txt` to `fan/`. Make each folder with `mkdir -p`. `README` has no dash, and must stay where it is.\n--- starter\nmkdir parts\ntouch parts/pump-01.log parts/pump-02.log parts/fan-notes.txt parts/fan-01.log parts/valve-test-1.log parts/README\n--- solution\ncd parts\nfor f in *-*; do part="${f%%-*}"; mkdir -p "$part"; mv "$f" "$part/"; done\n--- hint\nTo keep the start, cut from the end. One `%` cuts the shortest match from the end, and `%%` the longest. Which one leaves `valve` from `valve-test-1.log`?\n--- hint\n`${f%%-*}` cuts everything from the first dash to the end.\n--- hint\nThe body has three commands: set `part`, `mkdir -p "$part"`, then `mv "$f" "$part/"`.\n--- check shell | Every file is in its part\'s folder\ncount parts/pump/* == 2\ncount parts/fan/* == 2\nfile parts/valve/valve-test-1.log\n--- check shell | README stayed, and nothing else is loose\nfile parts/README\ncount parts/*-* == 0\nmissing parts/valve-test\n--- check shell | Done with one loop and %%\ncwd parts\nused %%-*\nused mkdir -p\n\n+++ practice | Tidy, then tally\n--- task\nGo into `inbox` with `cd inbox`. Then:\n\n1. With **one** `for` loop, move every file that has an extension into a folder named after that extension, using `${f##*.}` and `mkdir -p`.\n2. With a second `for` loop over the list `csv md png`, write one line per folder into `tally.txt`, in that order: the folder\'s name, a space, and how many files it holds, counted with `ls` and `wc -l`:\n\n```\ncsv 2\nmd 1\npng 3\n```\n\nSend the whole loop\'s output into `tally.txt` with one `>` after `done`. Nothing is loose in `inbox` afterwards except `tally.txt`.\n--- starter\nmkdir inbox\ntouch inbox/a.png inbox/b.png inbox/c.png inbox/d.csv inbox/e.csv inbox/f.md\n--- solution\ncd inbox\nfor f in *.*; do ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; done\nfor d in csv md png; do echo "$d $(ls "$d" | wc -l)"; done > tally.txt\n--- hint\nDo the tidy first, then the tally: if `tally.txt` existed before the tidy, the loop over `*.*` would move it into `txt/`.\n--- hint\nA `>` after `done` catches everything the loop prints: `for d in csv md png; do …; done > tally.txt`.\n--- check shell | The files are tidied\ncount inbox/png/*.png == 3\ncount inbox/csv/*.csv == 2\nfile inbox/md/f.md\n--- check shell | tally.txt counts each folder\nfile inbox/tally.txt ~= csv 2\\nmd 1\\npng 3\n--- check shell | Only tally.txt is loose\ncount inbox/*.* == 1\ncwd inbox\n\n+++ practice | A folder with a dot in its name\n--- task\nGo into `downloads` with `cd downloads`. With **one** `for` loop over `*.*`, move every **file** that has an extension into a folder named after its extension, using `${f##*.}` and `mkdir -p`.\n\nTwo traps:\n\n- `site.old` is a **folder**, not a file, but `*.*` matches it too. It must stay where it is, untouched: use `[ -f "$f" ]` inside the loop so only files are moved.\n- `notes final.txt` has a space in its name.\n\n`backup.tar.gz` goes to `gz/`, and `README` stays where it is.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p downloads/site.old\ntouch downloads/site.old/index.html downloads/photo.jpg "downloads/notes final.txt" downloads/todo.txt downloads/backup.tar.gz downloads/README\n--- solution\ncd downloads\nfor f in *.*; do if [ -f "$f" ]; then ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; fi; done\n--- hint\nPut the three tidy commands inside `if [ -f "$f" ]; then …; fi`, so a folder\'s name is simply skipped.\n--- hint\n`${f##*.}` cuts up to the **last** dot, so `backup.tar.gz` gives `gz`.\n--- check shell | Every file went to its folder\ncount downloads/txt/*.txt == 2\nfile downloads/gz/backup.tar.gz\nfile downloads/jpg/photo.jpg\n--- check shell | The folder site.old was left alone\ndir downloads/site.old\nfile downloads/site.old/index.html\nmissing downloads/old\n--- check shell | README stayed, and a file test was used\nfile downloads/README\nused [ -f\ncwd downloads\n\n+++ practice | Two bugs in the tidy loop\n--- task\nA teammate wrote this loop to tidy `downloads` by extension, but has not run it yet:\n\n```\nfor f in *; do ext="${f#*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; done\n```\n\nA dry run shows two bugs: `README` would be moved into a folder called `README`, and `site.tar.gz` would go into a folder called `tar.gz` instead of `gz`.\n\nGo into `downloads` with `cd downloads`. Do a dry run of your **fixed** loop first (the same loop with `echo` in front of `mv`), then run it for real.\n--- starter\nmkdir downloads\ntouch downloads/site.tar.gz downloads/photo.jpg downloads/plan.txt downloads/README\n--- solution\ncd downloads\nfor f in *.*; do ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; echo mv "$f" "$ext/"; done\nfor f in *.*; do ext="${f##*.}"; mkdir -p "$ext"; mv "$f" "$ext/"; done\n--- hint\nWhich list leaves out names without a dot? And how many `#` signs cut up to the **last** dot?\n--- hint\nLoop over `*.*`, and use `${f##*.}`.\n--- check shell | The dry run showed the right plan\nprinted-line mv site.tar.gz gz/\n--- check shell | Every file is where it belongs\nfile downloads/gz/site.tar.gz\nfile downloads/jpg/photo.jpg\nfile downloads/txt/plan.txt\nmissing downloads/tar.gz\n--- check shell | README stayed loose\nfile downloads/README\nmissing downloads/README/README\n\n+++ practice | Photos by month\n--- task\nThe camera names its photos like `IMG_2026-03-14_001.jpg`: `IMG_`, the date as year-month-day, an underscore and a number. Go into `camera` with `cd camera`.\n\n1. With **one** `for` loop over `IMG_*.jpg`, move every photo into a folder named after its **year and month**: `IMG_2026-03-14_001.jpg` goes to `2026-03/`. Inside the body, first store the name without `IMG_` in a variable, then cut the rest off that. `notes.txt` is not a photo and must stay where it is.\n2. With a second `for` loop over the list `2026-03 2026-04`, print each month and how many photos its folder holds, counted with `ls` and `wc -l`:\n\n```\n2026-03 2\n2026-04 3\n```\n--- starter\nmkdir camera\ntouch camera/IMG_2026-03-14_001.jpg camera/IMG_2026-03-30_002.jpg camera/IMG_2026-04-01_003.jpg camera/IMG_2026-04-01_004.jpg camera/IMG_2026-04-22_005.jpg camera/notes.txt\n--- solution\ncd camera\nfor f in IMG_*.jpg; do d="${f#IMG_}"; month="${d%-*}"; mkdir -p "$month"; mv "$f" "$month/"; done\nfor m in 2026-03 2026-04; do echo "$m $(ls "$m" | wc -l)"; done\n--- hint\nAfter `d="${f#IMG_}"`, `d` holds `2026-03-14_001.jpg`. Cut from the end, up to the **last** dash.\n--- hint\nOne `%` cuts the shortest match from the end: `${d%-*}` cuts `-14_001.jpg` and leaves `2026-03`.\n--- hint\nThe body: `d="${f#IMG_}"; month="${d%-*}"; mkdir -p "$month"; mv "$f" "$month/"`.\n--- check shell | Every photo is in its month\ncount camera/2026-03/*.jpg == 2\ncount camera/2026-04/*.jpg == 3\ncount camera/IMG_* == 0\n--- check shell | The tally is right\nprinted-exactly 2026-03 2\\n2026-04 3\n--- check shell | notes.txt stayed\nfile camera/notes.txt\ncwd camera\n\n=== term3-12a | Stopping a script: exit and standard error\n--- teach\nLast lesson\'s script tidied a folder you had looked at yourself. A tool that other people run has to cope with being used wrongly, and it has to report what went wrong. That takes two small pieces, and this lesson teaches them: a script choosing its own exit status, and a script sending its messages to standard error. Next lesson you put them together into a real tool.\n\nThink of a vending machine that jams. It stops. It shows a code number that the repair person can look up. And it shows a message on its little screen, not in the tray where the snacks come out. A good script does the same three things.\n\n### A script has an exit status too\n\nIn the exit-status lesson, every command left a number: `0` for success, anything else for failure. A script is no different. When it finishes, `$?` holds its exit status. Normally that is [[the status of the last command it ran|last-status]]:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat last.sh\nls missing\n~/project $ bash last.sh\nls: cannot access \'missing\': No such file or directory\n~/project $ echo $?\n2\n```\n\n### exit: stop here, with this number\n\n**`exit`** stops the script straight away. `exit 3` is read "exit with status three": stop now, and finish with status `3`. Any lines after it never run.\n\n```\n~/project $ cat stop.sh\necho "stopping now"\nexit 3\necho "never printed"\n~/project $ bash stop.sh\nstopping now\n~/project $ echo $?\n3\n```\n\nThe script chose its own status. `exit 0` means "stop, and report success". Scripts use [[the numbers by custom|status-meaning]]: `1` for a general failure, and `2` for "you used it wrong".\n\n### >&2: send this to standard error\n\nIn the intermediate course you met a command\'s two outputs: **standard output**, stream 1, for results, and **standard error**, stream 2, for problems. You caught errors with `2>`. Now your script can write its own messages to standard error. Add `>&2` to the end of a command, read "send this to standard error":\n\n```\n~/project $ echo "careful: old data" >&2\ncareful: old data\n```\n\nOn the screen it looks exactly the same. The difference shows when you redirect. `warn.sh` prints a result, and a warning with `>&2`:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat warn.sh\necho "result: 42"\necho "careful: old data" >&2\n~/project $ bash warn.sh > out.txt\ncareful: old data\n~/project $ cat out.txt\nresult: 42\n```\n\nThe `>` caught only standard output, so the file holds only the result. The warning [[stayed on the screen|two-streams]], where a person will see it. It works the other way round too: `bash warn.sh 2> err.txt` puts only the warning into `err.txt`.\n\n**Watch out:** the `&` matters. `echo "oops" >2`, without it, does not send anything to standard error. It makes a file called `2` and writes `oops` into it. `>&2` needs the `&` to say "[[stream number two|ampersand]], not a file called 2".\n\n::: context last-status A script that forgets its failures\nBecause a script normally ends with the status of its last command, an earlier failure can be hidden:\n\n```\n~/project $ cat l2.sh\nls missing\necho done\n~/project $ bash l2.sh\nls: cannot access \'missing\': No such file or directory\ndone\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n```\n\n`ls` failed, but `echo done` worked, so the whole script reports success. Anyone checking `$?` is told all is well. That is why careful scripts stop with their own `exit` the moment something is wrong.\n:::\n\n::: context status-meaning Which number to pick\nOnly `0` has a fixed meaning: success. The rest is custom, but a widely followed one:\n\n- `1` for a general failure, such as a file that is not there;\n- `2` for being used wrongly, such as a missing argument. Bash\'s own built-in commands use `2` this way.\n\nAn exit status is always a whole number from 0 to 255. Write `exit 256` and it wraps round to `0`, which would report success by mistake. So keep to small numbers, and write down what each one means for your tool.\n\nOne more thing: typing `exit` at the prompt, not in a script, closes a real terminal. This practice terminal stays open and tells you so.\n:::\n\n::: context two-streams Results in one place, messages in another\nA script\'s two outputs can go to different places.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 150" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="10" y="50" width="110" height="46" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="65" y="78" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">warn.sh</text>\n  <line x1="120" y1="62" x2="206" y2="34" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="214,31 203,29 207,39" fill="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <text x="150" y="36" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">1</text>\n  <rect x="218" y="14" width="130" height="36" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="283" y="30" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">out.txt</text>\n  <text x="283" y="44" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">result: 42</text>\n  <line x1="120" y1="84" x2="206" y2="112" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="214,115 207,107 203,117" fill="#b4232c"/>\n  <text x="150" y="120" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">2</text>\n  <rect x="218" y="96" width="130" height="36" rx="6" fill="#f2b880" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="283" y="112" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">the screen</text>\n  <text x="283" y="126" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">careful: old data</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nPipes carry only standard output too. So in `bash warn.sh | wc -l`, the warning goes to the screen, and `wc` counts only the one result line. A message sent with `>&2` can never be mixed into data that someone is saving or piping.\n:::\n\n::: context ampersand Why the & is there\nAfter a `>`, the shell expects a file name. The `&` tells it "what follows is a stream number, not a name". So `>&2` means "send standard output to wherever stream 2 is going".\n\nYou have seen the same shape before, the other way round: `2>&1` in the intermediate course meant "send stream 2 wherever stream 1 is going". Both are the shell joining one stream to another. Without the `&`, `>2` and `2>1` are ordinary redirections into files called `2` and `1`.\n:::\n--- task\nBuild a script called `launch.sh` with these three lines, adding them one at a time with `echo \'…\'` (`>` for the first, `>>` for the others):\n\n1. `echo "countdown held" >&2`\n2. `exit 3`\n3. `echo "liftoff"`\n\nThen:\n\n1. Run it with `bash launch.sh`, and print its exit status with `echo $?`. You should see `3`, and never `liftoff`.\n2. Run it again as `bash launch.sh 2> held.txt`, so the message goes into `held.txt` instead of onto the screen.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'echo "countdown held" >&2\' > launch.sh\necho \'exit 3\' >> launch.sh\necho \'echo "liftoff"\' >> launch.sh\nbash launch.sh\necho $?\nbash launch.sh 2> held.txt\n--- hint\nThe first line is `echo \'echo "countdown held" >&2\' > launch.sh`. The single quotes keep `>&2` inside the script, instead of your shell using it now.\n--- hint\nAdd `exit 3` and `echo "liftoff"` with `>>`. `cat launch.sh` should show all three lines.\n--- hint\n`bash launch.sh`, then `echo $?` straight after it. Then `bash launch.sh 2> held.txt`, and `cat held.txt` shows the message.\n--- check shell | The script stops with status 3\nfile launch.sh contains >&2\nfile launch.sh contains exit 3\nprinted-exactly 3\n--- check shell | exit really stopped it\nran bash launch.sh\nnot-printed liftoff\n--- check shell | The message went to standard error, and 2> caught it\nfile held.txt == countdown held\nused 2>\n\n+++ practice | The result in the file, the warning on the screen\n--- task\nBuild a script called `measure.sh` with two lines, added one at a time with `echo \'…\'`:\n\n1. `echo "temperature: 21"`, the result;\n2. `echo "sensor is old"`, a warning, sent to standard error.\n\nThen run it as `bash measure.sh > result.txt`. The file must hold only the result, and the warning must still appear on the screen.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'echo "temperature: 21"\' > measure.sh\necho \'echo "sensor is old" >&2\' >> measure.sh\nbash measure.sh > result.txt\n--- hint\n`>&2` at the end of a command sends its output to standard error. Keep it inside the single quotes, so it goes into the script.\n--- hint\n`>` catches only standard output, so the warning is left on the screen.\n--- check shell | result.txt holds only the result\nfile result.txt == temperature: 21\n--- check shell | The warning reached the screen\nprinted-line sensor is old\n--- check shell | The script sends the warning to standard error\nfile measure.sh contains >&2\nused > result.txt\n\n+++ practice | A script that answers with grep\'s status\n--- task\nBuild a script called `oncrew.sh` with one line: a quiet `grep` that asks whether `crew.txt` contains the script\'s first argument. It has no `exit` of its own, so its status is `grep`\'s.\n\nRun it as `bash oncrew.sh Ada` and print its exit status with `echo $?` (it should be `0`). Then run it as `bash oncrew.sh Yuri` and print its status (it should be `1`).\n--- starter\necho "Ada" > crew.txt\necho "Lin" >> crew.txt\n--- solution\necho \'grep -q "$1" crew.txt\' > oncrew.sh\nbash oncrew.sh Ada\necho $?\nbash oncrew.sh Yuri\necho $?\n--- hint\nA script finishes with the status of the last command it ran, here the `grep -q`.\n--- hint\nThe line is `grep -q "$1" crew.txt`, written into the file with single quotes.\n--- check shell | Ada is on the crew: status 0\nran bash oncrew.sh Ada\nprinted-exactly 0\n--- check shell | Yuri is not: status 1\nran bash oncrew.sh Yuri\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | The script asks grep quietly\nfile oncrew.sh contains grep -q\n\n+++ practice | Stop on errors\n--- task\nBuild a script called `logcheck.sh` with two lines:\n\n1. an `if` that checks quietly whether the file named in the first argument contains `ERROR`; if it does, it prints `errors in ` followed by the file\'s name **to standard error**, and exits with status `1`;\n2. `echo "$1 clean"`.\n\nRun it on `good.log` and print its status (`good.log clean`, then `0`). Then run it on `bad.log` and print its status (`errors in bad.log`, then `1`). The second run must never print `bad.log clean`.\n--- starter\necho "INFO all fine" > good.log\necho "ERROR pump stalled" > bad.log\n--- solution\necho \'if grep -q ERROR "$1"; then echo "errors in $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\' > logcheck.sh\necho \'echo "$1 clean"\' >> logcheck.sh\nbash logcheck.sh good.log\necho $?\nbash logcheck.sh bad.log\necho $?\n--- hint\nThe `then` branch has two commands: the message with `>&2`, then `exit 1`.\n--- hint\nLine 1: `if grep -q ERROR "$1"; then echo "errors in $1" >&2; exit 1; fi`.\n--- check shell | good.log is clean, status 0\nprinted-line good.log clean\nprinted-exactly 0\n--- check shell | bad.log stops with status 1\nprinted-line errors in bad.log\nprinted-exactly 1\nnot-printed bad.log clean\n--- check shell | The message goes to standard error\nfile logcheck.sh contains >&2\nfile logcheck.sh contains exit 1\n\n+++ practice | Keep the warning out of the count\n--- task\nBuild a script called `list.sh` with four lines, added one at a time with `echo \'…\'`:\n\n1. `echo "alpha"`\n2. `echo "bravo"`\n3. `echo "charlie"`\n4. a warning, `echo "list may be old"`, sent to standard error.\n\nThen run `bash list.sh | wc -l`. It must print `3`: the warning must not be counted.\n\nMind the `&`: without it, the fourth line would write the warning into a file called `2`.\n--- starter\n--- solution\necho \'echo "alpha"\' > list.sh\necho \'echo "bravo"\' >> list.sh\necho \'echo "charlie"\' >> list.sh\necho \'echo "list may be old" >&2\' >> list.sh\nbash list.sh | wc -l\n--- hint\nA pipe carries only standard output. A line sent with `>&2` goes straight to the screen instead.\n--- hint\nLine 4 is `echo \'echo "list may be old" >&2\' >> list.sh`.\n--- check shell | Only the three data lines were counted\nprinted-line 3\nprinted-line list may be old\nused | wc -l\n--- check shell | The warning went to standard error\nfile list.sh contains >&2\n--- check shell | No stray file called 2\nmissing 2\n\n+++ practice | The message that never appeared\n--- task\n`config.sh` should print `config missing` on standard error and exit with status `1`. A teammate wrote it like this:\n\n```\necho "config missing" >2\nexit 1\n```\n\nWhen it ran, no message appeared, and a file called `2` turned up.\n\n1. Write `config.sh` again, fixed, with the same two lines.\n2. Delete the stray file `2`.\n3. Run it as `bash config.sh 2> err.txt`, and print its status with `echo $?`. `err.txt` must hold `config missing`, and the status must be `1`.\n--- starter\necho \'echo "config missing" >2\' > config.sh\necho \'exit 1\' >> config.sh\nbash config.sh\n--- solution\necho \'echo "config missing" >&2\' > config.sh\necho \'exit 1\' >> config.sh\nrm 2\nbash config.sh 2> err.txt\necho $?\n--- hint\n`>2` means "into a file called 2". The `&` is what says "stream number two".\n--- hint\nLine 1 becomes `echo "config missing" >&2`. Then `rm 2`.\n--- check shell | The message went to standard error, and 2> caught it\nfile err.txt == config missing\n--- check shell | The status is 1\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | The stray file is gone, and the script is fixed\nmissing 2\nfile config.sh contains >&2\n\n+++ practice | The go/no-go script\n--- task\nBuild a script called `preflight.sh`. It checks three stations in order, `fuel.txt`, `nav.txt` and `comms.txt`, each of which should hold exactly the line `GO`:\n\n- for each station that says `GO`, it prints `fuel go` (or `nav go`, `comms go`) on standard output;\n- at the first station that does not, it prints `fuel no-go` (or `nav no-go`, `comms no-go`) on **standard error**, and stops with status `3` for fuel, `4` for nav, `5` for comms;\n- if all three say `GO`, it finishes by printing `all go`.\n\nWrite it as four lines: one `if` line per station, then the last `echo`.\n\n1. Run it as `bash preflight.sh > poll.txt`, then print its status. `nav.txt` says `NO-GO`, so the status is `4`, `nav no-go` appears on the screen, and `poll.txt` holds only `fuel go`.\n2. Write `GO` into `nav.txt`, and run `bash preflight.sh` again: it prints `fuel go`, `nav go`, `comms go` and `all go`.\n--- starter\necho "GO" > fuel.txt\necho "NO-GO" > nav.txt\necho "GO" > comms.txt\n--- solution\necho \'if grep -q "^GO$" fuel.txt; then echo "fuel go"; else echo "fuel no-go" >&2; exit 3; fi\' > preflight.sh\necho \'if grep -q "^GO$" nav.txt; then echo "nav go"; else echo "nav no-go" >&2; exit 4; fi\' >> preflight.sh\necho \'if grep -q "^GO$" comms.txt; then echo "comms go"; else echo "comms no-go" >&2; exit 5; fi\' >> preflight.sh\necho \'echo "all go"\' >> preflight.sh\nbash preflight.sh > poll.txt\necho $?\necho "GO" > nav.txt\nbash preflight.sh\n--- hint\n`NO-GO` contains `GO`, so match the whole line: `grep -q "^GO$" fuel.txt`.\n--- hint\nEach station\'s line has the shape `if …; then echo "… go"; else echo "… no-go" >&2; exit N; fi`.\n--- hint\nThe first run\'s standard output goes into `poll.txt`, so only `fuel go` lands there; `nav no-go` goes to standard error, which stays on the screen.\n--- check shell | The first run stopped at nav, with status 4\nfile poll.txt == fuel go\nprinted-line nav no-go\nprinted-exactly 4\n--- check shell | After the fix, all go\nfile nav.txt == GO\nlast-printed-exactly fuel go\\nnav go\\ncomms go\\nall go\n--- check shell | Stops with its own statuses, messages on standard error\nfile preflight.sh contains exit 4\nfile preflight.sh contains >&2\n\n=== term3-12 | Build a tool: a script that checks its input\n--- teach\nLast lesson a script learned to stop with `exit` and to send its messages to standard error with `>&2`. Now you put those to work in a tool that other people could run. It checks what it was given before doing anything, and says in plain words what went wrong.\n\nThink of a pilot before take-off. They do not start the engines and hope. They go down a checklist first: fuel, doors, controls. If one item fails, they stop right there and say which one. A careful script starts the same way.\n\n### Guard clauses: check first, then work\n\nA **guard clause** is a check at the top of a script that stops it, with a message, if something is wrong. You met the idea at the end of the `[ … ]` lesson. Like a [[guard at a gate|guard-word]], it lets the script go on only if everything is in order. Two checks come up again and again: was an argument given at all, and does the file it names exist?\n\n### Check 1: was anything given?\n\n`$#`, from the scripts lesson, is how many arguments the script was given. `[ $# -eq 0 ]` asks "is the number of arguments equal to zero?":\n\n```\nif [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: count.sh FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\n```\n\nRead it aloud: "if there are no arguments, then send a usage message to standard error, and exit with status 2." A **usage message** is one line that shows how the tool should be used. `FILE` in capitals is [[a placeholder|usage-line]]: it means "put a file name here". Status `2` is the custom for "you used it wrong".\n\n### Check 2: does the file exist?\n\n```\nif [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such file: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\n```\n\n"If there is **not** a file called whatever `$1` holds, then say so on standard error, and exit with status 1." The `!` flips the question, as in the `[ … ]` lesson, and the quotes keep a name with spaces in one piece.\n\n### The work: wc -l < "$1"\n\nOnly after both checks does the script do its job. Here the job is to count the lines of the file:\n\n```\nwc -l < "$1"\n```\n\n`<` is read "from". It is the opposite of `>`. `>` sends a command\'s output **into** a file; `<` [[feeds a file into|input-redirect]] a command\'s standard input. It makes a difference to what `wc` prints:\n\n```\n~/project $ wc -l app.log\n5 app.log\n~/project $ wc -l < app.log\n5\n```\n\nGiven the file name as an argument, `wc` prints the name after the count. Fed the file on its standard input, it [[never learns the name|no-name]], so it prints only the number. That is handy in a tool whose answer should be one clean number.\n\nAny command can do the real work. `grep -c ERROR "$1"`, from the intermediate course, would print how many lines of the file contain `ERROR`.\n\n### The whole tool\n\nHere are the three lines together, in order: the guards first, then the work.\n\n```\n~/project $ cat count.sh\nif [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: count.sh FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\nif [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such file: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\nwc -l < "$1"\n```\n\n### Test every path\n\nHere **path** has a new meaning: not a file\'s address, but one of the routes a script can take through its lines. The **happy path** is the route where everything is right. Testing only that one is not enough, so run the tool once down [[every path|every-path]], and check `echo $?` after each:\n\n```\n~/project $ bash count.sh\nusage: count.sh FILE\n~/project $ echo $?\n2\n~/project $ bash count.sh nope.txt\nno such file: nope.txt\n~/project $ echo $?\n1\n~/project $ bash count.sh app.log\n5\n~/project $ echo $?\n0\n```\n\nEach route gives its own message and its own status. Another script can now [[react to each one|callers]].\n\n**Watch out:** a guard needs its `exit`. Leave it out, as in `then echo "usage: count.sh FILE" >&2; fi`, and the script prints the usage message and then carries on anyway. The next line runs with an empty `$1`, and fails with a confusing error about a file with no name.\n\n::: context guard-word Why "guard"\nA guard at a gate checks each visitor before they come in, so nobody has to deal with trouble later, deep inside the building. A guard clause does the same for a script: bad input is turned away at the first lines, before any file has been moved or changed.\n\nEngineers call this **failing early**. A script that stops at line 1 saying `no such file: data.csv` is far easier to fix than one that gets halfway through its work and then breaks in a way that points at nothing useful.\n:::\n\n::: context usage-line How usage messages are written\nCommand-line tools share a way of writing usage lines, so you can read one you have never seen before. The tool\'s name comes first. Words in capitals are placeholders that you replace. Parts in square brackets are optional.\n\n```\nusage: count.sh FILE\nusage: backup.sh FILE [FOLDER]\n```\n\nThe second means "a file, and a folder if you want". Many real tools print their usage line when you run them with `--help`, or when you run them wrongly, as this one does.\n:::\n\n::: context input-redirect Output out, input in\n`>` and `<` point the way the text flows: out of the command into a file, or out of a file into the command.\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 130" font-family="Inter, Arial, sans-serif">\n  <rect x="20" y="14" width="96" height="32" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="68" y="35" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">ls</text>\n  <line x1="118" y1="30" x2="228" y2="30" stroke="#1d6fd1" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="236,30 226,25 226,35" fill="#1d6fd1"/>\n  <text x="176" y="24" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1d6fd1">&gt;</text>\n  <rect x="240" y="14" width="100" height="32" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="290" y="35" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">list.txt</text>\n  <rect x="20" y="74" width="96" height="32" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="68" y="95" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">wc -l</text>\n  <line x1="238" y1="90" x2="128" y2="90" stroke="#b4232c" stroke-width="2"/>\n  <polygon points="120,90 130,85 130,95" fill="#b4232c"/>\n  <text x="180" y="84" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b4232c">&lt;</text>\n  <rect x="240" y="74" width="100" height="32" rx="6" fill="#8fb8f0" stroke="#1f2a44" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n  <text x="290" y="95" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2a44">app.log</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThe arrow shape of each sign is a good reminder: it points the way the text travels.\n:::\n\n::: context no-name Why the name disappears\nWhen you write `wc -l app.log`, `wc` opens the file itself, so it knows the name and prints it. With `wc -l < app.log`, the shell opens the file and connects it to `wc`\'s standard input. All `wc` sees is lines arriving, the same as from a pipe, with no name attached.\n\n`cat app.log | wc -l` gives the same bare number, but it starts an extra program, `cat`, to do a job the shell can do alone. That is why scripts tend to use `<`.\n:::\n\n::: context every-path Testing the unhappy paths\nFlight software teams spend a large share of their testing on things going wrong: a sensor that sends nonsense, a file that is missing, a message that arrives twice. They cause these faults on purpose, which is called **fault injection**, to prove that the error handling really works before it is ever needed.\n\nYour three test runs are the same idea at a small size. The no-argument and missing-file paths are exactly the ones nobody tries by accident until the day they matter.\n:::\n\n::: context callers Other programs read the status\nBecause each path ends with its own status, another script can decide what to do without reading any messages:\n\n```\nif bash count.sh app.log > /dev/null; then echo "log is readable"; fi\n```\n\nThe `> /dev/null` throws the count away; only the status is used. Build systems and CI pipelines work this way. They run tool after tool and read each exit status, and that only works if every tool, including the ones you write, reports honestly with `exit`.\n:::\n--- task\nWrite a script called `errors.sh`, which takes the name of a log file as its first argument. Build it line by line with `echo \'…\'`, like your earlier scripts:\n\n- with **no argument**, it prints `usage: errors.sh LOGFILE` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- if the file **does not exist**, it prints `no such log: ` followed by the name, such as `no such log: missing.log`, to standard error, and exits with status `1`;\n- otherwise it prints the **number of lines** in the file that contain `ERROR`.\n\nThen test every path: run it with no argument, then with `missing.log`, then with `app.log`. After each of the first two runs, print its exit status with `echo $?`.\n--- starter\necho "INFO start" > app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "INFO retry" >> app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "ERROR network" >> app.log\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: errors.sh LOGFILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\' > errors.sh\necho \'if [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such log: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\' >> errors.sh\necho \'grep -c ERROR "$1"\' >> errors.sh\nbash errors.sh\necho $?\nbash errors.sh missing.log\necho $?\nbash errors.sh app.log\n--- hint\nFollow `count.sh` from the explanation: two guard clauses, then the work. Line 1 checks `$#`: `if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: errors.sh LOGFILE" >&2; exit 2; fi`. Write it into the file with `echo \'…\' > errors.sh`.\n--- hint\nLine 2 checks the file: `if [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such log: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi`, added with `>>`. Line 3 does the work: `grep -c ERROR "$1"`.\n--- hint\nTest each path: `bash errors.sh`, then `echo $?` (it should print `2`); `bash errors.sh missing.log`, then `echo $?` (`1`); then `bash errors.sh app.log`, which should print `3`.\n--- check shell | With no argument: usage, and status 2\nprinted-line usage: errors.sh LOGFILE\nprinted-exactly 2\n--- check shell | With a missing file: a clear error, and status 1\nprinted-line no such log: missing.log\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | With a real log: the number of ERROR lines\nprinted-exactly 3\nfile errors.sh contains exit 2\nfile errors.sh contains >&2\n\n+++ practice | The first line, or how to use it\n--- task\nWrite a script called `first.sh`, built line by line with `echo \'…\'`, which takes a file name as its first argument:\n\n- with **no argument**, it prints `usage: first.sh FILE` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- otherwise it prints the **first line** of the file, with `head`.\n\nTest both paths: run `bash first.sh` and print its status with `echo $?`, then run `bash first.sh plan.txt`.\n--- starter\necho "burn at T+120" > plan.txt\necho "coast to apogee" >> plan.txt\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: first.sh FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\' > first.sh\necho \'head -n 1 "$1"\' >> first.sh\nbash first.sh\necho $?\nbash first.sh plan.txt\n--- hint\nThe guard asks `[ $# -eq 0 ]`: were no arguments given? Its branch prints the usage line with `>&2`, then `exit 2`.\n--- hint\nThe work is `head -n 1 "$1"`.\n--- check shell | With no argument: usage, and status 2\nprinted-line usage: first.sh FILE\nprinted-exactly 2\n--- check shell | With a file: its first line\nprinted-exactly burn at T+120\n--- check shell | The guard really stops the script\nfile first.sh contains exit 2\nfile first.sh contains >&2\n\n+++ practice | A folder, not a file\n--- task\nWrite a script called `howmany.sh`, built line by line with `echo \'…\'`, which takes the name of a folder as its first argument:\n\n- if there is **no folder** by that name, it prints `not a folder: ` followed by the name, such as `not a folder: notes.txt`, to standard error, and exits with status `1`;\n- otherwise it prints how many things the folder holds, as a single number, with `ls` and `wc -l`.\n\nTest both paths: run it on `notes.txt` (a file, not a folder) and print its status, then run it on `photos`.\n--- starter\nmkdir photos\ntouch photos/a.jpg photos/b.jpg photos/c.jpg notes.txt\n--- solution\necho \'if [ ! -d "$1" ]; then echo "not a folder: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\' > howmany.sh\necho \'ls "$1" | wc -l\' >> howmany.sh\nbash howmany.sh notes.txt\necho $?\nbash howmany.sh photos\n--- hint\n`-d` asks "is there a folder with this name?", and `!` turns the question around.\n--- hint\nLine 1: `if [ ! -d "$1" ]; then echo "not a folder: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi`. Line 2: `ls "$1" | wc -l`.\n--- check shell | A file is refused, with status 1\nprinted-line not a folder: notes.txt\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | The folder\'s contents were counted\nprinted-exactly 3\n--- check shell | The guard asks about a folder\nfile howmany.sh contains -d\nfile howmany.sh contains exit 1\n\n+++ practice | Line counts, skipping what is missing\n--- task\nWrite a script called `sizes.sh`, built line by line with `echo \'…\'`, which takes any number of file names:\n\n- with **no arguments**, it prints `usage: sizes.sh FILE...` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- otherwise, for each file in turn: if it does not exist, it prints `skip: ` and the name to standard error and goes on to the next; if it does, it prints the name, a colon and its number of lines, like `a.txt: 3 lines`, counting with `wc -l <`.\n\nRun it with no arguments and print its status. Then run `bash sizes.sh a.txt missing.txt b.txt`.\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "a $i" >> a.txt; done\nfor i in 1 2; do echo "b $i" >> b.txt; done\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: sizes.sh FILE..." >&2; exit 2; fi\' > sizes.sh\necho \'for f in "$@"; do if [ -f "$f" ]; then echo "$f: $(wc -l < "$f") lines"; else echo "skip: $f" >&2; fi; done\' >> sizes.sh\nbash sizes.sh\necho $?\nbash sizes.sh a.txt missing.txt b.txt\n--- hint\nThe second line is a `for` loop over `"$@"` with an `if` inside it. In this tool a missing file is skipped, not a reason to stop, so its branch has no `exit`.\n--- hint\n`$(wc -l < "$f")` is the bare number of lines, with no file name after it.\n--- check shell | No arguments: usage, and status 2\nprinted-line usage: sizes.sh FILE...\nprinted-exactly 2\n--- check shell | Every file reported, the missing one skipped\nprinted-exactly a.txt: 3 lines\\nskip: missing.txt\\nb.txt: 2 lines\n--- check shell | Counted from standard input\nfile sizes.sh contains wc -l <\n\n+++ practice | Exactly two arguments\n--- task\nWrite a script called `copyto.sh`, built line by line with `echo \'…\'`, which copies a file into a folder: `copyto.sh FILE FOLDER`.\n\n- unless it is given **exactly two** arguments, it prints `usage: copyto.sh FILE FOLDER` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- if the file does not exist, it prints `no such file: ` and the name to standard error, and exits with `1`;\n- if the folder does not exist, it prints `no such folder: ` and the name to standard error, and exits with `1`;\n- otherwise it copies the file into the folder.\n\nTest it with these four runs:\n\n1. `bash copyto.sh notes.txt backup extra` (three arguments: refused, nothing copied);\n2. `bash copyto.sh notes.txt nowhere`;\n3. `bash copyto.sh "flight plan.txt" backup`;\n4. `bash copyto.sh ghost.txt backup`.\n--- starter\nmkdir backup\necho "go" > notes.txt\necho "burn at T+120" > "flight plan.txt"\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -ne 2 ]; then echo "usage: copyto.sh FILE FOLDER" >&2; exit 2; fi\' > copyto.sh\necho \'if [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such file: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\' >> copyto.sh\necho \'if [ ! -d "$2" ]; then echo "no such folder: $2" >&2; exit 1; fi\' >> copyto.sh\necho \'cp "$1" "$2/"\' >> copyto.sh\nbash copyto.sh notes.txt backup extra\nbash copyto.sh notes.txt nowhere\nbash copyto.sh "flight plan.txt" backup\nbash copyto.sh ghost.txt backup\n--- hint\n"Exactly two" is `[ $# -ne 2 ]` for the refusal: the number of arguments is not equal to 2. Checking only for zero would let three arguments through.\n--- hint\nQuote `"$1"` and `"$2"` everywhere, so `flight plan.txt` stays one name.\n--- check shell | Three arguments were refused, and nothing was copied\nprinted-line usage: copyto.sh FILE FOLDER\nmissing backup/notes.txt\n--- check shell | A missing folder and a missing file were reported\nprinted-line no such folder: nowhere\nprinted-line no such file: ghost.txt\n--- check shell | The file with a space was copied\ncount backup/flight?plan.txt == 1\ncount backup/* == 1\n\n+++ practice | The guard that let it through\n--- task\n`top.sh` should print the first three lines of a file. A teammate wrote it like this:\n\n```\nif [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: top.sh FILE" >&2; fi\nhead -n 3 "$1"\n```\n\nRun with no argument, it prints the usage line, then an error from `head`, and finishes with status `1`. Write `top.sh` again, fixed, so that with no argument it prints **only** the usage line and exits with status `2`. Test it: run `bash top.sh` and print its status, then run `bash top.sh log.txt`.\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do echo "line $i" >> log.txt; done\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: top.sh FILE" >&2; fi\' > top.sh\necho \'head -n 3 "$1"\' >> top.sh\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: top.sh FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\' > top.sh\necho \'head -n 3 "$1"\' >> top.sh\nbash top.sh\necho $?\nbash top.sh log.txt\n--- hint\nPrinting the usage message does not stop a script. What command does?\n--- hint\nAdd `exit 2` to the guard\'s branch: `…>&2; exit 2; fi`.\n--- check shell | With no argument: only the usage line\nprinted-exactly usage: top.sh FILE\n--- check shell | …and status 2\nprinted-exactly 2\nfile top.sh contains exit 2\n--- check shell | With a file: its first three lines\nprinted-exactly line 1\\nline 2\\nline 3\n\n+++ practice | A pattern counter with every path tested\n--- task\nWrite a script called `grepcount.sh`, built line by line with `echo \'…\'`, used as `grepcount.sh PATTERN FILE`:\n\n- unless it is given exactly two arguments, it prints `usage: grepcount.sh PATTERN FILE` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- if the file does not exist, it prints `no such file: ` and the name to standard error, and exits with status `1`;\n- otherwise it prints how many lines of the file contain the pattern, in a sentence like `3 lines match ERROR`.\n\nThen test every path, printing the status with `echo $?` after each of the first two:\n\n1. `bash grepcount.sh ERROR` (one argument);\n2. `bash grepcount.sh ERROR nope.log`;\n3. `bash grepcount.sh ERROR app.log`;\n4. `bash grepcount.sh PANIC app.log`, which must print `0 lines match PANIC`.\n--- starter\necho "INFO start" > app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "ERROR disk" >> app.log\necho "INFO retry" >> app.log\necho "ERROR network" >> app.log\n--- solution\necho \'if [ $# -ne 2 ]; then echo "usage: grepcount.sh PATTERN FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\' > grepcount.sh\necho \'if [ ! -f "$2" ]; then echo "no such file: $2" >&2; exit 1; fi\' >> grepcount.sh\necho \'echo "$(grep -c "$1" "$2") lines match $1"\' >> grepcount.sh\nbash grepcount.sh ERROR\necho $?\nbash grepcount.sh ERROR nope.log\necho $?\nbash grepcount.sh ERROR app.log\nbash grepcount.sh PANIC app.log\n--- hint\nHere the file is the **second** argument, so the file check uses `"$2"`.\n--- hint\n`grep -c` prints `0` when nothing matches, so the sentence still comes out right. Put it inside `$(…)` in an `echo`.\n--- hint\nLine 3: `echo "$(grep -c "$1" "$2") lines match $1"`.\n--- check shell | Wrong use: usage, and status 2\nprinted-line usage: grepcount.sh PATTERN FILE\nprinted-exactly 2\n--- check shell | A missing file: a clear error, and status 1\nprinted-line no such file: nope.log\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | The counts, zero included\nprinted-exactly 3 lines match ERROR\nprinted-exactly 0 lines match PANIC\n\n=== term3-gate | The command line, advanced: mastery gate\n--- teach\nThis gate covers the whole course: variables and quoting, exporting to the programs you run, `$(…)`, exit statuses with `&&`, `||` and `if`, questions with `[ … ]`, `for` loops and cutting names with `%` and `#`, scripts with arguments, `#!`, `chmod +x` and `./`, `sed`, `xargs` and `find -exec`, debugging with `bash -x` and `set -u`, and tools that guard their input with `exit` and `>&2`. Most problems mix several of these. There are 10 problems and 12 questions in 112 minutes. You pass with 7 problems and 10 questions right, and there are no hints. To get ready, redo the practice problems of the lessons that felt hard without opening their hints, check every script you write with `cat`, and test every path through it.\n--- gate\npass 7\nquestions 10\nminutes 112\n\n+++ problem | Deploy with the right settings\n--- task\n`VERSION` holds the version number of the release, and `deploy.sh` prints `deploying ` followed by `$APP_VERSION`, ` to ` and `$TARGET`.\n\n1. Set `APP_VERSION` to the contents of `VERSION` and export it, without typing the version number yourself.\n2. Run `bash deploy.sh` with `TARGET` set to `staging` for that one run only. It prints `deploying 4.2.1 to staging`.\n3. Export `TARGET` as `prod`, and run `bash deploy.sh` again. It prints `deploying 4.2.1 to prod`.\n4. Write the line `last deploy: 4.2.1 to prod by you` into `deploy.log`, with every changing part filled in from a variable or `$(whoami)`.\n--- starter\necho "4.2.1" > VERSION\necho \'echo "deploying $APP_VERSION to $TARGET"\' > deploy.sh\n--- solution\nexport APP_VERSION=$(cat VERSION)\nTARGET=staging bash deploy.sh\nexport TARGET=prod\nbash deploy.sh\necho "last deploy: $APP_VERSION to $TARGET by $(whoami)" > deploy.log\n--- check shell | The version came from the file, exported\nenv APP_VERSION == 4.2.1\nused $(cat VERSION)\n--- check shell | The staging run, for one run only\nprinted-line deploying 4.2.1 to staging\nused TARGET=staging bash\n--- check shell | The production run\nprinted-line deploying 4.2.1 to prod\nenv TARGET == prod\n--- check shell | deploy.log was written from the variables\nfile deploy.log == last deploy: 4.2.1 to prod by you\nused $(whoami)\n\n+++ problem | Rename, but never overwrite\n--- task\nThe camera saved its pictures with the ending `.JPG`. Rename every `.JPG` file so it ends in `.jpg` instead (`moon.JPG` becomes `moon.jpg`), with **one** `for` loop.\n\nBut `mars.jpg` already exists, and it is a different picture from `mars.JPG`. A file must never be renamed onto a name that is already taken: when the new name exists, leave the `.JPG` file as it is and print `skip ` followed by its name, like `skip mars.JPG`. One picture has a space in its name.\n--- starter\necho "red one" > mars.JPG\necho "red two" > mars.jpg\necho "grey" > moon.JPG\ntouch "deep sky.JPG"\n--- solution\nfor f in *.JPG; do new="${f%.JPG}.jpg"; if [ -e "$new" ]; then echo "skip $f"; else mv "$f" "$new"; fi; done\n--- check shell | The free names were taken\nfile moon.jpg == grey\ncount deep?sky.jpg == 1\n--- check shell | mars.jpg was not overwritten, and mars.JPG was skipped\nfile mars.jpg == red two\nfile mars.JPG == red one\nprinted-line skip mars.JPG\n--- check shell | Only the skipped file still ends in .JPG\ncount *.JPG == 1\ncount *.jpg == 3\n\n+++ problem | Point the configs at the new server\n--- task\nThe database moved from `db.old.lan` to `db.new.lan`. The settings files end in `.conf` and live at any depth under `etc`; one of them has a space in its name, and some lines mention the old server twice.\n\n1. In every `.conf` file under `etc`, change **every** `db.old.lan` to `db.new.lan`, in the files themselves, with one `find` command. `etc/notes.txt` also mentions the old server, and must not be changed.\n2. Print how many lines under `etc` still mention `db.old.lan`, as a single number, with one pipeline.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p etc/app etc/web\necho "host=db.old.lan" > etc/app/db.conf\necho "replica=db.old.lan:5433,db.old.lan:5434" >> etc/app/db.conf\necho "upstream=db.old.lan" > "etc/web/main site.conf"\necho "port=443" > etc/web/tls.conf\necho "we moved off db.old.lan in May" > etc/notes.txt\n--- solution\nfind etc -name "*.conf" -exec sed -i \'s/db.old.lan/db.new.lan/g\' {} \\;\ngrep -r db.old.lan etc | wc -l\n--- check shell | db.conf points at the new server everywhere\nfile etc/app/db.conf ~= host=db.new.lan\\nreplica=db.new.lan:5433,db.new.lan:5434\n--- check shell | The file with a space was changed too, and the others kept\nfile etc/web/tls.conf == port=443\nfile etc/notes.txt == we moved off db.old.lan in May\ncount etc/web/*.conf == 2\n--- check shell | Only the note still mentions the old server\nprinted-exactly 1\nused -exec sed -i\n\n+++ problem | A backup tool you run by name\n--- task\nCreate an executable script called `save.sh`, starting with the shebang line `#!/bin/bash`, used as `save.sh FILE`:\n\n- with **no argument**, it prints `usage: save.sh FILE` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- if the file does not exist, it prints `no such file: ` and the name to standard error, and exits with status `1`;\n- otherwise it makes the folder `backups` if it is not there yet, copies the file into it with `.bak` added to its name, and prints `saved backups/` and the new name, like `saved backups/crew.txt.bak`.\n\nThen test it, always running it as `./save.sh`:\n\n1. with no argument, then print its status;\n2. with `ghost.txt`, then print its status;\n3. with `"flight plan.txt"`;\n4. as one line that runs `./save.sh crew.txt` and, only if it worked, prints `crew saved`.\n--- starter\necho "burn at T+120" > "flight plan.txt"\necho "ada lin sam" > crew.txt\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > save.sh\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: save.sh FILE" >&2; exit 2; fi\' >> save.sh\necho \'if [ ! -f "$1" ]; then echo "no such file: $1" >&2; exit 1; fi\' >> save.sh\necho \'mkdir -p backups\' >> save.sh\necho \'cp "$1" "backups/$1.bak"\' >> save.sh\necho \'echo "saved backups/$1.bak"\' >> save.sh\nchmod +x save.sh\n./save.sh\necho $?\n./save.sh ghost.txt\necho $?\n./save.sh "flight plan.txt"\n./save.sh crew.txt && echo "crew saved"\n--- check shell | A proper executable script\nfile save.sh contains #!/bin/bash\nfile save.sh exec\nran ./save.sh\n--- check shell | Wrong use and a missing file, each with its status\nprinted-line usage: save.sh FILE\nprinted-exactly 2\nprinted-line no such file: ghost.txt\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | Both backups were made, one with a space in its name\ncount backups/flight?plan.txt.bak == 1\nfile backups/crew.txt.bak == ada lin sam\nprinted-line saved backups/flight plan.txt.bak\n--- check shell | A caller used its status\nprinted-line crew saved\nused ./save.sh crew.txt &&\n\n+++ problem | Debug the nightly job\n--- task\n`nightly.sh` should copy every `.csv` report from `reports` into `archive`, and then print `archived 3 of 3 reports`. It holds:\n\n```\nset -u\nsrc=reports\ndest=archive\nmkdir -p "$dest"\nfor f in "$src"/*.csv; do cp $f "$dest/"; done\necho "archived $(ls "$dest" | wc -l) of $(ls "$scr"/*.csv | wc -l) reports"\n```\n\nRunning it prints errors, and one report never reaches `archive`. It has **two** bugs. Find them, write `nightly.sh` again with both fixed (all six lines, built with `echo \'…\'`), and run it with `bash nightly.sh` so it prints `archived 3 of 3 reports`.\n--- starter\nmkdir reports\necho "q1" > reports/q1.csv\necho "q2" > reports/q2.csv\necho "q3" > "reports/q3 final.csv"\necho \'set -u\' > nightly.sh\necho \'src=reports\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'dest=archive\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "$dest"\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'for f in "$src"/*.csv; do cp $f "$dest/"; done\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'echo "archived $(ls "$dest" | wc -l) of $(ls "$scr"/*.csv | wc -l) reports"\' >> nightly.sh\n--- solution\nbash -x nightly.sh\necho \'set -u\' > nightly.sh\necho \'src=reports\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'dest=archive\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'mkdir -p "$dest"\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'for f in "$src"/*.csv; do cp "$f" "$dest/"; done\' >> nightly.sh\necho \'echo "archived $(ls "$dest" | wc -l) of $(ls "$src"/*.csv | wc -l) reports"\' >> nightly.sh\nbash nightly.sh\n--- check shell | Every report is archived\ncount archive/*.csv == 3\ncount archive/q3?final.csv == 1\n--- check shell | The job reports it\nprinted-line archived 3 of 3 reports\n--- check shell | Both bugs fixed, set -u kept\nfile nightly.sh excludes $scr\nfile nightly.sh contains cp "$f"\nfile nightly.sh contains set -u\n\n+++ problem | Clear the caches\n--- task\nUnder `build` are cache **files** ending in `.cache`, at several depths; one has a space in its name. There is also a **folder** called `img.cache`, which holds `logo.png`: it is not a cache file, and it and its contents must stay.\n\n1. Delete every cache file under `build`, with one `find` command that deletes each file itself.\n2. Print how many files are left under `build`, as a single number, with one pipeline that starts with `find`.\n--- starter\nmkdir -p build/js build/img.cache build/css/old\ntouch build/a.cache "build/js/app bundle.cache" build/css/old/b.cache build/js/app.js build/css/site.css build/img.cache/logo.png\n--- solution\nfind build -type f -name "*.cache" -exec rm {} \\;\nfind build -type f | wc -l\n--- check shell | Every cache file is gone\nmissing build/a.cache\nmissing build/css/old/b.cache\ncount build/js/* == 1\n--- check shell | The folder img.cache and everything else stayed\ndir build/img.cache\nfile build/img.cache/logo.png\nfile build/js/app.js\nfile build/css/site.css\n--- check shell | Three files are left\nprinted-exactly 3\nused -exec rm\n\n+++ problem | Archive the big logs\n--- task\nMake a folder called `big`. Then, with **one** `for` loop over `*.log`, move every log with **more than 5** lines into `big`. A log with exactly 5 lines is not big. One log has a space in its name.\n\nFinally, save the names of the logs now in `big` into `big.txt`, one per line, in alphabetical order.\n--- starter\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do echo "a $i" >> a.log; done\nfor i in 1 2; do echo "b $i" >> b.log; done\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do echo "c $i" >> "c d.log"; done\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do echo "e $i" >> e.log; done\n--- solution\nmkdir big\nfor f in *.log; do if [ "$(cat "$f" | wc -l)" -gt 5 ]; then mv "$f" big/; fi; done\nls big > big.txt\n--- check shell | The two big logs were moved\ncount big/*.log == 2\nfile big/a.log\ncount big/c?d.log == 1\n--- check shell | The others stayed, the 5-line one included\nfile b.log\nfile e.log\n--- check shell | big.txt lists them in order\nfile big.txt ~= a.log\\nc d.log\n\n+++ problem | Validate every config\n--- task\nA config file is valid only if its **first line** is exactly `#CONFIG v2`.\n\n1. Write a script called `valid.sh` that takes a file name as its first argument, prints nothing at all, and finishes with exit status `0` if that file is valid and `1` if it is not.\n2. With **one** `for` loop over `*.cfg` that uses `bash valid.sh` as the condition of an `if`, print one line per file, `NAME ok` or `NAME bad`:\n\n```\na.cfg ok\nb.cfg bad\nc d.cfg ok\ne.cfg bad\n```\n--- starter\necho "#CONFIG v2" > a.cfg\necho "mode=flight" >> a.cfg\necho "#CONFIG v1" > b.cfg\necho "#CONFIG v2" > "c d.cfg"\necho "# notes first" > e.cfg\necho "#CONFIG v2" >> e.cfg\n--- solution\necho \'head -n 1 "$1" | grep -q "^#CONFIG v2$"\' > valid.sh\nfor f in *.cfg; do if bash valid.sh "$f"; then echo "$f ok"; else echo "$f bad"; fi; done\n--- check shell | Every config judged by its first line\nprinted-exactly a.cfg ok\\nb.cfg bad\\nc d.cfg ok\\ne.cfg bad\n--- check shell | valid.sh answers with its status\nfile valid.sh contains $1\nused if bash valid.sh\n--- check shell | The configs were not changed\nfile e.cfg lines == 2\nfile a.cfg lines == 2\n\n+++ problem | Move the most common kind\n--- task\nGo into `inbox` with `cd inbox`.\n\n1. Store the **most common extension** in the folder in a variable called `top`, with one pipeline inside `$(…)`: survey the extensions, count them, keep the top line, and cut it down to the extension alone.\n2. Make a folder named after `$top`, and move every file with that extension into it.\n3. Print the line `moved 5 csv files`, where `5` is counted from the new folder with `ls` and `wc -l`, and `csv` comes from `$top`.\n--- starter\nmkdir inbox\ntouch inbox/a.csv inbox/b.csv inbox/c.csv inbox/d.csv inbox/e.csv inbox/f.png inbox/g.png inbox/h.png inbox/i.txt inbox/j.txt\n--- solution\ncd inbox\ntop=$(ls | sed \'s/.*\\.//\' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -n 1 | sed \'s/.* //\')\nmkdir "$top"\nmv *."$top" "$top/"\necho "moved $(ls "$top" | wc -l) $top files"\n--- check shell | top holds the most common extension\nvar top == csv\nused top=$(\n--- check shell | Every csv file was moved\ncount inbox/csv/*.csv == 5\ncount inbox/*.csv == 0\ncount inbox/*.png == 3\n--- check shell | The count was printed from the folder\nprinted-line moved 5 csv files\ncwd inbox\n\n+++ problem | The mission report tool\n--- task\nCreate an executable script called `report.sh`, starting with the shebang line, used as `report.sh LOG...`:\n\n- with **no arguments**, it prints `usage: report.sh LOG...` to standard error, and exits with status `2`;\n- for each log in turn: if it exists, it prints its name and how many of its lines contain `ERROR`, like `a.log: 2 errors`; if it does not, it prints `missing: ` and the name to standard error, and goes on to the next;\n- then it prints `checked ` and how many logs it was given, like `checked 2 logs`;\n- it finishes with status `1` if any log was missing, and `0` if none was.\n\nTest it, running it as `./report.sh` and printing its status with `echo $?` after each run:\n\n1. with no arguments;\n2. with `a.log "b c.log"`;\n3. with `a.log gone.log`.\n--- starter\necho "ERROR disk" > a.log\necho "INFO ok" >> a.log\necho "ERROR net" >> a.log\necho "INFO ok" > "b c.log"\n--- solution\necho \'#!/bin/bash\' > report.sh\necho \'if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then echo "usage: report.sh LOG..." >&2; exit 2; fi\' >> report.sh\necho \'status=0\' >> report.sh\necho \'for f in "$@"; do if [ -f "$f" ]; then echo "$f: $(grep -c ERROR "$f") errors"; else echo "missing: $f" >&2; status=1; fi; done\' >> report.sh\necho \'echo "checked $# logs"\' >> report.sh\necho \'exit $status\' >> report.sh\nchmod +x report.sh\n./report.sh\necho $?\n./report.sh a.log "b c.log"\necho $?\n./report.sh a.log gone.log\necho $?\n--- check shell | No arguments: usage, and status 2\nprinted-line usage: report.sh LOG...\nprinted-exactly 2\n--- check shell | Two real logs: a report for each, and status 0\nprinted-exactly a.log: 2 errors\\nb c.log: 0 errors\\nchecked 2 logs\nprinted-exactly 0\n--- check shell | A missing log is reported, and the status is 1\nprinted-exactly a.log: 2 errors\\nmissing: gone.log\\nchecked 2 logs\nprinted-exactly 1\n--- check shell | A proper executable script\nfile report.sh contains #!/bin/bash\nfile report.sh exec\nran ./report.sh\n\n+++ question | Quotes and braces\n--- ask\nWhat does the last line print?\n\n```\nplanet=mars\necho \'$planet\' "${planet}s" "$planets"\n```\n--- answer\n$planet marss\n--- why\nSingle quotes keep `$planet` exactly as typed. `${planet}s` is the value of `planet` followed by an `s`: `marss`. `$planets` looks up a different variable, `planets`, which was never set, so it is empty and adds nothing you can see.\n\n+++ question | Cutting both ends\n--- ask\nWhat does the last line print?\n\n```\nf=backup.tar.gz\necho "${f%.*} ${f##*.} ${f%%.*}"\n```\n--- answer\nbackup.tar gz backup\n--- why\n`%` cuts the shortest match of `.*` from the end, only `.gz`, leaving `backup.tar`. `##` cuts the longest match of `*.` from the start, everything up to the last dot, leaving `gz`. `%%` cuts the longest match from the end, everything from the first dot, leaving `backup`.\n\n+++ question | Why the script saw nothing\n--- ask\nYou type `CITY=Oslo` and then `bash show.sh`, where `show.sh` holds `echo "$CITY"`. It prints an empty line. Why?\n--- choice\n`bash` clears every variable when it starts, so a script can only use variables it sets itself.\n--- choice correct\nThe script runs in a child process, which gets a copy of only the exported variables, and `CITY` was never exported.\n--- choice\nVariables have to be written in small letters to reach a script.\n--- choice\nThe script needed `$1` instead of `$CITY`.\n--- why\nA plain variable lives only in your shell. A child process starts with a copy of the environment: the variables marked with `export`. `export CITY`, or `CITY=Oslo bash show.sh` for one run, would let the script see it.\n\n+++ question | A missing log\n--- ask\nThere is no file called `gone.log`. What does this line do?\n\n```\ngrep -q ERROR gone.log && echo "errors" || echo "clean"\n```\n--- choice\nIt prints `errors`, because `grep` could not rule them out.\n--- choice\nIt prints nothing, because `-q` hides everything.\n--- choice correct\nIt prints `grep`\'s error message about the missing file, then `clean`.\n--- choice\nIt stops with an error, and neither `echo` runs.\n--- why\n`grep` cannot open the file, so it prints an error on standard error (`-q` does not hide that) and finishes with status `2`. Any status other than `0` counts as failure, so `&&` skips `errors` and `||` runs `echo "clean"`. The log is reported clean when it does not even exist, which is why a missing file needs its own check.\n\n+++ question | The script that lost its argument\n--- ask\nThese lines should build and run a script that greets whoever is named in its first argument. Which line is wrong?\n\n```\n1  echo \'#!/bin/bash\' > hi.sh\n2  echo "echo Hello, $1" >> hi.sh\n3  chmod +x hi.sh\n4  ./hi.sh Ada\n```\n--- choice\nLine 1: the shebang must be added with `>>`.\n--- choice correct\nLine 2: double quotes let your shell fill in `$1` while writing the file, and it is empty then, so the script holds `echo Hello,`.\n--- choice\nLine 3: `chmod +x` must come before the file is written.\n--- choice\nLine 4: a script in this folder must be run as `hi.sh Ada`.\n--- why\nA script line meets the shell twice: once when you write it and once when the script runs. Double quotes expand `$1` at the first moment, when it is empty. Single quotes, `echo \'echo "Hello, $1"\' >> hi.sh`, keep it for the second.\n\n+++ question | The status of a script\n--- ask\n`check.sh` holds two lines: `ls missing`, then `echo done`. There is no file called `missing`. What does the last line print?\n\n```\nbash check.sh\necho $?\n```\n--- answer\n0\n--- why\nA script finishes with the status of the last command it ran. `ls missing` failed, but `echo done` worked, so the script reports `0`. That is why a careful script stops with its own `exit 1` as soon as something goes wrong.\n\n+++ question | The empty name\n--- ask\n`name` is set but empty. Why does `[ $name = ada ]` print `unary operator expected`?\n--- choice\n`=` cannot compare strings; it needs `-eq`.\n--- choice correct\nUnquoted, the empty variable disappears, so `[` is left with `= ada ]`, which is not a question it can read.\n--- choice\nVariables are not allowed inside `[ … ]`.\n--- choice\n`ada` has to be in quotes, or `[` reads it as a command.\n--- why\nThe shell fills in `$name` and splits the result into words before `[` runs. An empty, unquoted value turns into no word at all. With quotes, `[ "$name" = ada ]` stays a comparison between an empty string and `ada`, and is simply false.\n\n+++ question | What the loop costs\n--- ask\nA folder holds n log files, each with about m lines. How does the running time of this loop grow?\n\n```\nfor f in *.log; do grep -c ERROR "$f"; done\n```\n--- choice\nO(n): the loop runs once per file.\n--- choice\nO(m): `grep` reads one file at a time.\n--- choice correct\nO(n × m): the loop runs n times, and each `grep -c` reads all m lines of its file.\n--- choice\nO(n²): every file is compared with every other file.\n--- why\nThe loop body runs once per file, and `grep -c` has to read every line of that file to count the matches: it cannot stop early the way `grep -q` can. n runs of about m lines each is about n × m lines read in all.\n\n+++ question | sed without -i\n--- ask\n`crew.txt` holds `pilot: guest`. You run `sed \'s/guest/ada/\' crew.txt`, and it prints `pilot: ada`. What does `crew.txt` hold now?\n--- choice\n`pilot: ada`\n--- choice correct\n`pilot: guest`\n--- choice\nNothing: `sed` empties the file it reads.\n--- choice\nBoth lines, `pilot: guest` and then `pilot: ada`.\n--- why\nWithout `-i`, `sed` only prints the changed text; the file is never touched. That is useful for checking a change safely. Add `-i` when the output is what you want, and the file itself is rewritten.\n\n+++ question | xargs and a space\n--- ask\nThe folder holds `a.tmp` and `my notes.tmp`. What happens when you run `ls *.tmp | xargs rm`?\n--- choice\nBoth files are deleted.\n--- choice correct\n`a.tmp` is deleted, and `rm` complains that `my` and `notes.tmp` do not exist.\n--- choice\nNothing is deleted, because `rm` does not read a pipe.\n--- choice\nOnly `my notes.tmp` is deleted.\n--- why\n`xargs` cuts its input at every space, not only at the ends of lines, so `my notes.tmp` arrives as two arguments. `find . -name "*.tmp" -exec rm {} \\;` hands each name over whole, spaces and all.\n\n+++ question | A missing ampersand\n--- ask\nA script has the line `echo "disk full" >2`. What does that line do?\n--- choice\nIt prints `disk full` on standard error.\n--- choice correct\nIt writes `disk full` into a file called `2`, and nothing appears on the screen.\n--- choice\nIt prints `disk full` and stops the script with status 2.\n--- choice\nIt prints an error, because `>2` is not allowed.\n--- why\nAfter `>`, the shell expects a file name, so `2` is simply a name. `>&2` needs the `&` to mean "stream number two", standard error.\n\n+++ question | Reading a trace\n--- ask\n`bash -x archive.sh` shows this line of its trace:\n\n```\n+ mv meeting notes.txt archive/\n```\n\nWhat does it tell you?\n--- choice\n`mv` got two arguments, and the move worked.\n--- choice correct\n`mv` got three arguments, because the file name `meeting notes.txt` was split at its space: the variable holding it was not in double quotes.\n--- choice\nThe folder `archive` does not exist yet.\n--- choice\nThe script has a typo in a variable name.\n--- why\nA trace shows each command after the variables are filled in, and writes a word with a space in it inside single quotes. A correctly quoted `"$f"` would show `+ mv \'meeting notes.txt\' archive/`. With no quotes in the trace, `mv` saw `meeting`, `notes.txt` and `archive/`.\n';export{e as default};